WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:02.580
November 26th, 2014.

00:00:02.580 --> 00:00:08.220
A plot of Federal land in the Midwestern United
States appears to be completely uninhabited,

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:13.260
but to those in the know, this is actually
the location of Biological Research Area-12

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:19.740
- a large SCP Foundation Facility that houses
and experiments on live biological entities

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:21.660
and hazardous tissue samples.

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:26.820
Already, it was proving to be a challenging
day for the personnel stationed at Area-12:

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:28.920
Due to what seemed like a freak technical&nbsp;

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:31.620
glitch, they were dealing&nbsp;
with a systems failure...

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:34.380
and a large-scale containment breach.

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:41.040
The deadly bladed petals of SCP - 143 were
drifting through the air, the acidic SCP - 153

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:47.295
roundworms were breeding, SCP - 811 was running
wild, and a number of amnesia-inducing SCP

00:00:47.295 --> 00:00:49.980
- 939 creatures had escaped their pen.

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:53.220
In short, it was a total disaster.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:56.340
Complete paranormal pandemonium.

00:00:56.340 --> 00:01:01.920
And things were about to get so much worse,
as the sounds of whirring helicopter blades

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:05.580
and the rumbling engines of heavy military
vehicles approached.

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:10.320
The embattled staff were relieved that Mobile
Task Force reinforcements were arriving so

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:15.360
quickly - after all, Area-12 was relatively
isolated and they’d only just put in the

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:16.080
call for help.

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:20.880
On site security staff were being overwhelmed
in the chaos of the containment breach and

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:24.900
they needed the big guns if they wanted to
get things under control before it was too

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:25.140
late.

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:30.360
What they didn’t know as the vehicles surrounded
their facilities is that it was already far

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:31.860
too late for most of them.

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:36.180
Soon, heavily armed troops in protective tactical
gear were storming the facility.

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:41.580
As they entered, terrified staff members ran
towards them for protection, and then stopped

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:42.300
in their tracks.

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:47.520
These soldiers didn’t bear the insignia
of the SCP Foundation, or any known Mobile

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:48.360
Task Force.

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:51.780
Nor did they bear the symbols of the Global
Occult Coalition.

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:54.420
This was something different altogether.

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:59.340
Some of the Foundation personnel began to
beg for assistance anyway, seeing it as an

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:00.960
“any port in a storm” situation.

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:06.540
They realized too late that this group wasn’t
here to save them, and met their end in a

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:08.820
hail of bullets from the soldiers’ assault
rifles.

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:14.100
When the actual Mobile Task Force finally
did arrive, they witnessed a horrifying sight.

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:18.960
Most of the on site Foundation personnel had
been shot dead, and there was no trace of

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:19.680
the culprits.

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:23.760
Even worse, several of the SCP - 939 creatures
were missing.

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:29.100
These are the predatory, pack-hunting creatures
that produce amnestic chemicals to lure and

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:30.300
disorient their prey.

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:34.860
These anomalies are dangerous enough on their
own, but in the hands of someone who really

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:39.900
knew how to use them, these living amnestic
factories could pose an extremely serious

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:40.380
threat.

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:43.680
The Mobile Task Force members knew what they
were dealing with here.

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:49.560
Only one group would have the nerve to perform
a high-casualty heist on an SCP Foundation

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:53.940
Facility during a containment breach: The
Chaos Insurgency.

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:58.620
The Mobile Task Force reported the incident
back to command and already knew what their

00:02:58.620 --> 00:02:59.460
next mission would be.

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:03.600
Track down the Insurgency splinter cell and
get the 939s back.

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:08.640
This mission would have the absolute highest
stakes, and if they weren’t successful,

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:13.740
there was no limit to the damage the Chaos
Insurgency could do with a creature as dangerous

00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:16.800
as SCP-939 under their control.

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:19.980
But who exactly are The Chaos Insurgency?

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:21.180
What do they want?

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:23.580
And why are they stealing anomalies?

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:28.680
The Chaos Insurgency is one of the most mysterious
and clandestine of the groups that fight against

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:30.180
the SCP Foundation.

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:35.100
They are different from the Global Occult
Coalition, a United Nation’s offshoot created

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:37.200
after the Seventh Occult War whose mission&nbsp;

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:40.380
is to destroy rather than&nbsp;
try to contain anomalies.

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:43.680
The Serpent’s Hand is on the opposite end
of the spectrum.

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:48.360
This group strives for the normalization of
the anomalous, and the destruction of the

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:53.160
webs of secrecy that keep the anomalous and
consensus reality separate.

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:58.620
Both the GOC and the Serpent’s Hand have
clear ideals and mission statements, but the

00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:02.460
Chaos Insurgency is less forthcoming about
their beliefs and convictions.

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:07.560
Something we know for sure about the Chaos
Insurgency though, is that they view anomalies

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:13.680
as tools to be utilized rather than unpredictable
elements to be contained, studied, or neutralized.

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:18.000
To this end, they do whatever it takes to
obtain more anomalies of their own.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:22.680
Whether it’s ruthlessly seeking out anomalies
in the wild or taking them from the Foundation

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:25.560
with coordinated strikes during moments of
weakness.

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:30.420
Though they lack the support and resources
of organizations like the Foundation and the

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:35.820
GOC, the Chaos Insurgency more than makes
up for it in devotion to their cause, their

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:37.800
unpredictability, and most of all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:40.260
their willingness
to use violence.

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:45.360
It’s difficult to separate the facts from
the rumors when it comes to the Chaos Insurgency.

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:50.220
Some believe that, to compensate for their
rejection by the United Nations as an official

00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:55.380
group dealing with anomalous incidents, they
instead receive support from certain dictatorships

00:04:55.380 --> 00:04:56.400
in the developing world.

00:04:56.400 --> 00:05:01.440
Funded by the blood money of various warlords,
they carry out their research on political

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:05.340
prisoners and captured refugees provided by
their murderous allies.

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:10.260
They’re also believed to illegally deal
both weapons and intelligence, helping the

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:13.860
dictators who fund them remain in power and
subjugate their own people.

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:18.900
The Foundation has been able to gather some
intel about the Chaos Insurgency’s organizational

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:21.960
structure, which looks like a strange mirror
of their own.

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:27.060
It’s led by the mysterious Delta Command,
headed by a figure known only as The Engineer.

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:31.920
Gamma Class personnel execute the orders of
Delta Command, using the lesser Beta Class

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:35.580
personnel as field operators, and finally
there is Alpha Class.

00:05:35.580 --> 00:05:39.960
They’re typically forced into conscription
from the states occupied by the Insurgency

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:44.760
and serve as cannon fodder for the group as
they track down as many anomalies as they

00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:44.940
can.

00:05:44.940 --> 00:05:49.020
And the Insurgency is believed to possess
a number of powerful anomalies already.

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:54.240
These include the Bell of Entropy - an object
that can cause a variety of destructive effects

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:59.580
depending on where it is struck - and the
Staff of Hermes - an anomalous object capable

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:03.420
of warping the physical and chemical properties
of any matter it touches.

00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:08.340
The Chaos Insurgency is only growing more
powerful as they continue their pursuit of

00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:11.400
money and power with a legion of militarized
anomalies.

00:06:12.420 --> 00:06:13.260
Their goal?

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:19.200
Total world domination.Other accounts are
a little more charitable to the beliefs and

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:20.880
causes of the Chaos Insurgency.

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:26.280
They’ve been described as a rebellion against
the ruthless early days of the SCP Foundation,

00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:29.700
when they had a more violent, take no prisoners
attitude.

00:06:29.700 --> 00:06:34.620
This rumor has likely been disseminated by
the Chaos Insurgency themselves though, as

00:06:34.620 --> 00:06:37.440
it paints them in the most positive and righteous
light.

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:42.900
In reality, the truth - as is often the case
- is somewhere in the middle.

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:48.060
Danger often comes from within, and the Chaos
Insurgency is no exception.

00:06:48.060 --> 00:06:53.580
One constant in all interpretations of the
origin of the Chaos Insurgency is that its

00:06:53.580 --> 00:06:58.560
members are rogue elements of the SCP Foundation,
and it’s commonly believed that they have

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:01.560
countless moles still deep in the organization
today.

00:07:02.220 --> 00:07:07.500
However, one well-kept secret among the upper
echelons of the Foundation is that the creation

00:07:07.500 --> 00:07:11.700
of the Chaos Insurgency is a lot less “unknown”
than they’d like you to think.

00:07:11.700 --> 00:07:15.660
Yes, danger really does often come from within.

00:07:15.660 --> 00:07:21.060
When researching the origins of the Chaos
Insurgency, you’re likely to see two dates

00:07:21.060 --> 00:07:25.740
popup again and again: 1924 and 1948.

00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:31.260
According to the official line from the SCP
Foundation, 1924 was the date of the Chaos

00:07:31.260 --> 00:07:36.660
Insurgency’s defection, and 1948 marked
the first series of violent raids that the

00:07:36.660 --> 00:07:39.000
Chaos Insurgency led against the Foundation.

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:41.820
But these are only half truths.

00:07:41.820 --> 00:07:46.620
While both dates are indeed significant in
the story of the Chaos Insurgency, it’s

00:07:46.620 --> 00:07:48.660
for entirely different reasons.

00:07:48.660 --> 00:07:54.660
1924 was the date when the Chaos Insurgency
- known at the time simply as “The Insurgency”

00:07:54.660 --> 00:07:57.060
- was created by the O5 Council.

00:07:57.060 --> 00:08:02.160
Why would the SCP Foundation’s commanding
authority knowingly create one of the Foundation’s

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:03.000
enemies?

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:08.460
Well, you have to understand that at the time,
the Insurgency served a very different purpose.

00:08:08.460 --> 00:08:13.440
They were intended to be a black ops group
for the O5 Council, capable of doing their

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:16.680
dirty work off the books and out of sight
of the rest of the Foundation.

00:08:16.680 --> 00:08:21.660
Especially its ethics committee, which is
often in conflict with O5 Command.

00:08:21.660 --> 00:08:26.940
Their members were recruited from Mobile Task
Force Alpha-1, also known as the Red Right

00:08:26.940 --> 00:08:30.660
Hand, a highly secretive MTF in the pocket
of the O5.

00:08:30.660 --> 00:08:35.640
For twenty-four years, they did the O5’s
dirty work while shielding the Foundation’s

00:08:35.640 --> 00:08:38.880
international reputation from any potential
fallout.

00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:46.020
They were faithful soldiers, until they found
themselves a new master: The Engine, a mysterious,

00:08:46.020 --> 00:08:50.400
anomalous object that began to invade and
infect the minds of the Insurgency.

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:55.560
The group’s human leader, the previously
mentioned Engineer, is merely a puppet of

00:08:55.560 --> 00:08:57.840
the engine - its human mouth piece.

00:08:57.840 --> 00:09:02.700
While the full extent of the Engine’s plans
remain mysterious to even members of the Chaos

00:09:02.700 --> 00:09:06.420
Insurgency, the Engine has been passing down
orders ever since.

00:09:06.420 --> 00:09:13.140
In 1948, the Insurgency fully defected, becoming
the Chaos Insurgency, and they’ve been a

00:09:13.140 --> 00:09:19.080
problem for the SCP Foundation ever since,
from raids to assassinations to threats of

00:09:19.080 --> 00:09:22.860
damaging the illusion of consensus reality
with their reckless behaviour.

00:09:22.860 --> 00:09:28.860
And now, thanks to their acquisition of SCP
- 939, they could get started on amnestics

00:09:28.860 --> 00:09:29.640
production too.

00:09:29.640 --> 00:09:35.280
Thankfully for the Foundation, they had prepared
for 939s getting out into the world, and all

00:09:35.280 --> 00:09:39.420
of the creatures housed at Area-12 had been
implanted with sub-dermal trackers.

00:09:39.960 --> 00:09:44.340
Several Mobile Task Forces were immediately
dispatched to home in on the signal, take

00:09:44.340 --> 00:09:47.460
out the Insurgents, and secure the 939s once
more.

00:09:47.460 --> 00:09:52.320
They tracked the signal to a warehouse in
the badlands of New Mexico where Task Force

00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:57.060
members stormed in and began a tense firefight
with the Chaos Insurgents - all Gamma and

00:09:57.060 --> 00:09:58.140
Beta Class, of course.

00:09:58.140 --> 00:10:02.700
The Delta Classes, just like 05s, are notoriously
slippery.

00:10:02.700 --> 00:10:08.460
They emerged like tactical ghosts from behind
boxes and exposed pipes, advancing and firing

00:10:08.460 --> 00:10:10.860
with no regard for their own lives and safety.

00:10:10.860 --> 00:10:12.660
Slaves to the Engine.

00:10:13.440 --> 00:10:18.120
It wasn’t like battling your run of the
mill cultists - these were highly organized

00:10:18.120 --> 00:10:22.800
and dangerous combatants, with training right
out of the Foundation’s Mobile Task Force

00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:23.160
playbook.

00:10:23.160 --> 00:10:28.560
Several Foundation soldiers were lost in the
crossfire, but ultimately, they won the day

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:33.720
- subduing the Chaos Insurgency Forces and
capturing the stolen 939s once more.

00:10:33.720 --> 00:10:38.580
Several of the Insurgents that’d been fatally
wounded in the battle were found to be Area-12

00:10:38.580 --> 00:10:41.580
personnel… double-agents for the Insurgency.

00:10:41.580 --> 00:10:47.220
Many of them died with smiles on their faces,
knowing they were defying the Foundation to

00:10:47.220 --> 00:10:48.180
their last breath.

00:10:48.180 --> 00:10:50.580
There was no way of knowing just how many&nbsp;

00:10:50.580 --> 00:10:53.220
of these secretive Chaos&nbsp;
Insurgents were undercover,

00:10:53.220 --> 00:10:56.760
deep in the fabric of the Foundation’s global
apparatus.

00:10:56.760 --> 00:11:02.580
A nearby insurgent, slowly dying from several
gunshot wounds, gave a wheezy laugh.

00:11:03.180 --> 00:11:07.680
As the Task Force operators approached, he
ranted that the Foundation’s obsession with

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:11.040
order, lies, and secrecy is the real disease.

00:11:11.040 --> 00:11:17.160
That Chaos and entropy is the fate of all
things, and that to use the anomalies they

00:11:17.160 --> 00:11:19.980
find for their own gain is simply common sense.

00:11:20.700 --> 00:11:25.800
In the world the Chaos Insurgency would someday
create, human beings would be the true masters

00:11:25.800 --> 00:11:30.720
of the universe, not just the perpetrators
of the twisted lie we call “normality.”

00:11:30.720 --> 00:11:35.520
He succumbed to his injuries shortly after,
and the Task Forces refocused their efforts

00:11:35.520 --> 00:11:38.700
on getting the 939s safely back to Area-12.

00:11:38.700 --> 00:11:43.020
What these Chaos Insurgency troops really
believed is an open question.

00:11:43.020 --> 00:11:48.720
After all, the power that dictates them - the
anomalous Engine - is a consciousness beyond

00:11:48.720 --> 00:11:49.440
humanity.

00:11:49.440 --> 00:11:53.760
Even the Engineer doesn’t know the true
scope of their master’s grand plan.

00:11:53.760 --> 00:11:59.160
If the rest of us are lucky, and the Chaos
Insurgency never reaches their mysterious

00:11:59.160 --> 00:12:04.320
goals, then neither will we.
Able wandered through the sands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.320 --> 00:12:12.180
a lone warrior, dragging a long, dark sword behind&nbsp;
him, his black cloak flowing in the gentle breeze.

00:12:12.180 --> 00:12:18.360
The sword was thirsty. It’d been too long&nbsp;
since it tasted blood. What had it been?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.360 --> 00:12:22.380
A day since he cut down ten men in&nbsp;
a tavern without breaking a sweat?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.380 --> 00:12:27.240
They’d bled and screamed like pigs as&nbsp;
he’d diced them into bloody chunks.

00:12:27.240 --> 00:12:33.060
He couldn’t remember their faces. They hadn’t&nbsp;
earned that. Very few combatants had been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.060 --> 00:12:38.280
remarkable enough to warrant committing to&nbsp;
memory. It was all just more dead flesh.

00:12:38.280 --> 00:12:44.700
He took a sip from his canteen and sighed. Did&nbsp;
this world hold no more challenges? What a boring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.700 --> 00:12:48.840
eternity was laying out before him.&nbsp;
His burden as the greatest warrior&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.840 --> 00:12:51.780
of all time weighed on him heavier than the chain.

00:12:51.780 --> 00:12:56.460
It was old and rusty, levered over&nbsp;
his shoulder and grasped in one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.460 --> 00:13:01.560
bloody hand. About fifteen feet behind&nbsp;
him, the chain was connected to a dark,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.560 --> 00:13:07.620
stone sarcophagus that was as much a part of&nbsp;
him as his eyes, skin, or heart. If ever he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.620 --> 00:13:13.800
slain in the glorious heat of battle, he’d rise&nbsp;
out of it, ready to fight and kill another day.

00:13:13.800 --> 00:13:18.360
All because of the actions of his&nbsp;
worthless, good for nothing brother…

00:13:18.360 --> 00:13:23.700
He looked up when he heard the rush of footsteps&nbsp;
and the clanking of armor. Warriors - or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.700 --> 00:13:28.860
whatever passed for them around here - about&nbsp;
twenty of them, circled all around him. Yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.860 --> 00:13:35.100
oh yes. His grip tightened around his sword.&nbsp;
One of the warriors called out something about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.100 --> 00:13:39.720
him being under arrest, by order of&nbsp;
the king, for murders beyond counting.

00:13:39.720 --> 00:13:45.420
Able couldn’t help but yawn. Words,&nbsp;
words, words. Why even bother?

00:13:45.420 --> 00:13:51.420
He dropped the chain, and in one fluid motion,&nbsp;
he threw his sword. In a fraction of a second,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.420 --> 00:13:56.040
it’d pierced the armor of the chattering&nbsp;
man, spearing him through his formerly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.040 --> 00:14:02.040
beating heart. The scream died in his throat,&nbsp;
he fell to his knees, then collapsed entirely.

00:14:02.040 --> 00:14:07.320
The other soldiers sent to kill or apprehend him&nbsp;
turned to their fallen leader and gasped. It was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.320 --> 00:14:13.500
that little gasp, that moment of distraction,&nbsp;
that sealed their fates. Able’s face cracked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.500 --> 00:14:18.600
into a whisper of a grin, as he drew two&nbsp;
long daggers from the darkness of his coat.

00:14:18.600 --> 00:14:21.120
He’d at least try to have fun with this…

00:14:21.120 --> 00:14:24.180
Before the others could even get&nbsp;
over their leader’s sudden death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.180 --> 00:14:30.060
Able had vaulted forward and begun his delicate&nbsp;
dance of slaughter. Every swing found its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.060 --> 00:14:36.000
way through armor and into skin. He sliced&nbsp;
throats, cleaved off heads, parried blows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:42.300
and pierced hearts. There was barely a single&nbsp;
scream. Able killed too quick for screams.

00:14:42.300 --> 00:14:48.420
In what would seem like the blink of an eye for&nbsp;
some, the soldiers around Able fell. Most dead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.420 --> 00:14:54.300
the rest dying. Some looked up to him in their&nbsp;
dying moments, in terrified awe at the efficacy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.300 --> 00:14:59.400
of their killer. In their dying moments,&nbsp;
they knew that they never had a chance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.400 --> 00:15:04.380
They might as well have faced the glistening&nbsp;
scythe of death himself on the battlefield.

00:15:04.380 --> 00:15:10.680
Able, on the other hand, rolled his eyes and&nbsp;
sighed. Another pathetic waste of time. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.680 --> 00:15:15.180
sensed movement in the corner of his eye: One&nbsp;
of the wounded soldiers was limping to his feet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.180 --> 00:15:19.920
trying to use the sword to lever himself off&nbsp;
of the ground. With a flick of each wrist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:19.920 --> 00:15:25.320
Able tossed his knives into the man, killing&nbsp;
him instantly. It really was that easy.

00:15:25.320 --> 00:15:30.780
“Your attempt to kill me does not offend&nbsp;
me,” he said, to whoever was still able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:30.780 --> 00:15:36.600
hear. “What offends me is that they would&nbsp;
send so few, and that those few would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.600 --> 00:15:43.740
such pitiful excuses for soldiers. This&nbsp;
wasn’t a battle - It was a mercy killing.”

00:15:43.740 --> 00:15:48.600
He was ready to turn around, grab the&nbsp;
chain, and carry on walking, when he felt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.600 --> 00:15:54.000
a sudden pain in his back. There was a slight&nbsp;
whistle, then another sharp spike of pain.

00:15:54.000 --> 00:15:57.000
There were now two arrows&nbsp;
sticking out of his back.

00:15:57.000 --> 00:16:03.600
Able turned, surprised, and saw a much larger&nbsp;
force standing behind him. Swordsmen, archers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.600 --> 00:16:08.280
men with clubs and axes and chains.&nbsp;
The ones he’d killed were little more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.280 --> 00:16:14.220
than a distraction. This was the real&nbsp;
threat. This was the real army. Perhaps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.220 --> 00:16:17.340
these fools would give him some actual exercise.

00:16:17.340 --> 00:16:23.400
He reached into his cloak and pulled out a&nbsp;
mighty obsidian battle axe. At the very least,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:23.400 --> 00:16:27.780
he’d try to have a little fun turning&nbsp;
this fighting force into cold cuts.

00:16:27.780 --> 00:16:33.360
A fog of arrows sailed through the air as he&nbsp;
charged forwards, perforating his body, but the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.360 --> 00:16:39.720
injuries didn’t slow him down. He lunged, slashed,&nbsp;
and cleaved. Even as the weapons struck him, he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:39.720 --> 00:16:46.500
carried on, killing person after person. At times,&nbsp;
it was almost exciting - Almost, but not quite.

00:16:46.500 --> 00:16:52.260
By the time he was done, none were left standing.&nbsp;
Thirty arrows were sticking out of him. He’d been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:52.260 --> 00:16:57.480
cut deep by more weapons than he could count on&nbsp;
his fingers and toes. He was breathing deeply,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.480 --> 00:17:02.100
scarred chest pumping up and down.&nbsp;
He coughed blood and cracked his neck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.100 --> 00:17:06.360
back into place. They might’ve cut him&nbsp;
a little too deep this time. No matter.

00:17:06.360 --> 00:17:10.320
Able fell to his knees, feeling&nbsp;
the life draining from him.

00:17:10.320 --> 00:17:15.480
He wondered, when he awoke from the coffin again,&nbsp;
what the world would look like. Sometimes it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:15.480 --> 00:17:22.680
days, sometimes weeks, months, or even years.&nbsp;
As he fell forward, dying once again, he hoped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:22.680 --> 00:17:27.180
that he’d wake into a world with a warrior&nbsp;
or beast that could actually challenge him.

00:17:27.180 --> 00:17:28.620
Maybe someday…

00:17:28.620 --> 00:17:34.560
This was one of Able’s many lives, hundreds&nbsp;
of years before he was contained by the SCP&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:34.560 --> 00:17:40.320
Foundation. He’s perhaps the greatest warrior&nbsp;
who ever lived, died, and lived again. He’s a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:40.320 --> 00:17:45.060
man so individually deadly that not only is he&nbsp;
kept in a containment chamber under the sea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:45.060 --> 00:17:50.880
surrounded by highly trained and armed guards,&nbsp;
he has his own localized on-site nuclear weapon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:50.880 --> 00:17:56.520
ready to blow away and annihilate him and his&nbsp;
entire containment area if deemed necessary.

00:17:56.520 --> 00:18:03.060
He may not be a contagious anomalous pathogen or&nbsp;
a lethal memetic hazard or a giant beast shooting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:03.060 --> 00:18:07.740
world-destroying fireballs in every direction,&nbsp;
but if this one-man massacre was left to his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:07.740 --> 00:18:12.180
own devices, there’s no doubt that he would&nbsp;
methodically slaughter his way through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:12.180 --> 00:18:17.880
human race until an XK-Class End of the World&nbsp;
Scenario was practically inevitable. He was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:17.880 --> 00:18:24.420
fueled by pure hatred and an almost bottomless&nbsp;
bloodlust. He simply lived to fight and kill.

00:18:24.420 --> 00:18:30.480
And not only did he have the will and the skill to&nbsp;
be a pure force of annihilation, but his anomalous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:30.480 --> 00:18:35.760
abilities also make him perfectly tailored to the&nbsp;
task. He has massively enhanced physical strength,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:35.760 --> 00:18:40.260
speed, and durability, taking the kind&nbsp;
of damage that would kill several normal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:40.260 --> 00:18:45.420
humans to reliably put him down - Though&nbsp;
even that is only a temporary measure.

00:18:45.420 --> 00:18:48.240
Able will always resurrect back into his black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:48.240 --> 00:18:51.900
sarcophagus to menace the&nbsp;
SCP Foundation another day.

00:18:51.900 --> 00:18:56.700
It is also effectively impossible to disarm&nbsp;
Able, because he has the anomalous ability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:56.700 --> 00:19:02.160
to pull deadly edged weapons from localized&nbsp;
pocket dimensions at will, and his proficiency&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:02.160 --> 00:19:07.680
with these weapons is unlike any warrior&nbsp;
the world has ever known, before or since.

00:19:07.680 --> 00:19:12.420
During containment breaches, he’s regularly&nbsp;
killed scores of trained Foundation guards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:12.420 --> 00:19:18.840
with both numerical advantages and considerably&nbsp;
more advanced ranged weapons. Despite being a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:18.840 --> 00:19:23.820
simple humanoid, he was taking up a truly&nbsp;
insane amount of containment resources.

00:19:23.820 --> 00:19:28.680
Despite his violent tendencies, Able&nbsp;
is still a recognizable sentient human,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:28.680 --> 00:19:34.620
albeit an extremely deadly anomalous one. This&nbsp;
led some higher-ups at the Foundation to come up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:34.620 --> 00:19:40.380
with an interesting idea: What if Able’s eternal&nbsp;
rage could be harnessed? What if they could use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:40.380 --> 00:19:45.900
their resources to reshape this rampaging killer&nbsp;
into a devoted sword of the Foundation’s cause?

00:19:45.900 --> 00:19:50.220
After all, if he wanted worthy&nbsp;
opponents, what could be more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:50.220 --> 00:19:54.540
worthy than the anomalous monsters that&nbsp;
the Foundation faced on a daily basis?

00:19:54.540 --> 00:19:59.820
And as long as they kept the sarcophagus,&nbsp;
even if Able was killed in the line of duty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:59.820 --> 00:20:05.280
he’d still be accounted for. In many ways, if&nbsp;
he could be trained and truly brought to heel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:05.280 --> 00:20:10.380
there could be no better asset to their&nbsp;
coming struggles. It was this logic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:10.380 --> 00:20:15.000
allowing anomalies to work for the SCP&nbsp;
Foundation in exchange for benefits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:15.000 --> 00:20:23.100
that led to the creation of a new, groundbreaking&nbsp;
Mobile Task Force: MTF Omega-7, Pandora’s Box.

00:20:23.100 --> 00:20:28.740
This group became the SCP Foundation’s hail&nbsp;
mary pass. For any particularly dangerous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:28.740 --> 00:20:33.840
or potentially deadly mission, they could send&nbsp;
in Able, along with a group of highly-trained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:33.840 --> 00:20:38.700
Foundation soldiers that even the ancient,&nbsp;
blade-wielding warrior held respect for. While,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:38.700 --> 00:20:44.220
like their namesake, Pandora’s Box, it would&nbsp;
all wind up in terrible tragedy, to begin with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:44.220 --> 00:20:48.060
they achieved some of the highest mission&nbsp;
successful results of any Mobile Task Force&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:48.060 --> 00:20:53.400
on the Foundation’s payroll. No task was too&nbsp;
challenging for them to swoop in and crush it.

00:20:53.400 --> 00:20:58.860
This was far from expected: Able, one of&nbsp;
the most violent SCPs they’d ever contained,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:58.860 --> 00:21:03.840
suddenly becoming a great asset to their&nbsp;
operations. A vital tool in their quest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:03.840 --> 00:21:08.940
to keep the anomalous at bay. He’d cleaved&nbsp;
through legions of Chaos Insurgency soldiers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:08.940 --> 00:21:13.320
during breaches into their secure sites.&nbsp;
He’d fought off the well-paid, well-trained,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:13.320 --> 00:21:18.540
and well-armed body guards of Marshall, Carter,&nbsp;
and Dark Ltd. during Foundation raids on their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:18.540 --> 00:21:23.520
clandestine operations. He’d even gone toe&nbsp;
to toe with some of the deadliest anomalies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:23.520 --> 00:21:28.980
in containment during mass escapes. It was hard&nbsp;
to imagine how they’d ever lived without him.

00:21:28.980 --> 00:21:32.940
Of course, while Able was happier than&nbsp;
he’d been in years - in his element,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:32.940 --> 00:21:38.160
in fact, as a working warrior, given varied&nbsp;
missions and frequent opponents - there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:38.160 --> 00:21:42.660
was still something nagging at him. His&nbsp;
thoughts were hounded by his white whale:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:42.660 --> 00:21:49.560
The endless search for a truly worthy opponent,&nbsp;
someone or something that could really give him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:49.560 --> 00:21:53.820
a run for his money. After millennia&nbsp;
of leaving opponents dead in his wake,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:53.820 --> 00:21:59.220
nothing would bring him more joy than meeting&nbsp;
something that actually knocked him on his ass.

00:21:59.220 --> 00:22:05.100
A new bar somewhere above him to work towards.&nbsp;
Oh, what a glorious day that would be…

00:22:05.100 --> 00:22:10.320
Eventually, the Foundation started to run into&nbsp;
a problem: They were running out of missions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:10.320 --> 00:22:15.120
to give Able. After all, he wasn’t the kind of&nbsp;
operative you could just give any mission to:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:15.120 --> 00:22:20.460
His potential for collateral damage was truly&nbsp;
staggering. He’d neutralize the anomaly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:20.460 --> 00:22:25.080
then slaughter everyone within a hundred-foot&nbsp;
range, just to work off some of the excess&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:25.080 --> 00:22:31.320
energy. Like a hand grenade, he was powerful,&nbsp;
but dangerously imprecise. If they ran out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:31.320 --> 00:22:36.720
high-priority missions, what were they supposed&nbsp;
to do? Just put Able back in his box to gather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:36.720 --> 00:22:41.340
dust until something else rolled around? He was&nbsp;
getting antsy enough between missions already…

00:22:41.340 --> 00:22:46.980
That’s when an unexpected member of&nbsp;
personnel stepped forward: Dr. Jack Bright.

00:22:46.980 --> 00:22:52.380
You see, Dr. Bright and Able had a history&nbsp;
and not an altogether pleasant one - Not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:52.380 --> 00:22:56.640
that anyone could really have a pleasant&nbsp;
history with Able. Jack was only a junior&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:56.640 --> 00:23:02.820
researcher when he had his run-in, carrying a&nbsp;
seemingly worthless medallion dubbed SCP-963&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:02.820 --> 00:23:07.020
back to its containment locker. That&nbsp;
was when a wall next to him exploded,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:07.020 --> 00:23:13.620
showering him with brick fragments and dust, only&nbsp;
to reveal Able standing behind the new aperture.

00:23:13.620 --> 00:23:19.680
Before young Dr. Bright even had a chance to&nbsp;
scream, Able had already cleaved through him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:19.680 --> 00:23:25.200
leaving him in two distinct parts that&nbsp;
were both very much dead. At least it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:25.200 --> 00:23:30.180
seemed that way, until it was revealed that&nbsp;
Dr. Bright’s consciousness had actually been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:30.180 --> 00:23:37.260
eternally bonded with SCP-963, giving him the&nbsp;
gift and curse of immortality. Since then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:37.260 --> 00:23:42.540
Dr. Bright had become increasingly reckless in&nbsp;
his conduct, perhaps hoping that the next time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:42.540 --> 00:23:48.540
he fades to black, the movie that is his sad,&nbsp;
strange life won’t just start to roll again.

00:23:48.540 --> 00:23:51.840
Of course, he hasn’t been&nbsp;
lucky in that regard yet.

00:23:51.840 --> 00:23:56.760
Naturally, this has given Dr. Bright complex&nbsp;
feelings about his fellow anomalous Foundation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:56.760 --> 00:24:02.760
employee. So when the call came around all the&nbsp;
senior researchers and site directors, asking if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:02.760 --> 00:24:08.940
there were any tasks that Able seemed fit for,&nbsp;
he had one very pressing suggestion. After all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:08.940 --> 00:24:13.320
it wasn’t that long after Dr. Bright had been&nbsp;
forced into a cross test with the intention&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:13.320 --> 00:24:19.080
of terminating SCP-682 - Which had not only&nbsp;
been a failure, but a generally painful and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:19.080 --> 00:24:24.600
exhausting experience. Now, perhaps it&nbsp;
would be Able’s turn to take his lumps.

00:24:24.600 --> 00:24:29.820
He happily put forth the suggestion, claiming&nbsp;
that surely the Foundation’s new sword-wielding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:29.820 --> 00:24:34.860
golden boy could give killing the Hard to&nbsp;
Destroy Reptile the old college try. After all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:34.860 --> 00:24:40.620
even if Able was killed in the process, he’d&nbsp;
just come right back. It was a situation where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:40.620 --> 00:24:46.140
they really could not lose, so why not take&nbsp;
a chance? What’s the worse that could happen?

00:24:46.140 --> 00:24:51.480
The O5 Council found Dr. Bright’s pitch&nbsp;
extremely compelling. He’d succeeded in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:51.480 --> 00:24:56.160
every mission they’d given him so far, so&nbsp;
perhaps he could carry that success into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:56.160 --> 00:25:01.980
the herculean task of actually terminating&nbsp;
SCP-682. One boundlessly bloodthirsty killer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:01.980 --> 00:25:07.260
might be the only thing truly capable of&nbsp;
taking out another of equal magnitude.

00:25:07.260 --> 00:25:13.260
When Able was informed of this latest mission,&nbsp;
he got a scary glint in his eye. They gave him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:13.260 --> 00:25:18.600
warning after warning: The beast is said&nbsp;
to be unkillable, it can adapt to anything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:18.600 --> 00:25:23.520
it’s killed scores of people and survived the&nbsp;
attacks of anomalies thought to be flawless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:23.520 --> 00:25:29.280
killers. The more it was explained to him, the&nbsp;
more Able felt the tingling sensation deep within:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:29.280 --> 00:25:34.260
Was this it? Had he now discovered the&nbsp;
perfect opponent? Something that would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:34.260 --> 00:25:39.360
actually challenge him, would actually&nbsp;
put him through his paces? Yes, yes, yes!

00:25:39.360 --> 00:25:43.800
He accepted the mission without question. Able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:43.800 --> 00:25:48.000
would fight SCP-682 until&nbsp;
his breath was no longer.

00:25:48.000 --> 00:25:54.120
In order to prepare for the match, SCP-682&nbsp;
was released into a secure area: Rocky,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:54.120 --> 00:25:59.640
desert-like terrain, bordered on all sides by&nbsp;
a Foundation perimeter, hundreds of meters away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:59.640 --> 00:26:04.560
on all sides. They thought it best for&nbsp;
the showdown to happen here. After all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:04.560 --> 00:26:09.840
with combatants like Able and SCP-682, it&nbsp;
was bound to make a mess, one way or another.

00:26:09.840 --> 00:26:14.580
Able strode with pronounced swagger onto&nbsp;
the battleground shortly afterwards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:14.580 --> 00:26:19.860
carrying perhaps the most powerful sword he’d&nbsp;
ever summoned. It was somewhere between a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:19.860 --> 00:26:25.980
claymore and a chainsaw, an unholy union that&nbsp;
gave the resulting weapon a degree of deranged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:25.980 --> 00:26:31.260
badassery not ever seen on the battlefields&nbsp;
of planet earth before. Carrying this thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:31.260 --> 00:26:37.320
Able felt like a King, and he was about to&nbsp;
slay the most ancient and bestial of monsters.

00:26:37.320 --> 00:26:43.140
As he approached 682 and took in the whole&nbsp;
of it, he could feel his heart pounding with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:43.140 --> 00:26:49.800
excitement in his chest. It was a huge, reptilian&nbsp;
nightmare. He could see its scales hardening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:49.800 --> 00:26:55.560
into a mighty carapace as he approached.&nbsp;
Its huge, serrated fangs. Its bulging,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:55.560 --> 00:27:02.400
sinewy muscles and insane, dagger-like&nbsp;
claws. Oh yes, this would be the one.

00:27:02.400 --> 00:27:07.740
The beast snarled at him as he approached. He&nbsp;
just smiled, puffed out his chest, and said…

00:27:07.740 --> 00:27:14.760
“I have heard tales of creatures like&nbsp;
you. Glorious beasts of scale and flesh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:14.760 --> 00:27:21.420
talon and fang, a prowess in battle even greater&nbsp;
than the immense intellect hiding behind those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:21.420 --> 00:27:27.600
bestial eyes. They said your kind once ruled&nbsp;
the Earth from enormous stockpiles of treasure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:27.600 --> 00:27:34.260
killing and eating all who displeased you. But&nbsp;
you were knocked from your throne, one by one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:34.260 --> 00:27:40.020
by the great warriors who walk this world&nbsp;
no longer, until there were no more,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:40.020 --> 00:27:46.680
and you became but mere myth. Even I had&nbsp;
thought you to be nothing but fairy tales,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:46.680 --> 00:27:51.900
but yet, here you stand&nbsp;
before me, a living dragon…”

00:27:51.900 --> 00:27:57.240
In response to Able’s lofty speech, the&nbsp;
monster merely grumbled and chided him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:57.240 --> 00:28:03.240
claiming he was little more than a pathetic SCP&nbsp;
Foundation lapdog, following orders and being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:03.240 --> 00:28:08.760
manipulated. It showed no respect for Able&nbsp;
as a valued enemy combatant - Merely another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:08.760 --> 00:28:14.160
nuisance thrown at it in a futile attempt to&nbsp;
finish its wretched and seemingly eternal life.

00:28:14.160 --> 00:28:20.040
Able couldn’t take such insolence. He leaped&nbsp;
forwards, bringing down his mighty chainsaw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:20.040 --> 00:28:24.960
claymore, ready to cleave the beast in&nbsp;
two. However, what he didn’t expect was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:24.960 --> 00:28:30.540
the move SCP-682 pulled next: Throwing its&nbsp;
head up against the blade of Able’s sword,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:30.540 --> 00:28:36.360
shredding away huge chunks of flesh and bone,&nbsp;
and utterly confusing Able in the process.

00:28:36.360 --> 00:28:39.540
For the first time in a&nbsp;
lifetime of intense battles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:39.540 --> 00:28:43.560
Able found himself thinking, “What&nbsp;
the hell am I up against here?”

00:28:43.560 --> 00:28:48.660
The force of 682’s headbutt threw Able&nbsp;
off balance, leaving his stomach briefly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:48.660 --> 00:28:54.540
exposed. But “briefly” was all SCP-682&nbsp;
needed. It thundered its massive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:54.540 --> 00:29:01.320
stony fist into Able’s gut, throwing him like&nbsp;
a ragdoll into a nearby rock with such a force&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:01.320 --> 00:29:06.600
that it nearly shattered the rock behind him.&nbsp;
It was a force like he hadn’t felt in years.

00:29:06.600 --> 00:29:13.080
He spat some bloody teeth and grinned. This&nbsp;
was just what the doctor ordered. He issued a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:13.080 --> 00:29:19.380
challenge to the beast in a long-dead language,&nbsp;
as it seized violently, regenerating, growing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:19.380 --> 00:29:24.480
taking on the stony qualities of the ground&nbsp;
around it. It looked like a vengeful living&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:24.480 --> 00:29:30.420
mountain. A true behemoth of a beast.&nbsp;
In other words, challenge accepted.

00:29:30.420 --> 00:29:35.940
Able pulled an obscenely giant mace from the&nbsp;
shadows of his cloak - The handle six feet long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:35.940 --> 00:29:40.560
with a chaos of swirling blades&nbsp;
and spikes. A perfect weapon for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:40.560 --> 00:29:43.380
slaying a dragon like this, he thought to himself.

00:29:43.380 --> 00:29:48.480
The two charged at each other, full of&nbsp;
power and fury. Able swung the mace,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:48.480 --> 00:29:53.400
once again shattering the creature’s head and&nbsp;
flinging it back across the battlefield with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:53.400 --> 00:29:58.080
sheer force of its strike. The decimated&nbsp;
lizard clawed its way into the ground,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:58.080 --> 00:30:03.720
devouring the rocks and the earth, integrating&nbsp;
more matter to fuel its regeneration.

00:30:03.720 --> 00:30:10.020
But it wasn’t long before Able was upon it&nbsp;
again, striking mercilessly, giving blows as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:10.020 --> 00:30:15.300
the monster gave brutal claw strikes in return.&nbsp;
They were ripping each other’s bodies apart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:15.300 --> 00:30:21.540
piece by piece, but Able felt so exhilarated&nbsp;
he could barely even notice. It was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:21.540 --> 00:30:27.480
fight of his life - A battle against a&nbsp;
truly worthy opponent. This was heaven.

00:30:27.480 --> 00:30:31.860
Able leaped into the air and unleashed a&nbsp;
volley of deadly chakram down onto the beast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:31.860 --> 00:30:36.540
shredding into its reinforced flesh. As&nbsp;
the force of gravity brought him down,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:36.540 --> 00:30:41.940
he pulled a mighty axe from his cloak, and&nbsp;
bellowed a warrior’s roar as he brought it down,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:41.940 --> 00:30:46.500
splattering into the nightmarish body of SCP-682.

00:30:46.500 --> 00:30:50.820
However, this did nothing to even&nbsp;
slow the beast down. It flipped over,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:50.820 --> 00:30:57.300
slashing Able with its claws. When Able stumbled,&nbsp;
it leaped on top of him, unleashing devastating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:57.300 --> 00:31:02.820
slashes and punches onto the fallen warrior&nbsp;
with the speed of a machine gun firing. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:02.820 --> 00:31:07.860
it raised its claw to deal the killing blow,&nbsp;
though, Able once again turned the tables.

00:31:07.860 --> 00:31:11.460
He produced a giant pair of&nbsp;
mechanical scissors from thin air,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:11.460 --> 00:31:15.780
and sliced off both of SCP-682’s forelegs.

00:31:15.780 --> 00:31:20.640
The beast descended with its mighty jaws to&nbsp;
devour Able, but he kicked up, with freakish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:20.640 --> 00:31:27.240
strength behind his bladed boot. The sheer force&nbsp;
of the kick flipped SCP-682 onto its back. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:27.240 --> 00:31:31.620
it was Able’s turn to execute his opponent,&nbsp;
though on some level he thought it would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:31.620 --> 00:31:35.700
an awful shame to lose such a terrific&nbsp;
beast from this world of cardboard.

00:31:35.700 --> 00:31:40.200
Still, a battle is a battle,&nbsp;
and this is how they go.

00:31:40.200 --> 00:31:46.440
He jumped onto 682 and went berserk, slashing&nbsp;
into it relentlessly with blade after blade,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:46.440 --> 00:31:50.280
pulling out a new one every time&nbsp;
the old one broke from his sheer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:50.280 --> 00:31:57.540
ferocity. He screamed in incoherent battle&nbsp;
fury, tearing, slicing, ripping, rending.

00:31:57.540 --> 00:32:01.200
Yes, yes, yes, yes!

00:32:01.200 --> 00:32:06.840
As Able stepped away to breathe, the beast&nbsp;
began to regenerate, releasing a shockwave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:06.840 --> 00:32:11.280
that started to warp reality around&nbsp;
it. But Able wouldn’t have this. No,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:11.280 --> 00:32:16.860
he would give this beast no quarter. It was time&nbsp;
to present the true pain he was notorious for..

00:32:16.860 --> 00:32:21.960
He pulled a long sword from his cloak and&nbsp;
charged, taking air and bringing it down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:21.960 --> 00:32:27.540
towards SCP-682’s head. The beast, sensing&nbsp;
the warrior’s presence, opened its mouth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:27.540 --> 00:32:33.780
unleashing a chasm of horrifying teeth within.&nbsp;
The two were on a fierce collision course. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:33.780 --> 00:32:38.685
the jaws closed, Able descended. Both&nbsp;
roared in infinite rage and bloodlust.

00:32:38.685 --> 00:32:38.770
As the sword came down, it cut SCP-682&nbsp;
clean in half. As the beast’s jaws clamped,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:38.770 --> 00:32:40.740
Able’s head and arm were severed from his&nbsp;
body. Both combatants fell to the ground,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:40.740 --> 00:32:47.220
just twitching. Oh, what a glorious, terrible&nbsp;
day it had been. Neither had died for good,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:47.220 --> 00:32:50.760
but both would remember this&nbsp;
incredible battle forever.

00:32:50.760 --> 00:32:55.800
When Able awoke once more in his&nbsp;
dark stone coffin, he did so with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:55.800 --> 00:33:03.120
a smile. What a battle! What a fight! What&nbsp;
a truly honorable pursuit! After so long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:03.120 --> 00:33:08.760
being bored and unfulfilled, he’d found an&nbsp;
opponent that got his blood pumping once more.

00:33:08.760 --> 00:33:12.000
His stomach grumbled. A post-battle feast was in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:12.000 --> 00:33:15.360
order. Now, where did they&nbsp;
put that magic pizza box…

00:33:15.360 --> 00:33:18.180
Does the Black Moon Howl?

00:33:18.180 --> 00:33:20.220
No. Not yet.

00:33:20.220 --> 00:33:27.240
See the boy. He was born in a time before names;&nbsp;
there weren’t enough humans around to need them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:27.240 --> 00:33:33.780
back then. He was one of a handful occupying&nbsp;
a coastal village, using a tongue long since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:33.780 --> 00:33:40.860
dead. They eked out a simple life - hunting,&nbsp;
gathering, fishing. The only thing on most of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:40.860 --> 00:33:47.700
their minds was surviving to see the next sunrise.&nbsp;
Yes, a simple life, free of complications.

00:33:47.700 --> 00:33:50.340
Until The Hermit appeared.

00:33:50.340 --> 00:33:55.140
The Boy would remember this man&nbsp;
for an eternity. Haggard and thin;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:55.140 --> 00:34:02.700
skin weathered by time and pain. A man that,&nbsp;
emaciated, walking with a long, gnarled cane that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:02.700 --> 00:34:09.060
honestly looked healthier than he did, shouldn’t&nbsp;
be alive. Even the Boy, who had scarcely seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:09.060 --> 00:34:15.960
beyond the bounds of his village, knew that the&nbsp;
Hermit was unnatural. An aberration. An anomaly.

00:34:15.960 --> 00:34:20.700
He walked into the center of the&nbsp;
village, sat down on a large stone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:20.700 --> 00:34:26.400
and waited. Nobody dared ask his&nbsp;
business, nor what the Hermit waited for.

00:34:26.400 --> 00:34:31.080
Then, a few days later, the Black Moon howled.

00:34:31.080 --> 00:34:36.120
The Boy saw the village’s youngest hunter&nbsp;
freeze one evening while out on a walk. Not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:36.120 --> 00:34:43.740
simply stand still, but freeze. Then, for an&nbsp;
instant, he became solid black. A coal statue.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:43.740 --> 00:34:50.460
And as soon as he’d changed, he was gone.&nbsp;
Obliterated. Not a trace of him remained.

00:34:50.460 --> 00:34:55.740
Such is the power of the Black Moon. It&nbsp;
can make any conscious being disappear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:55.740 --> 00:35:01.500
in an instant. Turned black, then wiped&nbsp;
from our plane of existence, never to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:01.500 --> 00:35:07.800
seen again. Its choice of victim seemed, at&nbsp;
each instance, to be utterly random, but it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:07.800 --> 00:35:14.400
would come for all who lived eventually. This is&nbsp;
known to some as the howling of the Black Moon.

00:35:14.400 --> 00:35:20.400
Later that same night, the Boy found himself&nbsp;
talking to the Hermit, who asked with small,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:20.400 --> 00:35:26.460
frantic eyes what he had seen. When the Boy&nbsp;
told him, he let out a deep, rattling sigh.

00:35:26.460 --> 00:35:32.520
The Boy, curious, asked him if he knew about&nbsp;
the nightmare he’d just witnessed. The Hermit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:32.520 --> 00:35:37.860
looked up. He’d been the first one, in the&nbsp;
Hermit’s millennia of pursuit, that had ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:37.860 --> 00:35:44.460
asked. In that moment, he knew he had found his&nbsp;
successor in the hunt for the Death of Ages.

00:35:44.460 --> 00:35:50.640
The Hermit told the Boy it went by many&nbsp;
names. The Great Finale, the Pale King,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:50.640 --> 00:35:57.180
but most common of all was the Black Moon. The&nbsp;
entity existed beyond the veil of our reality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:57.180 --> 00:36:04.140
a creature of pure energy, though nobody could&nbsp;
really be sure of its true nature. The Hermit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:04.140 --> 00:36:09.720
had been tracking it, learning about it, and&nbsp;
trying to destroy it for thousands of years.

00:36:09.720 --> 00:36:15.420
And yet, it only took him four pathetic&nbsp;
minutes to tell the Boy everything he knew.

00:36:15.420 --> 00:36:20.760
The Boy, knowing still that something about&nbsp;
the Hermit was unnatural, asked how he came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:20.760 --> 00:36:27.900
to be in this position. The Hermit told the boy&nbsp;
he was the Counterbalance, a kind of chosen one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:27.900 --> 00:36:33.360
destined to face and perhaps even defeat&nbsp;
the Black Moon someday. The Counterbalance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:33.360 --> 00:36:37.680
receives a number of truly extraordinary&nbsp;
gifts for inheriting the responsibility:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:38.520 --> 00:36:42.780
Eternal life, eternal youth,&nbsp;
near physical immortality.

00:36:42.780 --> 00:36:49.260
But they will be haunted by their purpose, doomed&nbsp;
to watch everyone they love die around them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:49.260 --> 00:36:55.620
as they continue to hunt their only true&nbsp;
equal and opposite, the Black Moon itself.

00:36:55.620 --> 00:37:01.380
The Hermit, in his own eyes, had failed&nbsp;
at his duty. He had grown weary, and now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:01.380 --> 00:37:07.140
he needed to pass the duty of Counterbalance&nbsp;
onto another. That other would be the Boy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:07.140 --> 00:37:12.180
He felt a sudden and profound change, along&nbsp;
with the knowledge that nothing would ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:12.180 --> 00:37:19.320
be the same again. He was no longer just&nbsp;
the Boy. Now, he was the Counterbalance.

00:37:19.320 --> 00:37:24.960
He watched the Hermit give him a slight nod out&nbsp;
of respect, and then crumble into dust before his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:24.960 --> 00:37:33.000
eyes. The Boy, the Counterbalance, looked up at&nbsp;
the sky and saw the stars twinkling. So bright, so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:33.000 --> 00:37:39.060
beautiful. Little did he know, his battle with the&nbsp;
Black Moon would outlast every single one of them.

00:37:39.060 --> 00:37:43.320
Does the Black Moon howl? Not without blood.

00:37:43.320 --> 00:37:49.680
The Boy grew into a man, as his village aged&nbsp;
and then died around him. Decades passed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:49.680 --> 00:37:53.880
then centuries, then millennia.&nbsp;
Tens of thousands of years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:53.880 --> 00:38:00.780
watching humanity develop and grow around him as&nbsp;
he continued his pursuit of that one elusive foe.

00:38:00.780 --> 00:38:05.700
As science and diagnostic technology gained&nbsp;
ground, absorbing and then evolving beyond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:05.700 --> 00:38:10.560
all the old superstitions, the Counterbalance&nbsp;
gained a better understanding of the Black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:10.560 --> 00:38:17.220
Moon - though even then, it still remained&nbsp;
essentially a stranger. The entity was entropic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:17.220 --> 00:38:24.000
a being of pure randomness and chaos without&nbsp;
consistent form. It didn’t exist in our universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:24.000 --> 00:38:28.320
but it could exercise its influence&nbsp;
here with so-called “Obliteration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:28.320 --> 00:38:33.360
Events” - much like the horrible fate that&nbsp;
befell the young hunter from the village.

00:38:33.360 --> 00:38:37.200
But that was only the&nbsp;
proverbial tip of the iceberg.

00:38:37.200 --> 00:38:43.380
The Counterbalance tracked and noted obliteration&nbsp;
events. They were exceedingly rare, at first.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:43.380 --> 00:38:49.140
Something that occurred once every thousand years&nbsp;
or so, like a terrible curse. But he couldn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:49.140 --> 00:38:54.420
help but notice a concerning trend emerging.&nbsp;
It started happening once a century...then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:54.420 --> 00:38:59.400
once a decade. He could feel the terrible&nbsp;
future stretching out in front of him. How,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:59.400 --> 00:39:04.860
over their shared eternity, the Black&nbsp;
Moon would gain more and more ground.

00:39:04.860 --> 00:39:10.860
Would there come a day where it took someone&nbsp;
once a year? One a month? A week, a day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:10.860 --> 00:39:18.540
an hour, a minute, a second? It’d spell the end&nbsp;
of all conscious life. A total victory for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:18.540 --> 00:39:26.520
Black Moon, the End of the Universe, the Death&nbsp;
of Ages. A complete existential obliteration.

00:39:26.520 --> 00:39:31.440
He was swept up in a sobering realization:&nbsp;
He couldn’t win this fight alone.

00:39:31.440 --> 00:39:36.900
However, while his hunt for the Black Moon had&nbsp;
been largely fruitless, the Counterbalance had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:36.900 --> 00:39:42.720
discovered many other things along the way,&nbsp;
Strange creatures, objects with extraordinary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:42.720 --> 00:39:48.360
powers, and events that couldn’t be explained&nbsp;
with rational science. Perhaps something among&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:48.360 --> 00:39:54.720
these oddities, these anomalies, would&nbsp;
hold the key to defeating his timeless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:54.720 --> 00:40:01.200
enemy. And it hadn’t just been these objects,&nbsp;
entities, and events, he’d also discovered some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:01.200 --> 00:40:07.980
truly exceptional people on his travels. Minds and&nbsp;
skills that rivaled even his own, despite his age.

00:40:07.980 --> 00:40:11.040
Perhaps they would be the ones to help him win.

00:40:11.040 --> 00:40:16.200
With the thirteen most brilliant and trusted&nbsp;
people the Counterbalance ever met, he decided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:16.200 --> 00:40:22.500
to form a Council. And from this Council, they&nbsp;
forged and directed an organization dedicated to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:22.500 --> 00:40:27.900
understanding and counteracting the strange&nbsp;
in all its forms, with the secret hope that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:27.900 --> 00:40:32.460
their search into darkness would yield&nbsp;
the answer to the Black Moon’s downfall.

00:40:32.460 --> 00:40:38.220
He called it the SCP Foundation. They&nbsp;
would Secure the anomalous, Contain it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:38.220 --> 00:40:44.160
and Protect all of humanity from its influence.&nbsp;
The Counterbalance also took on a new title,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:44.700 --> 00:40:50.880
befitting of his new role: The Administrator. And&nbsp;
even the Black Moon itself was given a moniker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:50.880 --> 00:40:54.180
in hopes of robbing it of&nbsp;
some of its frightening power.

00:40:54.180 --> 00:40:57.060
SCP-001.

00:40:57.060 --> 00:41:01.440
Does the Black Moon howl? Only at the blind.

00:41:01.440 --> 00:41:07.320
The year was now 1987. The SCP Foundation&nbsp;
had been operating for over a century,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:07.320 --> 00:41:11.100
and thanks to their secret possession&nbsp;
of anomalous wisdom and technology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:11.100 --> 00:41:16.740
their own advancement was thousands of years ahead&nbsp;
of the rest of humanity. While there still wasn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:16.740 --> 00:41:21.720
a silver bullet solution to the Black Moon,&nbsp;
and its deadly howls were becoming all the more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:21.720 --> 00:41:27.300
frequent as the decades went on, the Foundation&nbsp;
did have some irons in the fire to combat it.

00:41:27.300 --> 00:41:32.400
Their ability to gather intel on both the&nbsp;
entity itself and its obliteration events&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:32.400 --> 00:41:37.980
had improved considerably, thanks to their&nbsp;
new global information network. Their top&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:37.980 --> 00:41:43.140
minds were also working on a highly classified&nbsp;
device known as the Singular Conceptual Bunker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:43.140 --> 00:41:49.020
which may one day come in handy for combating&nbsp;
the extra-dimensional entity directly. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:49.020 --> 00:41:53.460
the most valuable piece of information they&nbsp;
ever gathered about the Black Moon was this:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:53.460 --> 00:42:00.720
It couldn’t howl when it was being watched. The&nbsp;
very act of engaged observation defanged it.

00:42:00.720 --> 00:42:06.120
The problem is, how can you observe something&nbsp;
that doesn’t technically exist inside your own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:06.120 --> 00:42:12.480
reality? In order to pull this off, the Foundation&nbsp;
would need to get extremely creative. Thankfully,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:12.480 --> 00:42:16.440
creative solutions to strange problems&nbsp;
are the Foundation’s specialty.

00:42:16.440 --> 00:42:22.980
Flash forward to 1993. Enter Dr. Moto, a&nbsp;
brilliant young scientist and conceptual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:22.980 --> 00:42:27.480
engineer working for the SCP Foundation.&nbsp;
With The Administrator’s consultation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:27.480 --> 00:42:33.000
he started the KEY Project, an arm&nbsp;
of the wider Project Oromasdes - the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:33.000 --> 00:42:37.800
umbrella initiative for using modified anomalous&nbsp;
objects in the battle against the Black Moon.

00:42:37.800 --> 00:42:43.020
The goal of the KEY Project was relatively&nbsp;
simple: If people couldn’t observe the Black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:43.020 --> 00:42:48.000
Moon directly, then the Foundation could make&nbsp;
proxies of the Black Moon that could be observed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:48.000 --> 00:42:53.280
almost like a kind of voodoo doll. These&nbsp;
new anomalies would only need to satisfy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:53.280 --> 00:42:59.100
three criteria: The inability to operate when&nbsp;
being observed, a hostility to conscious life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:59.100 --> 00:43:04.560
and the ability to end conscious life of their&nbsp;
own volition when not being observed. Through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:04.560 --> 00:43:09.660
conceptual engineering, a link theoretically&nbsp;
could be forged between these objects and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:09.660 --> 00:43:14.220
the Black Moon, allowing observation of&nbsp;
them to stop the obliteration events.

00:43:14.220 --> 00:43:19.740
However, despite being a good idea in theory,&nbsp;
Dr. Moto’s efforts were marred with errors and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:19.740 --> 00:43:25.500
tragedies. One object wasn’t deadly enough, simply&nbsp;
appearing behind people in a threatening pose when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:25.500 --> 00:43:30.480
they weren’t looking. Another one killed purely&nbsp;
through collateral damage - a giant sculpture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:30.480 --> 00:43:35.460
of a human head that immediately attempted&nbsp;
escape by barging through Site-01 - the center&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:35.460 --> 00:43:41.100
for Anti-Black Moon operations - and killing&nbsp;
nineteen people in the process. Another one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:41.100 --> 00:43:46.560
of Moto’s objects, a huge black sphere, simply&nbsp;
immediately exploded, killing twelve people.

00:43:46.560 --> 00:43:51.720
And in the most horrific misstep of all,&nbsp;
one of Moto’s objects caused a mass death&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:51.720 --> 00:43:57.780
event in a nearby hotel, where 142 people&nbsp;
were spontaneously incinerated when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:57.780 --> 00:44:04.200
object - a series of interlocking stalactites&nbsp;
and stalagmites - was left unobserved for 0.2&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:04.200 --> 00:44:09.060
seconds. Almost all of Moto’s objects were&nbsp;
terminated in the aftermath, either being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:09.060 --> 00:44:15.420
too useless or too dangerous to keep around. The&nbsp;
young scientist felt a deep shame, but forged on.

00:44:15.420 --> 00:44:21.060
He made one truly brilliant creation that&nbsp;
satisfied all the criteria: A sculpture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:21.060 --> 00:44:25.980
incapable of moving while being watched, but&nbsp;
would snap the neck of the nearest conscious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:25.980 --> 00:44:30.300
entity if left unobserved for even a&nbsp;
fraction of a second. Its relatively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:30.300 --> 00:44:35.040
minimal killing left it easy to contain&nbsp;
without causing mass deaths, and despite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:35.040 --> 00:44:40.260
all the other deaths that had sadly occurred&nbsp;
during the KEY Project, Dr. Moto believed that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:40.260 --> 00:44:45.660
the lives saved in the long run by stopping the&nbsp;
Black Moon’s howls would justify the sacrifice.

00:44:45.660 --> 00:44:50.100
The problem is...the KEY&nbsp;
Project didn’t stop anything.

00:44:50.100 --> 00:44:54.840
Not long after this, there was the first&nbsp;
recorded double obliteration event in Rome,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:54.840 --> 00:45:00.420
where a young tourist couple had both been&nbsp;
obliterated simultaneously. All the deaths&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:00.420 --> 00:45:05.640
in the KEY Project had been for nothing. The&nbsp;
Black Moon was only getting more powerful.

00:45:05.640 --> 00:45:10.200
The shame and the guilt was too much for&nbsp;
Dr. Moto. He left a note in his office,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:10.200 --> 00:45:15.600
reading, “We've been looking at nothing. I'm&nbsp;
sorry, Administrator. I've failed you, sir.”

00:45:15.600 --> 00:45:19.920
Moto’s corpse was later found in the&nbsp;
sculpture’s temporary containment chamber,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:19.920 --> 00:45:25.500
his neck snapped. The KEY Project was, in&nbsp;
summary, shut down and its one surviving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:25.500 --> 00:45:33.780
creation transported to Site-19 in late 1993,&nbsp;
where it was designated as SCP-173. Another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:33.780 --> 00:45:38.340
painful failure for the Administrator.&nbsp;
Back to the drawing board once more.

00:45:38.340 --> 00:45:42.540
Does the Black Moon howl? Not while stars shine.

00:45:42.540 --> 00:45:48.660
millennia stretched on. Almost everyone died,&nbsp;
except The Administrator, thanks to his gift - or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:48.660 --> 00:45:55.200
perhaps curse - as the Counterbalance to the&nbsp;
Black Moon. Science marched on, the SCP Foundation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:55.200 --> 00:46:02.100
marched on, but all this progress, all this power,&nbsp;
was nothing against the incomprehensible influence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:02.100 --> 00:46:08.460
of SCP-001. The Black Moon was howling more&nbsp;
frequently than ever, all the way up to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:08.460 --> 00:46:14.760
year 3156, when the Foundation launched the SEEK&nbsp;
Project under the support of Project Oromasdes.

00:46:14.760 --> 00:46:18.720
As more and more people were wiped&nbsp;
out in frequent obliteration events,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:18.720 --> 00:46:23.640
the Administrator became painfully aware that&nbsp;
perhaps the answers to the Black Moon problem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:23.640 --> 00:46:29.220
wouldn’t be found on earth. Using state of the art&nbsp;
technology, with a little help from the anomalous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:29.220 --> 00:46:34.260
the SCP Foundation began work on an&nbsp;
autonomous space-faring vessel that could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:34.260 --> 00:46:40.500
search the stars for the key to the Black Moon’s&nbsp;
destruction. It was an awe-inspiring creation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:40.500 --> 00:46:47.160
a huge craft powered by artificial intelligence,&nbsp;
with a universal translator, cryogenic units,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:47.160 --> 00:46:51.060
and hundreds of autonomous drones&nbsp;
to perform more targeted searches.

00:46:51.060 --> 00:46:55.980
SEEK was waved off into the unforgiving&nbsp;
depths of space. The Administrator could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:55.980 --> 00:46:59.400
only hope that it would come&nbsp;
back with worthwhile answers.

00:46:59.400 --> 00:47:04.200
The first of the three notable planets SEEK&nbsp;
arrived on was one theoretically capable of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:04.200 --> 00:47:10.020
supporting human life, except for its brutal&nbsp;
and constant blizzards and snowstorms. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:10.020 --> 00:47:13.560
SEEK’s drones were deployed, they&nbsp;
did discover signs of civilization,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:13.560 --> 00:47:20.220
based around sentient spherical creatures, but no&nbsp;
signs of actual life remained. Records and statues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:20.220 --> 00:47:24.120
found across the planet seemed to indicate&nbsp;
that the Black Moon was responsible for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:24.120 --> 00:47:29.160
destruction of the planet’s civilization, causing&nbsp;
so many obliteration events that the remaining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:29.160 --> 00:47:35.400
survivors went mad from the fear and stress,&nbsp;
leading to mass death in the ensuing chaos.

00:47:35.400 --> 00:47:40.980
The next planet was discovered centuries&nbsp;
later, in the year 3499. While this planet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:40.980 --> 00:47:45.840
could also theoretically support human life,&nbsp;
it suffered from frequent volcanic eruptions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:45.840 --> 00:47:50.700
that rendered much of its surface a flaming&nbsp;
mess. However, there were still the dormant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:50.700 --> 00:47:55.440
ruins of a once advanced civilization of&nbsp;
conscious beings. Much like the prior planet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:55.440 --> 00:48:00.180
they’d been driven extinct by Black Moon&nbsp;
obliteration events a century before the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:00.180 --> 00:48:05.640
SEEK even arrived. Unlike the last planet,&nbsp;
however, it seems that they accepted their fate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:05.640 --> 00:48:11.820
and went gently into the night. The planet was now&nbsp;
overrun by billions of armored, bat-like creatures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:11.820 --> 00:48:17.460
that operated on pure instinct, and thus, were&nbsp;
not considered conscious enough to be obliterated.

00:48:17.460 --> 00:48:23.280
The final planet was reached in 3764,&nbsp;
and was the most fruitful of the three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:23.280 --> 00:48:29.100
discoveries. This planet was hyper-advanced, fully&nbsp;
urbanized and covered in sprawling megacities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:29.100 --> 00:48:33.900
with records and technology over a thousand&nbsp;
years ahead of Earth. Before the Black Moon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:33.900 --> 00:48:38.340
killed almost all of them, they were a&nbsp;
species of humanoid telepathic fungi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:38.340 --> 00:48:43.500
and had developed an awareness of the Black Moon’s&nbsp;
existence that was on par with that of humanity’s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:43.500 --> 00:48:49.020
They even had their own equivalent of the SCP&nbsp;
Foundation actively working on countermeasures.

00:48:49.020 --> 00:48:54.480
And most amazingly of all, SEEK found one&nbsp;
surviving member of the species on the planet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:54.480 --> 00:48:59.760
cryogenically frozen. The craft was immediately&nbsp;
instructed to collect the survivor and return&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:59.760 --> 00:49:04.440
home for interrogation. The Administrator&nbsp;
was preparing for what could have been the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:04.440 --> 00:49:09.360
most important conversation since he met the&nbsp;
Hermit, all those thousands of years ago.

00:49:09.360 --> 00:49:13.560
Does the Black Moon howl? Only when waning.

00:49:13.560 --> 00:49:18.840
When the surviving creature, codenamed SAGE,&nbsp;
was returned to Earth, The Administrator was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:18.840 --> 00:49:22.920
eager to finally speak with it. Like&nbsp;
the rest of its now-extinct species,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:22.920 --> 00:49:28.440
SAGE spoke through powerful telepathic mindwaves,&nbsp;
which only The Administrator - thanks to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:28.440 --> 00:49:33.060
his Counterbalance abilities - was able to&nbsp;
receive at close range without being harmed.

00:49:33.060 --> 00:49:38.520
Incidentally, it wasn’t long until the very fact&nbsp;
of the Administrator’s nature as a Counterbalance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:38.520 --> 00:49:43.380
came up in the mental conversation.&nbsp;
SAGE could tell, just by being in his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:43.380 --> 00:49:47.640
presence. They discovered a number of vital&nbsp;
truths over their brief time communicating:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:47.640 --> 00:49:52.920
That SAGE’s survival had been pure luck, for&nbsp;
starters. The Black Moon is still very much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:52.920 --> 00:49:58.740
capable of obliterating conscious beings in an&nbsp;
unconscious state. The Administrator also learned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:58.740 --> 00:50:03.900
that he was merely the latest in an extremely&nbsp;
long line of Counterbalances across time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:03.900 --> 00:50:09.960
space, and species, though everyone but&nbsp;
him had waived the duty, passed it on.

00:50:09.960 --> 00:50:13.200
SAGE had one question to ask&nbsp;
the Administrator in turn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:13.200 --> 00:50:20.460
“What is SCB?” The Singular Conceptual Bunker,&nbsp;
being worked on and perfected for thousands of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:20.460 --> 00:50:24.840
years by now, by the Foundation’s top&nbsp;
scientists and conceptual engineers.

00:50:24.840 --> 00:50:32.220
The Administrator replied, “Victory. But it&nbsp;
will take a very, very long time.” Specifically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:32.220 --> 00:50:38.280
so long that he would see the stars go out&nbsp;
around him, one by one. Shocked, SAGE asked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:38.280 --> 00:50:43.320
him what good victory would do him then. Rather&nbsp;
than say it aloud, he replied with a thought.

00:50:43.320 --> 00:50:48.960
SAGE paused, and said, “I see. How&nbsp;
blasphemous of you. Hopefully it works.”

00:50:48.960 --> 00:50:53.700
After this, the Administrator proceeded to&nbsp;
the Singular Conceptual Bunker and entered it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:53.700 --> 00:50:59.160
leaving instructions for the Foundation to&nbsp;
be run by a newly formed O5 Council in his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:59.160 --> 00:51:05.700
indefinite absence. Thousands of years later,&nbsp;
in the year 5011, SAGE spoke one more time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:05.700 --> 00:51:11.940
repeating the words, “Hopefully, hopefully”&nbsp;
before turning solid black, and disappearing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:11.940 --> 00:51:17.760
The Black Moon had claimed one more victim,&nbsp;
but billions more had gone in the interim.

00:51:17.760 --> 00:51:23.100
The Administrator had no more answers&nbsp;
to give. At least, no more answers that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:23.100 --> 00:51:28.020
anyone but him would understand. He was&nbsp;
inside the Singular Conceptual Bunker now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:28.020 --> 00:51:32.820
loaded into a device known as TOME&nbsp;
- an experimental memorial module,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:32.820 --> 00:51:38.580
meant to pick up and record all the last messages&nbsp;
of every dying civilization across the universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:38.580 --> 00:51:45.840
when the time finally came. All he could do&nbsp;
was wait. And wait was exactly what we did.

00:51:45.840 --> 00:51:48.240
Does the Black Moon howl?

00:51:48.240 --> 00:51:50.400
Yes. Yes it does.

00:51:50.400 --> 00:51:57.120
Years pass. Too many to count. It’s a time after&nbsp;
names now, and TOME sits in the very center,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:57.120 --> 00:52:02.580
drinking in the end of the universe. The last&nbsp;
of all the human colonies across the universe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:02.580 --> 00:52:10.860
were obliterated by the Black Moon back in the&nbsp;
year 7329, so, so, so long ago, but some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:10.860 --> 00:52:16.800
final messages of fear, panic, and distress&nbsp;
still echoed around the Administrator’s mind.

00:52:16.800 --> 00:52:23.220
"Hello, is there anyone here? We — we require&nbsp;
assistance! There's … it's … it's taking people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:23.220 --> 00:52:28.260
every day! We need help! There's barely&nbsp;
anyone left! We need help! Hello? Hello?!"

00:52:28.260 --> 00:52:34.800
"Cabal 09:43! We have abandoned the false&nbsp;
flesh! We have abandoned the false flesh! The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:34.800 --> 00:52:42.360
shepherds crook broken 'neath my knee! Cabal&nbsp;
09:43! Cabal 09:43! Forgive us! Forgive us!"

00:52:42.360 --> 00:52:46.440
"We're going to leave this on.&nbsp;
It's so dark outside now. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:46.440 --> 00:52:49.200
blotting out the sun. It's … I have to go now."

00:52:51.060 --> 00:52:51.720
"RESPOND•FIRST•CONVENIENCE&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:54.480 --> 00:52:57.900
EMERGENCY•SITUATION•DEVELOPING&nbsp;
REQUIRE•ADDITIONAL•RESOURCES."

00:52:57.900 --> 00:53:02.400
"My fault. Your fault. Our fault.&nbsp;
My fault. Your fault. Our fault. My&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:02.400 --> 00:53:04.620
fault. Your fault. Our fault. Rip out&nbsp;
my brain now. Rip out my brain now!"

00:53:04.620 --> 00:53:13.560
And a small child, the last on Earth, simply&nbsp;
asking, “Hello?” into an indifferent microphone.

00:53:13.560 --> 00:53:19.320
But the Administrator had to wait, as&nbsp;
the Singular Conceptual Bunker became the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:19.320 --> 00:53:25.020
Solitary Conceptual Bunker. He was the last&nbsp;
conscious being in the universe, and still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:25.020 --> 00:53:30.600
he needed to wait, as the stars went dark&nbsp;
outside. Only when there was nothing outside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:30.600 --> 00:53:35.640
but black was it finally time for the&nbsp;
Counterbalance’s long game to pay off.

00:53:35.640 --> 00:53:40.740
There was nothing left of our universe.&nbsp;
The only thing here was the SCB,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:40.740 --> 00:53:47.100
and the Black Moon itself. With everything else&nbsp;
gone, the Black Moon only had one conscious being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:47.100 --> 00:53:52.620
left to obliterate. It opened the door to the&nbsp;
Solitary Conceptual Bunker, and stepped inside.

00:53:52.620 --> 00:53:59.820
This...this doesn’t make sense. How can the&nbsp;
Black Moon, an entity beyond our dimension,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:59.820 --> 00:54:05.220
beyond physical form, take a step?&nbsp;
Good question. The same question,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:05.220 --> 00:54:09.180
incidentally, that was going through&nbsp;
the Black Moon’s mind as it entered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:09.180 --> 00:54:14.100
the bunker. It didn’t look at all how&nbsp;
the entity expected: It was like a bar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:14.100 --> 00:54:20.100
a counter with rows of bottles behind it,&nbsp;
a jukebox playing in the corner. A man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:20.100 --> 00:54:26.040
stood behind the bar, cleaning glasses.
The Counterbalance. The Administrator.

00:54:26.040 --> 00:54:31.620
He said, "Well, there you are! Certainly took&nbsp;
your time. Can I pour you a little something?"

00:54:31.620 --> 00:54:36.540
This only served to increase the Black&nbsp;
Moon’s confusion. It had form here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:36.540 --> 00:54:42.600
dark smoke compressed into a vaguely&nbsp;
humanoid shape. It could speak? It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:42.600 --> 00:54:47.460
could think? None of this made any&nbsp;
sense. The being that’d just wiped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:47.460 --> 00:54:53.520
out all conscious life and seen the very death&nbsp;
of the universe was truly and utterly confused.

00:54:53.520 --> 00:54:58.620
The Administrator just seemed to be enjoying&nbsp;
himself, preparing for a confrontation hundreds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:58.620 --> 00:55:03.480
of billions of years in the making. The&nbsp;
Singular Conceptual Bunker - or perhaps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:03.480 --> 00:55:10.620
the Singular Containment Bunker - was a truly&nbsp;
ingenious creation. A place of pure ideas, where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:10.620 --> 00:55:17.880
everything inside was on the same level. Here,&nbsp;
there were no immortals. No Gods. Just ideas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:17.880 --> 00:55:25.140
on the same level playing field. And it was time&nbsp;
for the Black Moon’s idea to come to an end.

00:55:25.140 --> 00:55:29.160
It was a trap, and the&nbsp;
entire universe was the bait.

00:55:29.160 --> 00:55:32.880
Without warning, the Administrator pulled&nbsp;
up a shotgun from underneath the table,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:32.880 --> 00:55:36.060
and unleashed both barrels&nbsp;
into the Black Moon’s chest.

00:55:36.060 --> 00:55:40.200
The creature took the hit and fought back,&nbsp;
dragging the Administrator to the ground,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:40.200 --> 00:55:45.480
beating him, strangling him. He could feel the&nbsp;
light fading under the monster’s relentless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:45.480 --> 00:55:50.760
assault, until he managed to get his desperate&nbsp;
hands on a glass ashtray. He beat the monster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:50.760 --> 00:55:54.780
over the head with it until its grip&nbsp;
loosened, and he was able to slide out.

00:55:54.780 --> 00:55:59.220
There, the killer of the universe was on the&nbsp;
ground before him. He grabbed the monster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:59.220 --> 00:56:04.380
held it in place, and beat it to death.&nbsp;
He was gravely injured by the battle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:04.380 --> 00:56:11.400
but The Black Moon was no more. Here, in&nbsp;
the Singular Conceptual Bunker, he had won.

00:56:11.400 --> 00:56:15.120
The Administrator, no longer the&nbsp;
counterbalance in the absence of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:15.120 --> 00:56:18.720
the Black Moon, hobbled over to&nbsp;
the jukebox, producing a single,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:18.720 --> 00:56:24.960
beautiful coin from his pocket. He pushed the coin&nbsp;
into the slot, wheezed a pained breath, and said:

00:56:24.960 --> 00:56:33.900
“The thing is … this place is only information.&nbsp;
T-There's nothing else out there. Not even matter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:33.900 --> 00:56:40.680
The universe closed its doors a long time ago. But&nbsp;
this place can go from information back to matter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:40.680 --> 00:56:47.520
with just the press of a button. L-Let's see what&nbsp;
happens when we introduce something to nothing…”

00:56:47.520 --> 00:56:52.440
For a second, it looks as though he&nbsp;
might fall, but he doesn’t. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:52.440 --> 00:56:58.380
he slams the button on the jukebox and, with&nbsp;
a relieved laugh, says, “Let there be light.”

00:56:58.380 --> 00:57:00.840
And there was light.

00:57:00.840 --> 00:57:04.920
From gigantic, indestructible,&nbsp;
self-regenerating reptiles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:04.920 --> 00:57:10.860
to enormous, tentacled, telepathic organisms,&nbsp;
it should come as no surprise that the SCP&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:10.860 --> 00:57:16.740
Foundation has gone head to head against a lot of&nbsp;
large-scale aggressors - or LSAs - in its time.

00:57:16.740 --> 00:57:22.020
Naturally, a creature of heightened size and&nbsp;
aggression can often prove challenging to contain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:22.020 --> 00:57:27.900
and the threat these LSAs pose is often far&nbsp;
too big to ignore. But anyone familiar with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:27.900 --> 00:57:32.280
the Foundation will tell you, they’re not&nbsp;
above using any methods necessary to keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:32.280 --> 00:57:37.680
these creatures contained: huge vats&nbsp;
of molecular acid, impenetrable cells,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:37.680 --> 00:57:45.240
disposable, D-Class personnel, even other SCPs.&nbsp;
But what other SCPs could possibly be big enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:45.240 --> 00:57:49.800
and tough enough, to handle some of&nbsp;
the Foundation’s biggest and baddest?

00:57:49.800 --> 00:57:56.160
Meet SCP-5514, otherwise&nbsp;
known as “The Dragon Slayer.”

00:57:56.160 --> 00:58:02.520
While it might sound like something out of&nbsp;
an anime, SCP-5514 is a massive robotic mech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:02.520 --> 00:58:08.160
designed to take on the worst other SCPs can throw&nbsp;
at it. For any who are unfamiliar with the term,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:08.160 --> 00:58:13.440
a mech, or mecha, usually refers to an&nbsp;
upright-standing machine or automaton&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:13.440 --> 00:58:17.400
controlled by a human pilot. What&nbsp;
distinguishes a mech from a vehicle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:17.400 --> 00:58:23.100
is their often-humanoid shape, standing bipedally,&nbsp;
and they are often hundreds of meters tall.

00:58:23.100 --> 00:58:29.340
All of this is true of SCP-5514, and in&nbsp;
fact, given that it requires a trained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:29.340 --> 00:58:33.660
member of Foundation staff to operate it,&nbsp;
the mech itself requires very little in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:33.660 --> 00:58:38.100
the way of containment. Only members of&nbsp;
Mobile Task Force Eta-5 are trained and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:38.100 --> 00:58:45.000
authorized to pilot SCP-5514. This is one&nbsp;
of the SCP Foundation’s specialized units,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:45.000 --> 00:58:51.960
specifically designed to deal with the threat of&nbsp;
Large-Scale Aggressors, much like SCP-5514 itself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:51.960 --> 00:58:57.960
But SCP-5514 wasn’t discovered or captured&nbsp;
by the Foundation to use for the containment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:57.960 --> 00:59:03.600
of LSAs, nor was it stolen from a foreign&nbsp;
military or found buried under the ground.

00:59:03.600 --> 00:59:07.560
Then, where did it come from? And who built it?

00:59:07.560 --> 00:59:12.300
Working with the Global Occult Coalition and&nbsp;
the government of Hy-Brasil, an anomalous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:12.300 --> 00:59:17.940
island off the west coast of Ireland, the&nbsp;
Foundation themselves constructed SCP-5514,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:17.940 --> 00:59:24.900
using various anomalous methods and techniques.&nbsp;
In 1988, a Foundation site was destroyed by an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:24.900 --> 00:59:30.120
unidentified LSA, highlighting the inadequacy&nbsp;
of the current defenses against these larger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:30.120 --> 00:59:34.020
more damage-resistant creatures.&nbsp;
The Foundation, the Coalition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:34.020 --> 00:59:40.560
and Hy-Brasil formed a joint operation,&nbsp;
The KEY Project, and examined SCP-2406,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:40.560 --> 00:59:46.260
an automaton ninety-three meters tall,&nbsp;
thought to be created by ancient Mekhanites.

00:59:46.260 --> 00:59:50.100
Together, the KEY Project opted to&nbsp;
create their own, similar machine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:50.100 --> 00:59:55.260
viewing it as the best way to defend against&nbsp;
further incursions with Large-Scale Aggressors.

00:59:55.260 --> 01:00:02.100
The construction of SCP-5514 began in 1990. The&nbsp;
intention of all parties involved in the KEY&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:02.100 --> 01:00:06.600
Project, including the Foundation, was that&nbsp;
The Dragon Slayer would be deployed in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:06.600 --> 01:00:12.000
event of an attack by an LSA. It would arrive&nbsp;
at cities under attack and immediately engage&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:12.000 --> 01:00:17.160
Large-Scale Aggressors in combat. Building of&nbsp;
the mech continued at a consistent pace for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:17.160 --> 01:00:24.120
eight years. However, it was the occurrence&nbsp;
of SCP-5391, and subsequent intervention by&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:24.120 --> 01:00:30.240
the O5 Council, that accelerated the creation&nbsp;
of The Dragon Slayer, by any means necessary.

01:00:30.240 --> 01:00:35.400
On June 30th 1998, a number of&nbsp;
seismic disturbances were detected,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:35.400 --> 01:00:40.560
including tsunamis, tremors, and volcanic&nbsp;
activity both underwater and above-ground.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:40.560 --> 01:00:44.220
What followed was the appearance&nbsp;
of multiple Large-Scale Aggressors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:44.220 --> 01:00:51.660
which would soon become designated as SCP-5391.&nbsp;
The exact kind of scenario that The Dragon Slayer&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:51.660 --> 01:00:57.180
was being built for had already arrived, and&nbsp;
the mech was still far from completion. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:57.180 --> 01:01:01.980
the Foundation and its allies deployed forces to&nbsp;
drive the enormous creatures back to the oceans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:01.980 --> 01:01:08.460
something needed to be done to bring&nbsp;
SCP-5514 into the fight, and fast.

01:01:08.460 --> 01:01:12.960
The O5 Council authorized the use of&nbsp;
anomalous materials in the continued&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:12.960 --> 01:01:18.000
construction of The Dragon Slayer, both to speed&nbsp;
up the process and have it ready for deployment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:18.000 --> 01:01:21.240
but also to give the mech every&nbsp;
advantage against the abundance&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:21.240 --> 01:01:29.640
of Large-Scale Aggressors from SCP-5391. As a&nbsp;
result, SCP-5514 was designed to incorporate&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:29.640 --> 01:01:34.980
features and technology far beyond that of&nbsp;
any conventional, military-grade weapons.

01:01:34.980 --> 01:01:40.020
The first hurdle: How do you power&nbsp;
a machine the size of SCP-5514?

01:01:40.020 --> 01:01:46.320
Naturally, with the most gigantic nuclear&nbsp;
furnace there is… the sun. More specifically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:46.320 --> 01:01:52.440
a perpetually-stable, miniaturized sun&nbsp;
known as SCP-037. Even though it’s only&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:52.440 --> 01:01:58.020
got a diameter of two inches, this little&nbsp;
sucker is better than premium fuel. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:58.020 --> 01:02:03.600
surface temperature of SCP-037 is around five&nbsp;
thousand Kelvin, generating plenty of energy&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:03.600 --> 01:02:10.200
to power the SCP-5514 mech. Stored in The&nbsp;
Dragon Slayer’s chest, this mini-sun is kept&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:10.200 --> 01:02:14.520
stable by sub-dimensional portals that vent&nbsp;
excess energy off of this plane of reality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:14.520 --> 01:02:18.840
stopping the mech, its pilot, and&nbsp;
anything around it from melting.

01:02:18.840 --> 01:02:23.460
In fact, SCP-037 produces so much juice that only&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:23.460 --> 01:02:28.320
one percent of its energy output&nbsp;
is enough to fully power SCP-5514.

01:02:28.320 --> 01:02:33.480
Now, that’s the power source sorted. But how do&nbsp;
you solve the weight problem? Given the sheer&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:33.480 --> 01:02:39.600
size of SCP-5514, it would be easy for it to be&nbsp;
cumbersome, and potentially cause catastrophic&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:39.600 --> 01:02:45.000
collateral damage to its surrounding area. Well,&nbsp;
the mech’s weight is a problem for somewhere else,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:45.000 --> 01:02:51.480
a whole other dimension in fact. Much like the&nbsp;
excess heat from its power source, various heavy&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:51.480 --> 01:02:57.180
portions of the SCP-5514 mech have had their&nbsp;
weight shunted off to a tiny pocket dimension.

01:02:57.180 --> 01:02:59.700
It was ensured, during the creation of the mech,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:59.700 --> 01:03:06.240
that this alteration was perfectly calculated so&nbsp;
that SCP-5514 wouldn’t lose any mass or density,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:06.240 --> 01:03:10.320
so it operates as if it were only&nbsp;
a fraction of its actual weight.

01:03:10.320 --> 01:03:15.240
Of course, being weightless makes flight&nbsp;
a whole lot easier. Oh did we forget to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:15.240 --> 01:03:21.360
mention that? SCP-5514 can fly as well.&nbsp;
This “feature” actually became a part&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:21.360 --> 01:03:25.860
of the mech completely by accident&nbsp;
during the construction of SCP-5514,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:25.860 --> 01:03:30.420
when an attempt to regulate the mech’s&nbsp;
internal circulation of air led to it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:30.420 --> 01:03:36.840
having its own gravity field. This allowed&nbsp;
SCP-5514 to fly, without the aid of any&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:36.840 --> 01:03:42.300
turbines or other means. While this was an&nbsp;
unintentional mistake, no attempt has ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:42.300 --> 01:03:48.540
been made to correct it, for fear that could&nbsp;
lead to SCP-5514 being grounded permanently.

01:03:48.540 --> 01:03:52.740
Naturally, going up against creatures&nbsp;
so large that they require their own&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:52.740 --> 01:03:59.460
sub-category means that SCP-5514 needs an&nbsp;
equally-formidable arsenal. So, let’s move on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:59.460 --> 01:04:05.100
to talk weaponry. Mounted on the mech’s shoulder&nbsp;
is a Beowulf-Sigurd Railgun, an anomalous weapon&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:05.100 --> 01:04:10.740
that also doesn’t obey the laws of physics at&nbsp;
all. The Beowulf-Sigurd uses altered gravity&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:10.740 --> 01:04:15.540
to affect the weight of its targets, causing&nbsp;
projectiles to impact with higher velocity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:15.540 --> 01:04:20.760
Even the thickest-skinned LSAs wouldn’t want to&nbsp;
be staring down the barrel end of one of those.

01:04:20.760 --> 01:04:28.320
Big guns aside, the SCP-5514 mech also wields&nbsp;
a Cold Iron Sword. Over sixty-five feet long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:28.320 --> 01:04:32.460
this weapon was contributed to The Dragon&nbsp;
Slayer by the Hy-Brasil Royal Court,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:32.460 --> 01:04:37.620
members of the collaborative KEY Project that&nbsp;
created the mech. Sure, a Large-Scale Aggressor&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:37.620 --> 01:04:42.480
with thicker hide might take a few extra swings&nbsp;
to draw blood, but it will feel those swings&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:42.480 --> 01:04:48.780
for a long time after, since any wounds inflicted&nbsp;
by the Cold Iron Sword will not regenerate.

01:04:48.780 --> 01:04:55.320
Serving as less of an offensive weapon, the&nbsp;
SCP-5514 mech also features a unique armament&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:55.320 --> 01:04:59.520
known as the Thousand Word Arrows.&nbsp;
As pretentious as it might sound,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:59.520 --> 01:05:04.980
within the mech are seven poets. Their role&nbsp;
is to write and recite poems that detail&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:04.980 --> 01:05:10.020
the slaying of monsters, and these recitals&nbsp;
are then broadcast from The Dragon Slayer.

01:05:10.020 --> 01:05:15.660
On the surface, this seems to have no practical&nbsp;
applications during a fight with LSAs, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:15.660 --> 01:05:20.940
the goal of the Thousand Word Arrows is a form&nbsp;
of psychological warfare. The recital of poems&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:20.940 --> 01:05:25.740
telling of the mech’s victory and the defeat of&nbsp;
Large-Scale Aggressors is intended to have the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:25.740 --> 01:05:32.460
effect of demoralizing SCP-5514’s adversaries,&nbsp;
while encouraging the pilot during combat.

01:05:32.460 --> 01:05:37.800
Additionally, worn atop the head of&nbsp;
the SCP-5514 mech, almost like a hat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:37.800 --> 01:05:43.740
is a discus with plasma-coated edges. If The&nbsp;
Dragon Slayer needs to deal damage at range,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:43.740 --> 01:05:48.300
then it can hurl this disc and recall it&nbsp;
immediately thanks to built-in electromagnets.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:48.300 --> 01:05:53.580
In emergency scenarios, if the Cold Iron&nbsp;
Sword is damaged or dropped and irretrievable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:53.580 --> 01:05:59.880
SCP-5514 is also equipped with an additional&nbsp;
melee weapon. Stored in the right arm of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:59.880 --> 01:06:04.620
mech is a Holdout Plasma Wristblade. This&nbsp;
superheated blade is strong enough to cut&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:04.620 --> 01:06:09.900
through almost anything. However, this blade&nbsp;
is strictly to be used as a backup weapon.

01:06:09.900 --> 01:06:17.340
Finally, should all else fail, one of SCP-5514’s&nbsp;
greatest strengths can also be used as a deadly&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:17.340 --> 01:06:21.960
weapon. The Emergency Sun Vent allows&nbsp;
a fraction of the excessive power from&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:21.960 --> 01:06:29.160
SCP-037 to be released, at the risk of causing&nbsp;
massive damage, not only to LSAs, but also any&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:29.160 --> 01:06:35.100
civilians or structures nearby. It is because of&nbsp;
the destructive risk involved, that this weapon&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:35.100 --> 01:06:42.300
is only authorized to be used as a final resort.&nbsp;
And luckily, SCP-5514 is currently undefeated.

01:06:42.900 --> 01:06:47.940
Since the arrival of multiple Large-Scale&nbsp;
Aggressors as a result of SCP-5391,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:47.940 --> 01:06:54.420
the SCP-5514 mech has managed to successfully&nbsp;
eliminate twelve of these LSA creatures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:54.420 --> 01:07:00.360
either by terminating or otherwise incapacitating&nbsp;
them. Given that its completion was fast-tracked&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:00.360 --> 01:07:06.060
through the use of anomalous elements,&nbsp;
SCP-5514’s first combat deployments also&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:06.060 --> 01:07:10.680
served as field tests of the mech’s operation&nbsp;
and its various weapons and features.

01:07:10.680 --> 01:07:18.060
Arriving in Tokyo overseen by the Foundation’s&nbsp;
own Captain Rosales and Dr. Kaori, SCP-5514’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:18.060 --> 01:07:23.100
first target was a creature designated&nbsp;
LSA-Wake-02, as well as several other&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:23.100 --> 01:07:27.600
unidentified large creatures. As the&nbsp;
LSA was about to attack Tokyo Harbor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:27.600 --> 01:07:34.080
SCP-5514 was dispatched, its arrival&nbsp;
heralded by the Thousand Word Arrows.

01:07:34.080 --> 01:07:40.080
“Champion! Champion! Exalt in the glory&nbsp;
of the Dragon Slayer!” the poets recited.

01:07:40.080 --> 01:07:45.480
Surprisingly, the poetry worked. Hearing&nbsp;
it had a noticeable effect on LSA-Wake-02,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:45.480 --> 01:07:50.820
causing the creature to back away shrieking. With&nbsp;
a single throw of the Rounded Recoiling Plasma,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:50.820 --> 01:07:58.320
SCP-5514 immediately beheaded Wake-02, damaging&nbsp;
a number of the other nearby LSAs as it retrieved&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:58.320 --> 01:08:03.300
the disk via its electromagnets. Once again,&nbsp;
the Thousand Word Arrows cheered on the mech&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:03.300 --> 01:08:08.760
and its pilot, reciting “The vicious beast’s&nbsp;
slain! Gone to those which were once bane!”

01:08:08.760 --> 01:08:15.720
After dispatching several of the minor LSAs with&nbsp;
its Cold Iron Sword, SCP-5514 became aware that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:15.720 --> 01:08:20.880
Wake-02 was not fully down for the count. A&nbsp;
second head had protruded from the mouth of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:20.880 --> 01:08:26.880
the creature’s first, issuing some sort of retreat&nbsp;
call to the remaining LSAs in Tokyo Harbor. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:26.880 --> 01:08:33.480
second head then shot towards SCP-5514, narrowly&nbsp;
missing its leg, but allowing another LSA to close&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:33.480 --> 01:08:39.780
the distance and prepare an attack. Luckily, the&nbsp;
SCP-5514 mech’s sword cleaved the beast in two.

01:08:39.780 --> 01:08:44.760
The mech began firing on the remains of&nbsp;
Wake-02 with its Beowulf-Sigurd Railgun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:44.760 --> 01:08:48.600
launching itself into the air and flying&nbsp;
towards the target while bringing its&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:48.600 --> 01:08:54.480
Cold Iron Sword down through the air.&nbsp;
With a single motion, SCP-5514 brought&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:54.480 --> 01:08:59.580
the blade all the way down the LSAs body,&nbsp;
from the creature’s head to its caudal fin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:59.580 --> 01:09:04.500
gutting the Large-Scale Aggressor&nbsp;
and splitting its entire body in two.

01:09:04.500 --> 01:09:13.020
After one final squirm, both halves were finally&nbsp;
still. SCP-5514 had passed its first field test.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:13.020 --> 01:09:18.840
The mech functioned exactly as designed, all&nbsp;
its various weapons and features working in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:18.840 --> 01:09:24.180
tandem to defeat a creature far too large and&nbsp;
powerful for any conventional force to handle.

01:09:24.180 --> 01:09:29.280
“And thus, the deed was done!&nbsp;
Exalt! Exalt! In the glory of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:29.280 --> 01:09:33.780
Dragon Slayer!” the Thousand Word Arrows&nbsp;
called out as the other LSAs retreated.

01:09:33.780 --> 01:09:39.240
One can’t help but feel cautiously optimistic&nbsp;
about our chances of survival knowing that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:39.240 --> 01:09:44.640
the Foundation has SCP-5514 as the&nbsp;
first line of defence against huge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:44.640 --> 01:09:50.880
monstrous beings that threaten humanity.&nbsp;
As the situation with SCP-5391 continues,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:50.880 --> 01:09:56.880
the SCP-5514 mech remains on the front&nbsp;
line, standing between innocent human&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:56.880 --> 01:10:01.080
beings and the looming shapes of&nbsp;
multiple Large-Scale Aggressors.

01:10:01.080 --> 01:10:06.240
With creatures that pose such a large-scale&nbsp;
threat, it certainly is lucky that disparate&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:06.240 --> 01:10:11.100
groups were able to put aside their differences&nbsp;
and work together to build a large-scale mech,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:11.100 --> 01:10:15.960
and because they did, now we have&nbsp;
The Dragon Slayer on our side.

01:10:15.960 --> 01:10:22.860
The year is 1939. It’s the dead of night in&nbsp;
Pingfang, a district of the Harbin Prefecture&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:22.860 --> 01:10:28.140
in Japanese Imperial Occupied China. A&nbsp;
squadron of over a hundred Chinese rebels&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:28.140 --> 01:10:34.140
led by Lieutenant Wang Wei, clutching Bergmann&nbsp;
MP 18 machine guns, hurry through the streets&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:34.140 --> 01:10:40.440
towards their destination: The bioweapons&nbsp;
lab operated by Unit 731 and monstrous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:40.440 --> 01:10:47.760
terrifying Japanese Surgeon General Shirō Ishii.&nbsp;
It is a mission of liberation...and revenge.

01:10:47.760 --> 01:10:53.580
If you know anything about Unit 731, just hearing&nbsp;
the name will send a chill down your spine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:53.580 --> 01:10:58.800
just as it did for the Chinese soldiers hoping&nbsp;
to perform a surprise assault on the Unit’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:58.800 --> 01:11:04.560
complex of horrors. Rumors had spread from the&nbsp;
Chinese prisoners of war taken there - and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:04.560 --> 01:11:08.760
knowledge of the horrible things happening&nbsp;
to their countrymen inside that building&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:08.760 --> 01:11:14.880
made their blood boil with white-hot fury. Their&nbsp;
mission was simple: They would launch an attack&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:14.880 --> 01:11:19.740
on the complex when the Unit least expected&nbsp;
it, save as many prisoners as they could,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:19.740 --> 01:11:24.660
while also taking revenge on as many Unit&nbsp;
Soldiers as they could get their hands on.

01:11:24.660 --> 01:11:28.800
But what the brave soldiers didn’t know&nbsp;
is that they were in for a battle they&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:28.800 --> 01:11:33.840
couldn’t hope to win. Because what they were&nbsp;
fighting was not, in the traditional sense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:33.840 --> 01:11:41.160
human. They were about to go toe to toe&nbsp;
with SCP-4007, an elite group of anomalous&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:41.160 --> 01:11:46.800
Japanese super-soldiers known as&nbsp;
the Pingfang 5. A name spoken in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:46.800 --> 01:11:51.120
fear by their enemies and victims, for&nbsp;
what little time both remained alive.

01:11:51.120 --> 01:11:56.760
The Chinese rebels were hiding in a stand of trees&nbsp;
outside the fortress, waiting for the right moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:56.760 --> 01:12:02.040
to strike, when the Pingfang 5 suddenly beat&nbsp;
them to the punch and descended upon them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:02.040 --> 01:12:07.200
The plan was thrown into chaos when the trees&nbsp;
burst into flames without so much as a hint of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:07.200 --> 01:12:13.800
artillery being fired. The soldiers, all hardened&nbsp;
men of war, began to grow scared. But then they&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:13.800 --> 01:12:20.520
saw something even more terrifying standing there&nbsp;
amongst the flames: 1st Lieutenant Mitsuo Kitano,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:20.520 --> 01:12:27.840
also known as Lightning Bolt, or SCP-4007-1 to&nbsp;
the Foundation. One of his hands was wreathed&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:27.840 --> 01:12:33.540
in blue sparks of electricity, and in the&nbsp;
other, he wielded a Type 14 Nambu pistol.

01:12:33.540 --> 01:12:38.280
Lieutenant Kitano continued his attack,&nbsp;
using his anomalous lightning to blow&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:38.280 --> 01:12:42.420
away soldier after soldier. When the&nbsp;
shocked rebels tried to return fire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:42.420 --> 01:12:45.120
they found that they couldn’t land a single shot.

01:12:45.120 --> 01:12:47.820
Kitano dodged every bullet.

01:12:47.820 --> 01:12:51.660
The second wave of the assault came&nbsp;
from behind as the rebels tried to flee&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:51.660 --> 01:12:58.260
Kitano’s wrath. Private Takashi Honda,&nbsp;
also known as The Ogre, or SCP-4007-2,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:58.260 --> 01:13:04.320
charged into the frey. He effortlessly wielded&nbsp;
a Type 11 Machine gun, and rained bullets down&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:04.320 --> 01:13:09.660
upon his unsuspecting foes. They shot back&nbsp;
at him, but the bullets seemed to just bounce&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:09.660 --> 01:13:17.100
off of his skin. He wasn’t bothered by them in the&nbsp;
slightest - He just smiled, and continued firing.

01:13:17.100 --> 01:13:22.080
The rebels quickly realized that the whole thing&nbsp;
had been a trap, but what had given them away?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:22.080 --> 01:13:27.420
As if on cue, one of their own soldiers turned&nbsp;
and began firing on his fellow soldiers with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:27.420 --> 01:13:34.980
his MP 18. This man wasn’t a rebel - in fact, he&nbsp;
wasn’t even Chinese. It was Corporal Joichiro Ida,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:34.980 --> 01:13:41.940
also known as The Fox, or SCP-4007-3. How could&nbsp;
he have possibly infiltrated the squadron?

01:13:41.940 --> 01:13:47.700
The answer is simple: He’s an anomalously&nbsp;
brilliant liar. In fact, it’s impossible&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:47.700 --> 01:13:54.540
to not believe a single thing that Corporal Ida&nbsp;
says, making him a true expert in espionage. Not a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:54.540 --> 01:13:59.340
single one of the other rebels ever had a chance&nbsp;
of sniffing him out before the doomed mission.

01:13:59.340 --> 01:14:03.660
If this betrayal surprised them, then what&nbsp;
came next must have seemed like something&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:03.660 --> 01:14:08.220
out of a nightmare. Lieutenant Wang Wei, the&nbsp;
man who’d spearheaded the entire mission,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:08.220 --> 01:14:13.440
turned and began picking off his own troops&nbsp;
with his pistol. The surviving soldiers didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:13.440 --> 01:14:18.540
understand - had the Lieutenant gone mad? They&nbsp;
tried to shoot him, but just like the others,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:18.540 --> 01:14:23.400
he dodged effortlessly and continued to&nbsp;
murder them. That’s because he wasn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:23.400 --> 01:14:27.720
Lieutenant Wang Wei at all - Wei had been&nbsp;
murdered in the forest earlier that night,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:27.720 --> 01:14:34.980
and replaced by Private Teruo Nishimura, also&nbsp;
known as SCP-4007-5, or The Shapeshifter.

01:14:34.980 --> 01:14:39.180
The Pingfang Five had infiltrated and&nbsp;
compromised the mission from before&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:39.180 --> 01:14:43.080
it had even begun. They never even stood a chance.

01:14:43.080 --> 01:14:47.100
During the chaos of the massacre, a few&nbsp;
of the rebels had managed to escape,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:47.100 --> 01:14:53.040
running through a nearby clearing into a thicket&nbsp;
of trees. If they could survive then perhaps they&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:53.040 --> 01:14:58.620
could regroup and lead a second assault on another&nbsp;
day. They had no hope of survival during a head-on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:58.620 --> 01:15:04.560
conflict with these anomalous supersoldiers,&nbsp;
they needed to get away and form a new strategy.

01:15:04.560 --> 01:15:07.020
But gunshots started ringing&nbsp;
out through the forest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:07.020 --> 01:15:12.240
tearing into their bodies and dropping the&nbsp;
men one by one. They tried to return fire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:12.240 --> 01:15:18.240
but they couldn’t even see who was shooting them.&nbsp;
It seemed to come from all directions; the rebels’&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:18.240 --> 01:15:22.500
dying thoughts were that the trees around them&nbsp;
must have been crawling with Japanese troops.

01:15:22.500 --> 01:15:29.460
The reality was even more frightening - it&nbsp;
was only one man. Private Shigeru Matsui,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:29.460 --> 01:15:34.140
codenamed Smoke for this ability to become&nbsp;
invisible at will. To the Foundation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:34.140 --> 01:15:41.220
he’s known as SCP-4007-4, the last member of&nbsp;
the Pingfang Five - the deadliest troops in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:41.220 --> 01:15:46.860
the entire Imperial Japanese Army. But unlike&nbsp;
many of the biological anomalies catalogued&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:46.860 --> 01:15:52.020
by the SCP Foundation, the Pingfang&nbsp;
Five were not born, they were made.

01:15:52.020 --> 01:15:58.680
In case you aren’t familiar with the infamous Unit&nbsp;
731 and their horrifying complex in Pingfang, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:58.680 --> 01:16:04.200
unit was a secret department of the Japanese army&nbsp;
ordered into existence by Emperor Hirohito himself&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:04.200 --> 01:16:10.440
for one sinister purpose: Researching chemical&nbsp;
and biological weapons for the Japanese Imperial&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:10.440 --> 01:16:15.660
Army. Their leader, the Surgeon General Shirō&nbsp;
Ishii, was essentially given blanket permission&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:15.660 --> 01:16:21.420
to do whatever he deemed necessary in order to&nbsp;
achieve results for his Emperor and his nation.

01:16:21.420 --> 01:16:25.380
Ishii took that directive and ran&nbsp;
with it, unleashing pseudo-scientific&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:25.380 --> 01:16:29.340
evil on a level matched only by the&nbsp;
Nazi extermination camps in Europe.

01:16:29.340 --> 01:16:34.500
Experimental weapons and horrific diseases&nbsp;
were tested on captured Chinese civilians,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:34.500 --> 01:16:39.720
political dissidents, and prisoners of war.&nbsp;
Thousands of people met horrific ends through&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:39.720 --> 01:16:45.420
execution, experimentation, and vivisection&nbsp;
- which is the dissection of a subject that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:45.420 --> 01:16:51.000
is still very much alive. How does this&nbsp;
relate to the Pingfang Five and SCP-4007?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:51.000 --> 01:16:57.300
It all comes back to a top secret project&nbsp;
overseen by Ishii himself: Project Shinka.

01:16:57.300 --> 01:17:03.960
While Unit 731 was established in 1935, in&nbsp;
1937 they began working in collaboration&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:03.960 --> 01:17:09.840
with the Imperial Japanese Anomalous Matters&nbsp;
Examination Agency - Think of it as Imperial&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:09.840 --> 01:17:14.940
Japan’s hypernationalist answer to the SCP&nbsp;
Foundation. They had become aware of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:14.940 --> 01:17:18.600
existence of anomalous individuals&nbsp;
in Imperial Controlled territories,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:18.600 --> 01:17:23.100
and General Ishii wanted to know whether&nbsp;
the powers manifested in these anomalous&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:23.100 --> 01:17:28.200
individuals could be induced in others through&nbsp;
forced organ removal and transplantation.

01:17:28.200 --> 01:17:33.180
To test this hypothesis, Ishii had the&nbsp;
Japanese Secret Police round up anomalous&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:33.180 --> 01:17:38.520
individuals in Imperial Territory en masse.&nbsp;
They were subjected to mass vivisections,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:38.520 --> 01:17:44.220
with the intention of isolating and removing&nbsp;
anomalous organs. It was an act so positively&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:44.220 --> 01:17:49.260
genocidal in proportion that East-Asia has&nbsp;
statistically fewer anomalous humans than&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:49.260 --> 01:17:54.120
they statistically should to this day. Ishii’s&nbsp;
intention was to transplant the organs into&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:54.120 --> 01:17:59.820
loyal volunteers from the Japanese military in&nbsp;
order to create an unbeatable military force.

01:17:59.820 --> 01:18:03.180
Perfect soldiers who would win the war for them.

01:18:03.180 --> 01:18:08.220
The vast majority of these twisted experiments&nbsp;
were complete failures, leading to the higher&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:08.220 --> 01:18:14.220
ups at Unit 731 almost writing Project Shinka off&nbsp;
as a waste of time and canceling the whole thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:14.220 --> 01:18:21.120
but there were soon five notable exceptions - the&nbsp;
very scary individuals you’ve already met. Not&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:21.120 --> 01:18:25.980
only did every member of the Pingfang Five end&nbsp;
up with a specific anomalous “superpower” as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:25.980 --> 01:18:31.740
result of the experiments, they also experienced&nbsp;
incredible, anomalous prowess across the board.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:31.740 --> 01:18:37.740
Members of the Five boast extended longevity,&nbsp;
meaning they rarely show their age. Enhanced&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:37.740 --> 01:18:43.860
physical abilities including faster reflexes,&nbsp;
incredible senses, and immense physical strength.

01:18:43.860 --> 01:18:48.540
They also appear to have advanced mental&nbsp;
development, displaying phenomenal tactical&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:48.540 --> 01:18:53.040
and strategic reasoning, as well as the&nbsp;
ability to effortlessly learn, read,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:53.040 --> 01:18:59.280
and speak multiple languages like Japanese,&nbsp;
English, Mandarin Chinese, Russian, and German.

01:18:59.280 --> 01:19:04.560
Of course, Japan eventually lost the war, but&nbsp;
the Pingfang Five had no intentions of ending&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:04.560 --> 01:19:09.420
their fight. They continued their missions&nbsp;
long after Japan formally surrendered,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:09.420 --> 01:19:14.760
causing chaos and violence across East-Asia.&nbsp;
After several unsuccessful engagements with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:14.760 --> 01:19:19.620
isolated members of the Pingfang Five - leading&nbsp;
to the deaths of many Foundation Agents and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:19.620 --> 01:19:26.820
civilians - The Foundation formed Mobile Task&nbsp;
Force Phi-51, aka MacArthur’s Dogs, an elite&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:26.820 --> 01:19:31.260
group of operatives trained for the specific&nbsp;
mission of bringing down the Pingfang Five.

01:19:31.260 --> 01:19:35.640
While the hunt rages on, three&nbsp;
of the five are already dead.

01:19:35.640 --> 01:19:42.180
SCP-4007-1, Mitsuo Kitano - the man who&nbsp;
can unleash bursts of electricity - met&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:42.180 --> 01:19:47.580
his end in Burma in 1948. During a&nbsp;
procedure called Operation Smokehouse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:47.580 --> 01:19:53.760
Phi-51 engaged Kitano in combat in the jungle. As&nbsp;
was one of his trademark techniques, he attempted&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:53.760 --> 01:19:58.740
to use his electricity powers to burn down the&nbsp;
jungle and escape during the chaos. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:58.740 --> 01:20:03.360
the Foundation operatives surrounded and boxed&nbsp;
him in. He couldn’t escape, and when the fires&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:03.360 --> 01:20:08.520
were put out, he was found dead on the ground -&nbsp;
the apparent cause of death was smoke inhalation.

01:20:08.520 --> 01:20:15.720
SCP-4007-2, Takashi Honda - the man with&nbsp;
the bulletproof skin - died in 1957 in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:15.720 --> 01:20:21.060
Philippines. Phi-51 engaged him in combat&nbsp;
in a mission dubbed Operation Homewrecker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:21.060 --> 01:20:26.340
which culminated in them calling down an airstrike&nbsp;
on him. In the aftermath, Honda was found dead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:26.340 --> 01:20:31.200
but his cause of death was determined as&nbsp;
having been from a powerful electric shock.

01:20:31.200 --> 01:20:37.980
And finally, SCP-4007-3, Joichiro Ida -&nbsp;
the man with the silver tongue - was found&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:37.980 --> 01:20:43.680
dead a year later in 1958. His corpse&nbsp;
was discovered in his room in Guiyang,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:43.680 --> 01:20:47.640
Southern China. He appeared to have been&nbsp;
strangled to death, and interestingly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:47.640 --> 01:20:51.720
there was evidence that he’d tried to engage&nbsp;
in a shootout with his killer, but hadn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:51.720 --> 01:20:56.460
succeeded in preventing his own murder. All of&nbsp;
these circumstances were somewhat mysterious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:56.460 --> 01:21:01.680
but the Foundation didn’t investigate them much&nbsp;
further after the bodies were tagged and bagged.

01:21:01.680 --> 01:21:10.200
SCP-4007-5, Teruo Nishimura - the shapeshifter&nbsp;
- is still on the run today, while SCP-4007-4,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:10.200 --> 01:21:15.780
Shigeru Matsui - the invisible man - is&nbsp;
doing just the opposite. He lives in Sarawak,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:15.780 --> 01:21:20.160
Malaysia, and works in open cooperation&nbsp;
with the SCP Foundation to help track&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:20.160 --> 01:21:25.380
down the fifth member of the Pingfang&nbsp;
Five. Not much has changed in the 64&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:25.380 --> 01:21:26.820
years since the death of Joichiro Ida,&nbsp;
and the search for Smoke is ongoing.

01:21:26.820 --> 01:21:33.900
It seemed like the search for SCP-4007-4 had gone&nbsp;
cold, until a Foundation archival clerk found&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:33.900 --> 01:21:40.980
something suspicious: The original paper copy&nbsp;
of the SCP-4007 document, which was dated a year&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:40.980 --> 01:21:47.880
before the Foundation had supposedly discovered&nbsp;
the existence of SCP-4007. As the archival worker&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:47.880 --> 01:21:52.500
dug further, they found a number of unsettling&nbsp;
discoveries that alter the true meaning of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:52.500 --> 01:21:58.860
SCP-4007. In the original paper documents, each&nbsp;
dead member of the Pingfang Five had organs&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:58.860 --> 01:22:03.900
missing. And each subsequent member’s death&nbsp;
involved a power possessed by a former member:&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:03.900 --> 01:22:10.560
-2 was electrocuted, and -3’s gun was useless, as&nbsp;
the killer had been wearing -2’s bulletproof skin.

01:22:10.560 --> 01:22:16.800
Who was behind this? Why? And most importantly&nbsp;
of all: How had it all been forgotten?

01:22:16.800 --> 01:22:23.160
Through more digging, the Foundation discovered&nbsp;
the truth about SCP-4007-4: His powers weren’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:23.160 --> 01:22:28.500
invisibility, they were antimemetic.&nbsp;
He can make people forget him at will,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:28.500 --> 01:22:34.020
essentially editing their memories, so he could&nbsp;
do whatever he wanted without detection...Like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:34.020 --> 01:22:38.700
for example, murdering his team members&nbsp;
and stealing their powers. They even&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:38.700 --> 01:22:43.560
managed to find an archived letter from&nbsp;
4 to 3, begging for his help in fighting&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:43.560 --> 01:22:48.300
some kind of unknown monster - something that&nbsp;
would require their combined powers to face,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:48.300 --> 01:22:53.340
whether that ended up being as five separate&nbsp;
men or one man with all of their powers.

01:22:53.340 --> 01:22:59.400
This whole time, SCP-4007-4 had been&nbsp;
playing the Foundation like a fiddle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:59.400 --> 01:23:04.140
Using their resources to help him track&nbsp;
down the final member of the Pingfang Five,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:04.140 --> 01:23:09.840
to recruit him into one final mission,&nbsp;
or murder him and steal his powers.

01:23:09.840 --> 01:23:15.960
But that still leaves one question: What&nbsp;
is this mission? What is this monster?

01:23:15.960 --> 01:23:20.220
The Foundation managed to find&nbsp;
out, at least partially. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:20.220 --> 01:23:23.940
charted the death locations of each&nbsp;
of the three dead Pingfang members,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:23.940 --> 01:23:29.820
and the living location of SCP-4007-4,&nbsp;
and discovered something amazing…

01:23:29.820 --> 01:23:36.060
Four of the five points on a perfect pentagon,&nbsp;
centering on the South China Sea. The Foundation&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:36.060 --> 01:23:42.240
also estimated that the pentagon would reveal&nbsp;
the location of SCP-4007-5 at the final point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:42.240 --> 01:23:46.200
but his location isn’t nearly as&nbsp;
interesting as what resides at&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:46.200 --> 01:23:48.960
the center of this massive geographical pentagon.

01:23:48.960 --> 01:23:54.540
Foundation divers discovered huge numbers of&nbsp;
sunken Japanese battleships, downed planes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:54.540 --> 01:23:59.760
and thousands of bones littering the seabed.&nbsp;
Based on the damage to these vessels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:59.760 --> 01:24:04.980
it was clear they hadn’t been shot or&nbsp;
blown up. No, they had been torn apart&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:04.980 --> 01:24:10.380
by something obscenely huge and powerful&nbsp;
underneath the water. The pentagon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:10.380 --> 01:24:15.900
a significant shape in sorcery, is likely&nbsp;
a massive, supernatural containment ritual,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:15.900 --> 01:24:21.300
keeping whatever unspeakable horror is&nbsp;
lurking under the ground there from escaping.

01:24:21.300 --> 01:24:24.660
A containment ritual that&nbsp;
could only be maintained by&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:24.660 --> 01:24:28.800
five special individuals in five special places…

01:24:28.800 --> 01:24:33.900
It was in this moment that the Foundation&nbsp;
came upon a truly horrifying revelation:&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:33.900 --> 01:24:39.300
They had misjudged the intentions of Project&nbsp;
Shinka. It was never about creating assets for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:39.300 --> 01:24:44.640
the war against the Allies, it was about a war&nbsp;
against something else entirely, something much&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:44.640 --> 01:24:50.760
more dangerous than and deadly than the squabbles&nbsp;
between men. It was about the entity that lurked&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:50.760 --> 01:24:56.460
below. A creature that nobody understood,&nbsp;
and that no conventional weapon can fight.

01:24:56.460 --> 01:25:01.200
And if the ritual is ever broken&nbsp;
and the beast is allowed to rise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:01.200 --> 01:25:05.760
the Pingfang Five will be the&nbsp;
very least of our problems.

01:25:05.760 --> 01:25:11.520
I used to complain about work all the time.&nbsp;
The long hours, the disrespect from my boss,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:11.520 --> 01:25:17.760
getting home exhausted, aching, and sunburned. I&nbsp;
worked in construction a lifetime ago. I’d give&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:17.760 --> 01:25:22.620
anything to go back to that now. What’s that they&nbsp;
always say? You don’t know what you’ve got until&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:22.620 --> 01:25:28.800
it’s gone? Maybe I deserve the hand I’ve been&nbsp;
dealt. I haven’t been a good person. I don’t play&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:28.800 --> 01:25:34.440
well with others. There was an argument with my&nbsp;
foreman, one that got a little too heated, and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:34.440 --> 01:25:38.100
just couldn’t reign in my temper.
The hammer I’d been using all day&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:38.100 --> 01:25:43.140
was still in my hand…but I digress.
I used to build things with my hands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:43.140 --> 01:25:48.720
but I’m in a different line of work now. If you&nbsp;
can call it work. That would imply I get paid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:48.720 --> 01:25:54.360
I clock in every morning, and I get to go&nbsp;
home afterward. I don’t. I’m a prisoner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:54.360 --> 01:26:00.660
really. A glorified lab rat, and property of&nbsp;
the all-powerful SCP Foundation. I’ve been&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:00.660 --> 01:26:06.360
here about three weeks, and everyone always says&nbsp;
D-Classes like us last about a month here. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:06.360 --> 01:26:11.280
the clock is ticking down to zero for me. I&nbsp;
haven’t had to do anything too dangerous yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:11.280 --> 01:26:17.040
but like I said. It’s just a matter of time.
I’m sitting in my cell, the same windowless box&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:17.040 --> 01:26:21.600
I’ve spent every day for the past three weeks,&nbsp;
when a guard bursts in and grabs me by the arm.&nbsp;

01:26:21.600 --> 01:26:27.480
“What’s going on?” I ask, but he ignores me.
Of course, why make conversation with a lab rat?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:27.480 --> 01:26:31.740
Better not to think of us as human at all.
He yanks me down the hall, opens a door,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:31.740 --> 01:26:36.300
and shoves me inside. There are several other&nbsp;
D-Class in there already, and they’re staring&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:36.300 --> 01:26:40.560
at something in the corner that I can’t&nbsp;
quite make out. I start to turn around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:40.560 --> 01:26:46.020
to take in the rest of the room, but a voice&nbsp;
comes over the loudspeaker, cold and clinical.&nbsp;

01:26:46.020 --> 01:26:50.280
“Look at the statue in the corner.&nbsp;
Do not take your eyes off it.”&nbsp;

01:26:50.280 --> 01:26:54.660
Well, when a disembodied voice at the SCP&nbsp;
Foundation tells you to look at something,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:54.660 --> 01:27:00.360
you’d better do it. So, I look.
It's a sculpture, made from concrete and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:00.360 --> 01:27:04.920
rebar. It looks pretty harmless, but I’ve heard&nbsp;
enough screams through the walls to know that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:04.920 --> 01:27:09.000
don’t keep too many harmless things caged&nbsp;
up here. Whatever this thing really is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:09.000 --> 01:27:14.280
I don’t want to know what it’ll do to me when&nbsp;
my back is turned. I stare at it, unblinking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:14.280 --> 01:27:18.720
feeling my eyes burn and tear up from the&nbsp;
dry air and concentration. I’m scared to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:18.720 --> 01:27:22.740
close my eyes for even a second, determined&nbsp;
not to be the first one in here to break.&nbsp;

01:27:22.740 --> 01:27:29.640
But something out there has other plans for&nbsp;
me, for us. I hear a warbling sound like a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:29.640 --> 01:27:35.280
motor struggling to start, the sudden crash of&nbsp;
thunder outside, and then…the room goes black.&nbsp;

01:27:35.280 --> 01:27:39.000
For a second I think I’ve gone blind, but&nbsp;
then I hear the other guys screaming, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:39.000 --> 01:27:43.860
yelling of scientists behind the wall. Somehow,&nbsp;
the power’s gone out. I can’t see anything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:43.860 --> 01:27:48.540
but I can hear the sound of stone scraping&nbsp;
against itself, the snap of breaking bones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:48.540 --> 01:27:54.000
the thud of a limp body slumping to the floor.
The room comes into focus again, as a backup&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:54.000 --> 01:27:58.680
generator kicks in and fills the space with weak&nbsp;
fluorescent light. But something’s different,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:58.680 --> 01:28:03.900
something’s wrong. The statue in the corner&nbsp;
is gone. The other two men are on the floor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:03.900 --> 01:28:07.740
heads twisted around backwards.&nbsp;
And the door is standing wide open.&nbsp;

01:28:07.740 --> 01:28:12.840
Is this a trap? A trick of some kind? If I&nbsp;
run out that door, will a guard be waiting&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:12.840 --> 01:28:17.520
there for me? I don’t know, but whatever might&nbsp;
happen to me if I try can’t be any worse than&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:17.520 --> 01:28:21.000
what will happen if I stay right here.
Through the wall, I hear that sickening&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:21.000 --> 01:28:27.000
snapping sound again, a corpse thudding to the&nbsp;
ground. They didn’t plan this. Something in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:27.000 --> 01:28:32.400
their experiment went horribly wrong, for them.&nbsp;
And maybe, just maybe, it went right for me.

01:28:32.400 --> 01:28:37.080
I don’t give it a second thought, I’m out the&nbsp;
door, running through the dimly lit halls,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:37.080 --> 01:28:40.620
through the maze of identical corridors&nbsp;
in search of some kind of exit.&nbsp;

01:28:40.620 --> 01:28:46.380
All around me, there’s chaos. Guards firing&nbsp;
their weapons at inhuman shapes that I catch&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:46.380 --> 01:28:51.000
a brief glimpse of as I run past, scientists&nbsp;
yelling for help, people going as fast as they&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:51.000 --> 01:28:55.200
can toward the danger and away from it.
I’m so caught up in it all, I don’t even&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:55.200 --> 01:28:59.220
notice when I run right into a woman in&nbsp;
a white lab coat, knocking us both to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:59.220 --> 01:29:03.720
ground. I scramble to my feet, and look at her&nbsp;
with wide eyes, waiting for her to turn me in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:03.720 --> 01:29:08.340
to call the guards. But she doesn’t.
“I’m so sorry,” I stammer.&nbsp;

01:29:08.340 --> 01:29:13.560
She holds out a hand, gesturing for me to help&nbsp;
her to her feet. So, I do. “Watch your step,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:13.560 --> 01:29:18.000
there,” she says, with a knowing smile. I&nbsp;
can’t help but notice how beautiful she is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:18.000 --> 01:29:24.240
twinkling brown eyes and thick black hair.&nbsp;
Then, I spot them, tucked under her hair but&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:24.240 --> 01:29:29.880
unmistakable. She has a pair of fox ears on top&nbsp;
of her head. It hits me in rapid succession then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:29.880 --> 01:29:35.580
all the things about this woman that don’t look&nbsp;
quite right. Her unusually long nails, too sharp&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:35.580 --> 01:29:40.620
and pointed for lab work. The feral glint in&nbsp;
her eyes. The halting cadence of her voice.&nbsp;

01:29:40.620 --> 01:29:45.060
This woman is not an employee of the&nbsp;
SCP Foundation. She’s doing the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:45.060 --> 01:29:48.000
thing I am: taking advantage of&nbsp;
the situation to try and escape.&nbsp;

01:29:48.000 --> 01:29:53.280
"I'll be more careful from now on,&nbsp;
ma’am,” I say. She winks and turns&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:53.280 --> 01:29:56.820
down the hall. I can see a tail poking&nbsp;
out beneath the bottom of her lab coat.&nbsp;

01:29:56.820 --> 01:30:03.360
“Safe travels!” I call after her. She laughs, a&nbsp;
dangerous sound, like she’s not the one who needs&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:03.360 --> 01:30:07.740
to worry about staying safe, and I wonder what&nbsp;
might have happened if I had caught her in a worse&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:07.740 --> 01:30:11.640
mood. Good thing my mom taught me my manners.
Now, which way should I go from here?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:11.640 --> 01:30:14.820
Should I follow the woman? I get the&nbsp;
feeling she doesn’t want any company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:14.820 --> 01:30:18.960
and I’d prefer to stay on her good side.
As I’m thinking it through, I hear a small&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:18.960 --> 01:30:23.400
popping sound behind me, like a vacuum&nbsp;
in the air being filled all of a sudden.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:23.400 --> 01:30:28.800
I spin around, and there’s a guy standing there,&nbsp;
ordinary as you please. Blonde hair, green eyes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:28.800 --> 01:30:34.260
wearing jeans, and a t-shirt that says “Mothman&nbsp;
Fan Club” in red letters. This guy looks pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:34.260 --> 01:30:38.880
out of place, more the type that you’d see&nbsp;
browsing a comic book store, but after my prior&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:38.880 --> 01:30:45.480
strange encounter I know not to assume anything.
“Hey man, what’s going on?” He asks me. I stare&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:45.480 --> 01:30:50.400
back, not sure what to say. “What did I miss?”&nbsp;
He continues. “I feel like I was gone for ages.”&nbsp;

01:30:51.480 --> 01:30:55.140
“Um,” I gesture to our collective&nbsp;
surroundings, the sound of alarms&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:55.140 --> 01:30:59.220
blaring and voices crying out for backup, the&nbsp;
inhuman shrieks of creatures being forced back&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:59.220 --> 01:31:05.820
into their cells. “There’s a lot going on.”
“Huh, yeah. Seems like it,” the guy sighs.&nbsp;

01:31:05.820 --> 01:31:09.960
“You wouldn’t happen to know how to get&nbsp;
out of here, would you?” I ask, hopefully.&nbsp;

01:31:09.960 --> 01:31:13.320
He shakes his head.
“Sorry, I don’t really come&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:13.320 --> 01:31:17.220
and go through the door here. Guess I’ll&nbsp;
just go back to my room until things calm&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:17.220 --> 01:31:20.520
down a bit. Do you want anything? I’ve&nbsp;
got a mini-fridge in there,” he offers.&nbsp;

01:31:20.520 --> 01:31:23.520
“…No thanks.”
“Suit yourself, man!”&nbsp;

01:31:23.520 --> 01:31:28.920
He shoves his hands in his pockets, and walks&nbsp;
away, whistling a tune to himself. If he’s this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:28.920 --> 01:31:33.180
unphased by teleporting into the middle&nbsp;
of a complete madhouse, I don’t even want&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:33.180 --> 01:31:37.200
to know what kind of stuff he’s seen. Nice&nbsp;
enough kid, though. Probably better company&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:37.200 --> 01:31:41.580
than anyone, or anything, else in this place.
If I’m not going to take him up on his offer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:41.580 --> 01:31:45.240
though, I should keep moving before someone&nbsp;
notices I’m not where I’m supposed to be.&nbsp;

01:31:45.240 --> 01:31:49.020
I round a corner just in time to see&nbsp;
another body drop. I whirl around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:49.020 --> 01:31:54.840
looking for that horrible statue, but I don’t&nbsp;
see it. Instead, I see what looks like a man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:54.840 --> 01:32:00.480
dressed in all black, wearing a mask in the shape&nbsp;
of a bird’s face with a long, pointed beak. He’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:00.480 --> 01:32:04.620
carrying an old-school doctor’s bag, and he’s&nbsp;
bending down to inspect the body of the guard&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:04.620 --> 01:32:09.360
that just keeled over. I should keep running,&nbsp;
I should do something, but I can’t look away.&nbsp;

01:32:09.360 --> 01:32:14.700
The man in black pulls a syringe full of thick&nbsp;
green liquid from his bag and injects it into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:14.700 --> 01:32:19.380
corpse. To my horror, the body begins to move,&nbsp;
thrashing around, hands opening and closing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:19.380 --> 01:32:26.100
grasping for something. It sits up, reanimated,&nbsp;
but…wrong. Its eyes are bulging and vacant like&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:26.100 --> 01:32:32.100
a goldfish, its mouth hangs slack. As the zombie&nbsp;
climbs to its feet, the figure in all-black turns&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:32.100 --> 01:32:37.140
to look at me, eyes shining from beneath his mask.
“Hello, my good fellow! How are you feeling&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:37.140 --> 01:32:40.260
today?” He calls to me, his&nbsp;
voice polished and polite.&nbsp;

01:32:40.260 --> 01:32:44.100
“I’m fine!” I shout, my hands&nbsp;
shaking, my chest tight.&nbsp;

01:32:44.100 --> 01:32:46.980
The doctor tilts his head&nbsp;
to the side thoughtfully.&nbsp;

01:32:46.980 --> 01:32:52.620
“You don’t look well, my friend. Perhaps you could&nbsp;
allow me to examine you?” The zombie shuffles&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:52.620 --> 01:32:57.120
toward me, and the doctor opens his bag. “There’s&nbsp;
a pestilence raging through this land, you see.”&nbsp;

01:32:57.120 --> 01:33:00.300
I’m not going to stick around to find&nbsp;
out what that exam might look like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:00.300 --> 01:33:04.320
or what he might do in the service of curing me.
“No thanks, I don’t have health insurance!”&nbsp;

01:33:04.320 --> 01:33:10.020
That is all I can think to say as I turn and run&nbsp;
back the way I came. I still have no idea where&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:10.020 --> 01:33:14.880
an exit might be, how I might get out of here. It&nbsp;
all looks the same, one long stretch of tile and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:14.880 --> 01:33:18.780
white. I guess a place like the Foundation&nbsp;
isn’t going to have red neon signs telling&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:18.780 --> 01:33:24.000
you how to escape. But this place can’t go on&nbsp;
forever…can it? I’m starting to have my doubts.&nbsp;

01:33:24.000 --> 01:33:29.040
Before I can get too lost in my own hopelessness,&nbsp;
the biggest man I’ve ever seen comes lumbering&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:29.040 --> 01:33:32.280
down the hall toward me. I worked with&nbsp;
some pretty big guys in construction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:32.280 --> 01:33:37.860
massive behemoths who would carry steel beams like&nbsp;
they were pool noodles, but this man is something&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:37.860 --> 01:33:43.080
else entirely. He’s gotta be…eight feet tall?&nbsp;
With fists that look like they could crush my&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:43.080 --> 01:33:46.800
skull in one hit. I’d better get out of this&nbsp;
monster’s way if I know what’s good for me.&nbsp;

01:33:46.800 --> 01:33:53.400
But as he passes me, he waves and gives me a&nbsp;
great big friendly - if unnaturally wide - smile.

01:33:53.400 --> 01:34:00.840
“Beautiful day, isn’t it?” he bellows in&nbsp;
a booming, French-accented voice. With his&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:00.840 --> 01:34:05.640
other hand, he picks at his teeth with something&nbsp;
white. A closer look reveals it to be a shard&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:05.640 --> 01:34:09.900
of bone. I have to imagine it’s human.&nbsp;
His lips are stained dark with blood.&nbsp;

01:34:09.900 --> 01:34:17.460
“You look lost, can I help you with something?”
A helpful people-eater. What a guy. I keep my&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:17.460 --> 01:34:20.700
distance but decide to try my luck.
“You wouldn’t happen to know&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:20.700 --> 01:34:24.300
where the exit is?” I say.
He beams at me, thrilled by my question.&nbsp;

01:34:24.300 --> 01:34:29.700
“I’m so glad you asked! It’s that way!” He&nbsp;
points down a hall to the left. “Just follow&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:29.700 --> 01:34:33.840
that hall all the way down, and take the only&nbsp;
right! That should get you where you’re going!”&nbsp;

01:34:33.840 --> 01:34:37.260
“Thanks!”
I give him a wave back as I head&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:37.260 --> 01:34:42.720
that way. As I go, I can hear the giant singing&nbsp;
opera to himself, something Italian. It reminds&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:42.720 --> 01:34:47.280
me of a record my grandma used to play, a&nbsp;
long time ago. Maybe this will get me free,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:47.280 --> 01:34:52.020
maybe it won’t, but it’s more information than I&nbsp;
had a second ago. Might as well give it a shot.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:52.020 --> 01:34:56.280
If I make it through this, I’m going to be in the&nbsp;
best shape of my life from all the running I’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:56.280 --> 01:35:00.240
done today. Maybe I’ll try to do a marathon.&nbsp;
Or maybe I shouldn’t get ahead of myself.&nbsp;

01:35:00.240 --> 01:35:05.580
There’s no one else down this particular hall,&nbsp;
at least no one that I can see. I slow my pace&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:05.580 --> 01:35:10.440
to a steady walk, keeping my eyes and ears&nbsp;
alert for any potential threats. So far,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:10.440 --> 01:35:16.140
so good. I reach the other side of the hall, and&nbsp;
take a right turn…and nothing. Just a wall. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:16.140 --> 01:35:20.160
giant son of a gun lied to me! I guess that’s&nbsp;
what I get for asking a cannibal for directions.

01:35:20.160 --> 01:35:25.140
I guess I’m lucky he just misled me instead of&nbsp;
having me for dessert. I’m about to plan my next&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:25.140 --> 01:35:30.780
move, when I feel something nudge against the&nbsp;
back of my leg. I turn, and there’s what I can&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:30.780 --> 01:35:35.580
only describe as a blob. Just a large mass&nbsp;
of orange slime, wrapping around my legs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:35.580 --> 01:35:40.980
making a friendly high-pitched gurgle. I should&nbsp;
be suspicious of it, after everything I’ve seen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:40.980 --> 01:35:45.660
but I’m not. I just get the feeling this&nbsp;
thing has my best interests at heart.&nbsp;

01:35:45.660 --> 01:35:51.240
“Hey, buddy…” I pat its slimy surface,&nbsp;
and it ripples delightedly, making another&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:51.240 --> 01:35:56.040
gurgling sound. It’s kind of cute, honestly.&nbsp;
I smell the scent of freshly brewed coffee,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:56.040 --> 01:36:00.960
one of my favorite smells in the world.&nbsp;
I haven’t had a decent cup in years.&nbsp;

01:36:00.960 --> 01:36:07.560
All of a sudden, I feel this overwhelming sense&nbsp;
of happiness, of hope. Even if it’s foolish to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:07.560 --> 01:36:11.520
think so, I honestly believe that everything&nbsp;
is going to be alright. This strange little&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:11.520 --> 01:36:16.620
friendly slime has given me the strength to carry&nbsp;
on. I wish I had a plushie version of this thing.&nbsp;

01:36:16.620 --> 01:36:21.360
There’s a sudden tickling sensation along my&nbsp;
legs, and I laugh, pushing the slime away gently.&nbsp;

01:36:21.900 --> 01:36:25.260
“Hey, stop that!”
It coos apologetically&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:25.260 --> 01:36:30.060
and bumps itself against me again like a stray dog&nbsp;
asking for scratches behind the ears. I wonder for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:30.060 --> 01:36:34.560
a second if I could take this thing with me, but&nbsp;
what would I even feed it? What kind of life could&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:34.560 --> 01:36:38.820
I give a domesticated slime creature? Better&nbsp;
to leave it with people who know how to care&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:38.820 --> 01:36:43.200
for it, even if they’re mad scientists.
I give it another pat, then head on my&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:43.200 --> 01:36:47.220
way. The slime oozes off in the opposite&nbsp;
direction, off to find more new friends,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:47.220 --> 01:36:52.380
I imagine. I had no idea there was anything nice&nbsp;
here, with everything horrible that I’ve seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:52.380 --> 01:36:57.060
today. Warms my cold heart, just a little bit.
It's strange, I’ve been walking for a while,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:57.060 --> 01:37:02.640
and I haven’t seen anyone else. It’s eerily&nbsp;
quiet over here, I can’t hear a thing except&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:02.640 --> 01:37:07.020
for that alarm in the distance. Not sure when all&nbsp;
the screaming went quiet, or where all the guards&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:07.020 --> 01:37:12.360
went. It’s peaceful, but I have to admit it’s&nbsp;
eerie. There’s no way it’s this easy. There’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:12.360 --> 01:37:16.620
no way I’ll be able to just walk out of here.&nbsp;
Life doesn’t work like that, especially not here.&nbsp;

01:37:16.620 --> 01:37:21.420
My eyes dart around, watching for any potential&nbsp;
danger as I go. Maybe all the action is on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:21.420 --> 01:37:25.680
other side of the facility, maybe something broke&nbsp;
out of containment that’s so big, so dangerous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:25.680 --> 01:37:29.880
that all hands are on deck to contain it.&nbsp;
Or maybe they’re all dead, and I’m next.&nbsp;

01:37:31.140 --> 01:37:35.100
I shouldn’t think like that. I feel the&nbsp;
hairs on the back of my neck prickle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:35.100 --> 01:37:41.460
and I have that paranoid feeling of being watched.&nbsp;
Like eyes, burning into me. I glance behind me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:41.460 --> 01:37:47.040
but there’s no one there. I stop walking, but&nbsp;
I don’t hear any other footsteps. Calm down,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:47.040 --> 01:37:50.640
there’s no one here with you, I&nbsp;
tell myself. Just keep moving.

01:37:50.640 --> 01:37:54.960
I turn my eyes back to the path ahead of me,&nbsp;
and I freeze. What was that? I saw something,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:54.960 --> 01:38:01.800
just for a second. Something long, thin, spindly,&nbsp;
like a stick bug but much, much bigger. When I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:01.800 --> 01:38:07.440
look for it, it’s gone. But I know that I saw it.&nbsp;
Or this place has now driven me insane. I glance&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:07.440 --> 01:38:11.280
to the side, and there it is again! For a split&nbsp;
second, so fast I could almost convince myself&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:11.280 --> 01:38:15.900
I imagined it. If I were at home, or walking&nbsp;
down an ordinary street, I might think I had.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:15.900 --> 01:38:20.820
But I’m not anywhere ordinary. And a slender&nbsp;
monster that can hide in my peripheral vision,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:20.820 --> 01:38:24.420
only be spotted for a millisecond at a time?
That seems par for the course&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:24.420 --> 01:38:27.840
in a place like this.
There! I just saw it again! It’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:27.840 --> 01:38:33.420
like it wants me to know it’s here, but that I’ll&nbsp;
never be able to pin it down. I’m not playing mind&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:33.420 --> 01:38:37.980
games with a monster, forget it. I’ll keep moving.
Another flash of the creature, closer than&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:37.980 --> 01:38:42.480
it was before. “Whatever, you don’t&nbsp;
scare me.” I say out loud. I’m lying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:42.480 --> 01:38:47.040
but I hope it can’t tell. If I just play it cool,&nbsp;
maybe this thing will get bored and find someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:47.040 --> 01:38:51.300
else to menace. I haven’t been running for a&nbsp;
long time now, but my heart is still pounding.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:51.300 --> 01:38:55.680
I can taste metal in the back of my throat.
Suddenly, all the sounds from before are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:55.680 --> 01:39:01.320
back. The gunfire, the screaming, the shriek of&nbsp;
alarms. I’ve made my way back to the action. One&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:01.320 --> 01:39:07.860
sound drowns out all the rest: a deafening&nbsp;
roar, animalistic and powerful and…hateful.

01:39:07.860 --> 01:39:13.020
It reminds me of a dinosaur in a movie, but with&nbsp;
a power that makes my bones rattle and my teeth&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:13.020 --> 01:39:18.780
chatter. The roar is followed by more screaming,&nbsp;
and a massive crash, the crunch of wood and stone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:18.780 --> 01:39:24.480
There’s another roar, more distant than before.&nbsp;
Wait…whatever made that sound, it just broke out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:24.480 --> 01:39:29.520
And I bet it left a pretty big hole in its wake.
I follow the sound and find exactly what I hoped&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:29.520 --> 01:39:35.700
I would: A massive opening in the wall, and&nbsp;
the sweet feeling of a breeze coming through.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:35.700 --> 01:39:40.320
I couldn’t find an exit, but this thing just&nbsp;
made one for me. I don’t waste any more time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:40.320 --> 01:39:45.300
I make a break for it, dancing around rubble and&nbsp;
pools of corrosive acid, and sprinting into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:45.300 --> 01:39:49.800
nearby forest. I’m ducking under branches,&nbsp;
darting around trees, and I run until I’m&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:49.800 --> 01:39:54.840
seeing spots and my lungs are gasping for air.
I don’t know how far I’ll make it, if there’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:54.840 --> 01:39:59.640
a town nearby or any kind of shelter.&nbsp;
But I promise one thing, if I survive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:59.640 --> 01:40:04.680
I’m going to be a better person. I’ll get it right&nbsp;
this time. I’ll earn my second chance at life.

01:40:04.680 --> 01:40:10.560
SCP-682 had escaped containment, and&nbsp;
it was all hands on deck at the SCP&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:10.560 --> 01:40:13.260
Foundation to try and stop the creature's rampage.

01:40:13.260 --> 01:40:18.600
All unarmed personnel were running to escape&nbsp;
before 682 had a chance to rip them apart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:18.600 --> 01:40:23.460
as the security team bravely fought to&nbsp;
incapacitate the hard-to-destroy reptile&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:23.460 --> 01:40:28.560
long enough to return him to his acid&nbsp;
tank. 682 was on an all-out offensive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:28.560 --> 01:40:34.320
stomping through the SCP's item gallery and&nbsp;
spewing acid at anyone who came within range.

01:40:34.320 --> 01:40:40.740
People, items, and parts of the room alike were&nbsp;
melting and sizzling as 682 attacked. It was an&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:40.740 --> 01:40:45.900
absolute bloodbath, and you'd have to have been&nbsp;
insane to try and fight back against it. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:45.900 --> 01:40:52.860
one being remained unfazed by the chaos, and stood&nbsp;
proud in the face of the omnicidal monster. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:52.860 --> 01:40:57.767
was another SCP, one who had been broken free&nbsp;
from its glass display case in the commotion.&nbsp;

01:40:57.767 --> 01:41:00.724
“Who dares to disturb the Prime&nbsp;
Minister Sinister?” It said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:00.723 --> 01:41:05.788
in a high tinny voice. “I shall rip your eyes&nbsp;
from their sockets and force you to eat them!”

01:41:05.789 --> 01:41:11.700
SCP 682 stopped to see who had threatened him. It&nbsp;
was almost laughable that this thing had dared to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:11.700 --> 01:41:16.560
make such a violent threat, because there was no&nbsp;
way that this little thing would be able to stand&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:16.560 --> 01:41:22.680
up to the unkillable 682. But its size didn't&nbsp;
stop it from trying to pick a fight all the same.

01:41:22.680 --> 01:41:27.900
The SCP was a tiny robot made from junk,&nbsp;
which looked more like a sculpture made&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:27.900 --> 01:41:32.100
from scrap that was found on the side of&nbsp;
the road than a functional automaton. Its&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:32.100 --> 01:41:36.480
head was made of an upside down voltmeter,&nbsp;
giving it the appearance of a smiling face,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:36.480 --> 01:41:40.620
and its arms were made of wrenches.&nbsp;
It was capable of walking around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:40.620 --> 01:41:45.240
though it seemed to have difficulty&nbsp;
moving, as it was very top heavy.

01:41:45.240 --> 01:41:49.320
It shook its rusty fist at SCP&nbsp;
682 and continued to threaten him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:49.320 --> 01:41:54.120
shouting - "You do not know the fury you have&nbsp;
unleashed! RoboLord the Destructor will end you!"

01:41:54.120 --> 01:42:00.780
SCP 682 swiped at the robot, annoyed by its&nbsp;
continued attempts to antagonize him. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:00.780 --> 01:42:04.200
robot toppled over and struggled&nbsp;
to stand again, but when it did,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:04.200 --> 01:42:11.220
it ran for 682's feet. It started to grab at 682's&nbsp;
toes, hitting them with its wrench hands. As you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:11.220 --> 01:42:20.760
might expect, this had almost no effect on 682.
"You do realize…you are…weak…pathetic" said 682,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:20.760 --> 01:42:24.180
raising his claw to get a better&nbsp;
look at the annoying little robot.

01:42:24.180 --> 01:42:28.200
It responded- "Lies and slander! The&nbsp;
Mayor of Mayhem is the most powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:28.200 --> 01:42:33.720
being in existence!" before promptly losing its&nbsp;
grip on 682's claw and falling to the floor.

01:42:33.720 --> 01:42:40.260
Now 682 was really getting tired of this thing.&nbsp;
He prepared to swipe at it again, this time using&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:40.260 --> 01:42:45.420
his full strength, which would certainly smash&nbsp;
the robot to pieces. But, luckily for the robot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:45.420 --> 01:42:51.540
its pestering had distracted 682 just long enough&nbsp;
for Foundation security to sneak up on him.

01:42:51.540 --> 01:42:57.360
Before 682 had a chance to smash the robot, the&nbsp;
Foundation fired on him with a volley of rockets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:57.360 --> 01:43:02.700
reducing him to a misshapen lump. The&nbsp;
security team collected up 682's remains,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:02.700 --> 01:43:08.340
ready to put them into a backup acid tank, but the&nbsp;
task was made slightly more difficult by the fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:08.340 --> 01:43:13.620
that the robot was running around underfoot,&nbsp;
trying in vain to now attack them instead.

01:43:13.620 --> 01:43:19.680
What was this strange little robot that thought&nbsp;
it could attack the unkillable lizard, 682? Meet&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:19.680 --> 01:43:27.600
SCP-1370, appropriately nicknamed “The Pesterbot”&nbsp;
by Foundation personnel. Pesterbot is a small&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:27.600 --> 01:43:33.000
robot made of junk that displays sentience and the&nbsp;
ability to move despite having no power source.

01:43:33.000 --> 01:43:38.220
Everything about SCP-1370 defies&nbsp;
common sense. The voltmeter that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:38.220 --> 01:43:43.620
serves as its head contains no sensors, but&nbsp;
it seems unable to see if the voltmeter is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:43.620 --> 01:43:47.700
covered. Its arms and legs are made of&nbsp;
wrenches and its wooden torso contains&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:47.700 --> 01:43:50.880
a speaker which it communicates&nbsp;
through in a tinny, monotone voice.

01:43:50.880 --> 01:43:59.580
Pesterbot lives in SCP Gallery 27, in a glass&nbsp;
display case which is 125 cm tall, 50 cm wide,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:59.580 --> 01:44:06.300
and 75 cm long. Level 2 personnel and higher are&nbsp;
allowed to remove the robot from containment at&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:06.300 --> 01:44:10.740
their discretion, but penalties will be incurred&nbsp;
for anyone who doesn't return to its case.

01:44:10.740 --> 01:44:16.500
Due to its impractical, top-heavy design,&nbsp;
Pesterbot falls over frequently when it walks&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:16.500 --> 01:44:21.420
around. This led the foundation to believe that&nbsp;
he was created as an art project and was later&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:21.420 --> 01:44:27.300
somehow imbued with anomalous properties, rather&nbsp;
than being made with the intent of being sentient.

01:44:27.300 --> 01:44:31.980
Pesterbot has demonstrated a high capacity&nbsp;
for learning, having been taught how to speak&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:31.980 --> 01:44:36.420
in English, French, and Latin. However,&nbsp;
the major factor hampering attempts to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:36.420 --> 01:44:40.800
further test its intelligence has nothing&nbsp;
to do with its robotic processing power.

01:44:40.800 --> 01:44:43.860
No, it’s because of its poor attitude.

01:44:43.860 --> 01:44:49.560
Pesterbot's encounter with SCP-682 and the&nbsp;
cockiness it expressed in the moment wasn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:49.560 --> 01:44:54.960
an aberration, this SCP really believes&nbsp;
it's a killing machine, and as a result,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:54.960 --> 01:45:01.800
it will pick a fight with anyone and anything that&nbsp;
moves. This even includes its own reflection - and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:01.800 --> 01:45:06.420
as a result the glass of its container must&nbsp;
be made as non-reflective as possible to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:06.420 --> 01:45:11.820
prevent the robot from damaging the case or&nbsp;
itself in attempts to fight its mirror self.

01:45:11.820 --> 01:45:18.180
Though its most commonly used nickname in the&nbsp;
Foundation is Pesterbot, SCP-1370 also goes by&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:18.180 --> 01:45:23.700
a variety of self-given epithets, including&nbsp;
Doom Bot 2000, Robolord the Destructor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:23.700 --> 01:45:28.320
and Darth Claw Killflex. It can be&nbsp;
made to add new names to this list&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:28.320 --> 01:45:33.120
through encouragement by staff, and over time&nbsp;
they have managed to add the name Pesterbot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:33.120 --> 01:45:38.460
as well as the even more ridiculous “Patheticon&nbsp;
the Garglemost”, to its lexicon of names.

01:45:38.460 --> 01:45:42.900
As you may have surmised, Pesterbot&nbsp;
is classified as Safe and is treated&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:42.900 --> 01:45:46.980
by most SCP foundation employees&nbsp;
as a humorous oddity rather than a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:46.980 --> 01:45:51.480
legitimate threat. Many tests have been done&nbsp;
on Pesterbot, and they've all conclusively&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:51.480 --> 01:45:56.220
determined that the robot is incapable of&nbsp;
inflicting any damage to its opponents.

01:45:56.220 --> 01:45:59.160
In fact, Pesterbot is more&nbsp;
of a danger to itself than&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:59.160 --> 01:46:02.940
to anything around it. Both because&nbsp;
it's incredibly clumsy and awkward,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:02.940 --> 01:46:08.520
and because it frequently picks fights with&nbsp;
other SCPs who are far more powerful than it.

01:46:08.520 --> 01:46:13.680
One such scenario that could've gone far worse&nbsp;
for Pesterbot than it did was its encounter&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:13.680 --> 01:46:20.820
with SCP-846, also known as Robo-Dude, one of&nbsp;
the many Dr. Wondertainment products currently&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:20.820 --> 01:46:26.640
kept in Foundation containment. Robo-Dude is an&nbsp;
ordinary-looking toy robot that, upon request,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:26.640 --> 01:46:32.220
can produce up to 350 “robo-accessories”&nbsp;
that function as real weapons.

01:46:32.220 --> 01:46:37.140
Robo-Dude can deploy everything from a&nbsp;
rocket launcher to a flamethrower to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:37.140 --> 01:46:41.940
gun that shoots out an unknown species of&nbsp;
insect that can chew through wood. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:41.940 --> 01:46:46.500
Robo-Dude seems to not be sentient, and&nbsp;
is unable to use these weapons unless&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:46.500 --> 01:46:50.160
asked to. That ultimately worked&nbsp;
out in the favor of Pesterbot.

01:46:50.160 --> 01:46:56.460
During another SCP-682 escape, the two robots were&nbsp;
brought into contact as they both had been broken&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:56.460 --> 01:47:01.920
out of their respective containment spaces during&nbsp;
the brouhaha. Robo-Dude, searching for a Robo-Pal&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:01.920 --> 01:47:07.680
to play with, came upon Pesterbot, who immediately&nbsp;
started spouting its usual overdramatic threats.

01:47:07.680 --> 01:47:12.900
“I am the Crushmaster, doom to all I&nbsp;
survey.” Pesterbot said. “Gaze upon my&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:12.900 --> 01:47:17.220
might and weep. Identify yourself, that I&nbsp;
might know whose destruction I shall sow.”

01:47:17.220 --> 01:47:21.000
Robo-Dude, not programmed to know&nbsp;
how to respond to such a statement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:21.000 --> 01:47:24.120
simply responded by asking&nbsp;
Pesterbot if it wanted to play.

01:47:24.120 --> 01:47:28.560
Pesterbot continued to trash-talk&nbsp;
Robo-Dude, until Robo-Dude decided&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:28.560 --> 01:47:32.880
the best way to play with its new pal&nbsp;
was to engage in its Robo-Dance mode.

01:47:32.880 --> 01:47:38.580
Pesterbot accepted the challenge, saying “Activate&nbsp;
all that you wish, but your fate is sealed. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:38.580 --> 01:47:43.620
Kill-o-tron can not be defeated. I shall render&nbsp;
you unto dust with my mad dancing skills."

01:47:43.620 --> 01:47:48.000
The two robots proceeded to engage in&nbsp;
a dance battle, which the awkwardly&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:48.000 --> 01:47:52.740
shaped Pesterbot failed miserably at.&nbsp;
While Robo-Dude was a competent dancer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:52.740 --> 01:47:57.300
Pesterbot could only gyrate pathetically, before&nbsp;
it fell over and rolled around on the ground,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:57.300 --> 01:48:01.200
hopelessly out of time to the music&nbsp;
coming from Robo-Dude's speakers.

01:48:01.200 --> 01:48:06.660
The song ended, and Robo-Dude declared&nbsp;
- “Robo-dance is complete, Robo-pal.”

01:48:06.660 --> 01:48:11.460
Pesterbot was unable to accept its&nbsp;
failure, and replied- "Ha. Pathetic one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:11.460 --> 01:48:15.420
you have been schooled in the art of the&nbsp;
dance by none other than Mechanobasher,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:15.420 --> 01:48:20.160
Scourge of a Thousand Worlds. Kneel before&nbsp;
me before I end your worthless existence."

01:48:20.160 --> 01:48:25.740
Robo-Dude, unimpressed with Pesterbot's poor&nbsp;
sportsmanship, deployed its hydrogen cannon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:25.740 --> 01:48:30.240
which it was programmed to do whenever&nbsp;
it detected a sore loser. Fortunately&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:30.240 --> 01:48:34.680
for Pesterbot, ”hydrogen cannon” was just&nbsp;
the name for Robo-Dude's water pistol.

01:48:34.680 --> 01:48:38.700
While Pesterbot will attempt to fight&nbsp;
anything that moves, the truth is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:38.700 --> 01:48:44.280
this robot will attack virtually anything it&nbsp;
perceives as being even slightly alive. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:44.280 --> 01:48:48.840
is perhaps best illustrated by one of the tests&nbsp;
of Pesterbot's abilities that involved putting a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:48.840 --> 01:48:53.460
small speaker at the base of a potted houseplant&nbsp;
and speaking through it from another room.

01:48:53.460 --> 01:48:57.000
The plant was placed across from&nbsp;
Pesterbot in the testing chamber&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:57.000 --> 01:49:01.320
and the interaction was monitored by&nbsp;
researchers from outside. Researcher&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:01.320 --> 01:49:05.460
Davies spoke through the speaker in the plant&nbsp;
pot. He asked Pesterbot if it could hear him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:05.460 --> 01:49:10.440
and it answered - “Who dares. All souls&nbsp;
will burn. You will feel the sharp sting&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:10.440 --> 01:49:15.000
of my wrath. Identify yourself so that I&nbsp;
may sing damnation upon you as you die.”

01:49:15.000 --> 01:49:19.440
The robot began approaching the plant.&nbsp;
Davies, speaking as the plant, identified&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:19.440 --> 01:49:24.900
himself, “I am a split-leaf philodendron, a&nbsp;
semi-woody shrub with large glossy leaves.”

01:49:24.900 --> 01:49:28.140
At this point, he had to try&nbsp;
very hard not to laugh at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:28.140 --> 01:49:30.720
absurdity of this test, and the robot's reaction.

01:49:30.720 --> 01:49:34.380
He continued “These leaves can&nbsp;
grow up to three feet long.”

01:49:34.380 --> 01:49:37.800
Pesterbot used its wrench arms to&nbsp;
try and wrestle with the leaves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:37.800 --> 01:49:43.140
but was bested by the mighty plant and unable&nbsp;
to cause any damage. Enraged by its failure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:43.140 --> 01:49:48.840
it said “Your mockery spells your doom. I have&nbsp;
arrived. You will be crushed betwixt my digits.”

01:49:48.840 --> 01:49:53.820
Pesterbot then fell over and was unable&nbsp;
to right itself. It struggled and spent 6&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:53.820 --> 01:49:57.060
minutes trying to stand before its&nbsp;
flailing knocked over the plant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:57.060 --> 01:50:01.980
which did nothing to help and in fact pinned&nbsp;
the robot to the ground. It was at this point&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:01.980 --> 01:50:06.540
that the researchers, who had taken time to&nbsp;
compose themselves after several laughing fits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:06.540 --> 01:50:11.520
entered the chamber and removed Pesterbot in&nbsp;
order to place it back in its display case.

01:50:11.520 --> 01:50:16.200
Despite how non-threatening Pesterbot&nbsp;
is, there is actually one situation&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:16.200 --> 01:50:20.400
in the Foundation's history where it has&nbsp;
actually posed a somewhat serious threat.

01:50:20.400 --> 01:50:25.080
When Mobile Task Forces were sent in to&nbsp;
rescue survivors of the events at Site-13,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:25.080 --> 01:50:30.120
also known as SCP-1730, they were&nbsp;
attacked by alternate universe&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:30.120 --> 01:50:33.540
versions of a variety of creatures&nbsp;
kept in Foundation containment.

01:50:33.540 --> 01:50:38.640
One of these creatures was Pesterbot, who&nbsp;
had in that universe somehow gained control&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:38.640 --> 01:50:44.040
of a larger mechanical body constructed of&nbsp;
discarded pieces of machinery. It threw other&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:44.040 --> 01:50:47.700
pieces of metal scrap at the task force&nbsp;
members as they tried to leave the site,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:47.700 --> 01:50:54.060
shouting in a much deeper and more intimidating&nbsp;
voice - “I am reborn to breathe devastation&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:54.060 --> 01:51:01.440
upon this fetid Earth. Pitiful humans. You will&nbsp;
feel the dark sting of my neverending torment.”

01:51:01.440 --> 01:51:06.060
Members of the task force could see&nbsp;
the original Pesterbot body on top of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:06.060 --> 01:51:09.600
the larger metal construct, waving its arms madly.

01:51:09.600 --> 01:51:15.000
The task force opened fire, to little effect.&nbsp;
Pesterbot tossed another piece of scrap at them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:15.000 --> 01:51:19.440
just narrowly missing. One task force&nbsp;
member threw a frag grenade at the robot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:19.440 --> 01:51:21.420
which it caught, blowing up its hand.

01:51:21.420 --> 01:51:25.920
Another one was able to jump into the air and&nbsp;
reach the tiny robot atop the larger body,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:25.920 --> 01:51:28.320
knocking it against the wall and shattering it.

01:51:28.320 --> 01:51:32.640
Even in the reality where Pesterbot&nbsp;
was able to build itself a larger body,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:32.640 --> 01:51:36.600
it still ultimately wasn't able&nbsp;
to put up that much of a fight.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:36.600 --> 01:51:42.480
Poor Pesterbot. Doomed to be a failure in&nbsp;
this, and apparently, every other reality.

01:51:42.480 --> 01:51:48.720
That's the story of SCP 1370, also known as the&nbsp;
Pesterbot, one of the most hilariously harmless&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:48.720 --> 01:51:54.540
anomalous entities that the foundation currently&nbsp;
has in containment. Perhaps one day he’ll achieve&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:54.540 --> 01:51:59.760
his goal of conquering the universe, he just has&nbsp;
to figure out how to conquer his toy box first.

01:51:59.760 --> 01:52:05.160
Hello again, dear viewers. You’ve caught me&nbsp;
during my break. What? Even researchers at&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:05.160 --> 01:52:09.600
the SCP Foundation need a little downtime&nbsp;
now and then. And considering they revoked&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:09.600 --> 01:52:14.820
my playtime privileges with The Living Lego and&nbsp;
the Nerfing Gun after the unfortunate incident in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:14.820 --> 01:52:19.560
the Site-19 Break Room, I’ve been downgraded to&nbsp;
keeping myself occupied with this little rubber&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:19.560 --> 01:52:24.240
ball. “I have a ball. Perhaps you'd like to bounce&nbsp;
it,” they said. Seems innocent enough, doesn’t it?&nbsp;

01:52:24.240 --> 01:52:32.640
Oh, have you learned nothing? Almost any&nbsp;
object, even something as innocuous as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:32.640 --> 01:52:38.040
Teddy Bear or a plastic paddling pool, can&nbsp;
hide secrets. Sure, any avid basketball fan&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:38.040 --> 01:52:42.660
will happily tell you that ball is life. But&nbsp;
sometimes, when you’re dealing with anomalies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:42.660 --> 01:52:52.500
a ball can also… be death. Such is the case of&nbsp;
SCP-018, also known as The Super Ball. An anomaly&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:52.500 --> 01:52:58.800
so powerful that the Foundation has even used&nbsp;
it to help recontain SCP-682 during a breach.

01:52:58.800 --> 01:53:05.040
To the untrained observer, it really isn’t much&nbsp;
to look at. A small, red rubber ball, about six&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:05.040 --> 01:53:11.160
centimeters in diameter, produced by the Wham-O&nbsp;
company in 1969. Yeah, we were as surprised as&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:11.160 --> 01:53:15.780
you to find out that good ol’ Dr. Wondertainment&nbsp;
had nothing to do with this little number. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:15.780 --> 01:53:21.660
might also be surprised by just how extensive the&nbsp;
containment procedures are for SCP-018 - After&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:21.660 --> 01:53:26.820
all, it’s rare that a competently non-sentient&nbsp;
anomaly makes its way into the Euclid class.

01:53:26.820 --> 01:53:32.640
This little rubber ball is kept in a&nbsp;
specially-made titanium-alloy metal restraint,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:32.640 --> 01:53:38.760
submerged in a dense polyethylene holding tank,&nbsp;
filled with a special endothermic compound that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:38.760 --> 01:53:44.040
draws the kinetic energy out of its surroundings.&nbsp;
Anyone entering the chamber needs to wear a unique&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:44.040 --> 01:53:50.160
kind of reinforced plate armor, and if SCP-018&nbsp;
ever manages to escape its containment tank,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:50.160 --> 01:53:55.020
nearby personnel are advised to treat it&nbsp;
as they would an active shooter situation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:55.020 --> 01:53:59.280
Lock yourself in a nearby room, duck&nbsp;
down below any cover you can find,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:59.280 --> 01:54:03.960
and wait for armed containment units to&nbsp;
show up and take control of the situation.

01:54:03.960 --> 01:54:08.280
At this point, you probably have a lot&nbsp;
of questions, chief among them being:&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:08.280 --> 01:54:13.380
Why is this children’s toy being given more&nbsp;
security precautions than most serial killers?

01:54:13.380 --> 01:54:16.980
The ball was first discovered when&nbsp;
a cleaning and disposal company was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:16.980 --> 01:54:21.960
hired to empty out an old Wham-O warehouse of&nbsp;
some defunct merchandise. One of the movers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:21.960 --> 01:54:27.000
Roy Fischler, embraced his inner child when he&nbsp;
saw a red, rubber ball sitting among all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:27.000 --> 01:54:32.520
dusty boxes of expired silly putty and broken&nbsp;
remote control robots. He thought to himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:33.120 --> 01:54:36.180
“what’s the harm in having a little fun&nbsp;
here? It’s all going into the trash anyway.”

01:54:36.180 --> 01:54:40.380
This would prove to be a terrible&nbsp;
mistake for all involved.

01:54:40.380 --> 01:54:47.880
The anomalous property of SCP-018 is the fact that&nbsp;
it’s able to bounce at 200% efficiency every time&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:47.880 --> 01:54:53.160
it’s bounced, increasing at an exponential&nbsp;
rate with each bounce unless it’s stopped&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:53.160 --> 01:54:58.500
by some equivalent force. So when Roy decided&nbsp;
to toss that little red ball onto the ground,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:58.500 --> 01:55:04.080
he’d unleashed a destructive force into that&nbsp;
warehouse that was more powerful than he&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:04.080 --> 01:55:09.600
ever could have imagined. With every bounce, the&nbsp;
ball increased its height and speed, ricocheting&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:09.600 --> 01:55:15.720
across the floor, walls, and ceiling like a bullet&nbsp;
run amok. And it was only just getting started.

01:55:15.720 --> 01:55:20.640
The workers hit the deck as the item that&nbsp;
would later be dubbed SCP-018 decided to put&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:20.640 --> 01:55:25.260
the “ball” in “ballistic.” It smashed&nbsp;
light fixtures. Knocked over piles of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:25.260 --> 01:55:30.540
boxes. Smashed through forklifts. When one&nbsp;
worker tried to catch it, it left a red-hot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:30.540 --> 01:55:35.280
ball-shaped hole in the middle of his palm.&nbsp;
It wasn’t long before the ball had built up&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:35.280 --> 01:55:39.780
such speed that the warehouse could no&nbsp;
longer contain it. It blasted out of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:39.780 --> 01:55:45.360
wall and rocketed into the nearby city, ready&nbsp;
to cause more abject chaos wherever it went.

01:55:45.360 --> 01:55:50.580
Windows were shattered. Street lights were&nbsp;
annihilated. Cars crashed. Thankfully,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:50.580 --> 01:55:54.720
no humans were killed by the rampaging&nbsp;
rubber menace, but in total five people&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:54.720 --> 01:56:01.260
were injured - And one unfortunate pigeon was&nbsp;
utterly obliterated on impact. It went on for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:01.260 --> 01:56:06.420
several days, with the ball reaching over 100&nbsp;
kilometers per hour at several points, before&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:06.420 --> 01:56:12.180
finally coming to rest at a nearby lake. At which&nbsp;
point, it was retrieved by Foundation personnel.

01:56:12.180 --> 01:56:16.320
There were two silver linings to this&nbsp;
incredibly strange anomalous incident:&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:16.320 --> 01:56:21.840
The first was, as previously alluded to,&nbsp;
nobody actually died. The second was that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:21.840 --> 01:56:27.420
due to the incredible speeds at which SCP-018&nbsp;
moved, none of the civilian witnesses had&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:27.420 --> 01:56:31.620
any idea what they’d just encountered.&nbsp;
As far as anomalous discoveries go,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:31.620 --> 01:56:34.680
this was pretty much as close&nbsp;
to a win as you can get.

01:56:34.680 --> 01:56:39.960
But for one ambitious researcher, it was&nbsp;
far more than just a pleasingly non-violent&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:39.960 --> 01:56:45.780
initial containment story - It was a doorway to&nbsp;
improving the Foundation’s technology as a whole.

01:56:45.780 --> 01:56:51.420
Meet Dr. Brian Karella. He’s what you&nbsp;
might call a maverick, a blue sky thinker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:51.420 --> 01:56:56.460
always on his grind. The cornerstone of Dr.&nbsp;
Karella’s philosophy was that the Foundation&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:56.460 --> 01:57:01.680
should be making more active use of its benign&nbsp;
anomalies to help track down and contain their&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:01.680 --> 01:57:06.120
more ornery foes. While everyone else&nbsp;
around him seemed to only see a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:06.120 --> 01:57:13.260
rubber nuisance that was better locked away, Dr.&nbsp;
Karella saw the immense potential in SCP-018.

01:57:13.260 --> 01:57:16.680
Namely, in providing a vital&nbsp;
enhancement to another piece&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:16.680 --> 01:57:20.700
of Foundation technology: The SCP-A5 Armor.

01:57:20.700 --> 01:57:25.620
It goes without saying that working for the&nbsp;
SCP Foundation is an incredibly dangerous job,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:25.620 --> 01:57:29.880
especially if you’re part of one of the&nbsp;
Foundation’s myriad mobile task forces.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:29.880 --> 01:57:35.820
The hunt for anomalies can lead to all kinds of&nbsp;
strange and inhospitable terrains. Before it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:35.820 --> 01:57:42.180
contained, SCP-096 was famously captured on an&nbsp;
icy, frozen tundra on top of a bleak mountain.

01:57:42.180 --> 01:57:46.500
Because of incidents like this, the&nbsp;
Foundation had first invested money&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:46.500 --> 01:57:51.540
in the SCP-A5 Tactical Armor Suit. Think&nbsp;
of them like the Foundation’s answer to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:51.540 --> 01:57:55.680
the Iron Man suit. However, when dressed in&nbsp;
several hundred pounds of reinforced metal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:55.680 --> 01:57:59.160
something important suffers&nbsp;
along the way: Mobility.

01:57:59.160 --> 01:58:04.320
It isn’t exactly easy to lug all that&nbsp;
equipment around. And when an anomaly&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:04.320 --> 01:58:08.760
is heading for the hills at high speeds, being&nbsp;
well-defended doesn’t count for much if you’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:08.760 --> 01:58:13.380
already ten miles behind the target in&nbsp;
pursuit. This, in Dr. Karella’s mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:13.380 --> 01:58:19.500
was exactly where SCP-018 came into play.&nbsp;
If that little red ball was built into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:19.500 --> 01:58:24.900
foot of an SCP-A5 Armor suit, it could be&nbsp;
used to bounce the operative wearing it to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:24.900 --> 01:58:29.400
tremendous heights. This would allow them&nbsp;
to not only give chance against fast-moving&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:29.400 --> 01:58:35.280
anomalies but also cover difficult terrain and&nbsp;
even scale great heights in a mere instant.

01:58:35.280 --> 01:58:41.880
But it wasn’t just an increase to mobility that&nbsp;
SCP-018 integration offered to the SCP-A5 suit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:41.880 --> 01:58:47.460
Picture this: With SCP-108 fused to the&nbsp;
sole of the suit’s reinforced metal foot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:47.460 --> 01:58:53.460
lending it 200% bouncing efficiency, it&nbsp;
could unleash a deadly, concrete-shattering&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:53.460 --> 01:58:57.180
kick. The kind of thing that would leave&nbsp;
you singing in falsetto for the rest of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:57.180 --> 01:59:00.780
your life if you were lucky enough to&nbsp;
survive it, if you know what I mean.

01:59:00.780 --> 01:59:06.660
To Dr. Karella, it was a match made in heaven.&nbsp;
It could increase both the mobility and combat&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:06.660 --> 01:59:12.540
effectiveness of the suits by a considerable&nbsp;
margin. And seeing as SCP-018 literally didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:12.540 --> 01:59:16.680
have a mind of its own, there’s no way it&nbsp;
could suddenly go rogue during a mission&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:16.680 --> 01:59:21.720
and compromise everything - just as Able&nbsp;
had during his stint as a Mobile Task Force&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:21.720 --> 01:59:26.580
operator before they ran out of targets for him&nbsp;
and he started slaughtering his own comrades.

01:59:26.580 --> 01:59:32.280
Dr. Karella was so confident in the efficacy&nbsp;
of this idea, he contacted the O5 Council&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:32.280 --> 01:59:37.080
personally to request approval for this&nbsp;
little pet project. This approval was granted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:37.080 --> 01:59:42.240
on a purely experimental basis. The technology&nbsp;
would have to prove itself before being put&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:42.240 --> 01:59:47.400
into common use. It wasn’t exactly what&nbsp;
Karella wanted to hear, but it was a step&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:47.400 --> 01:59:52.500
in the right direction. He just needed the&nbsp;
perfect opportunity to put them to the test.

01:59:52.500 --> 01:59:58.320
And then it came: SCP-682, the&nbsp;
Infamous Hard to Destroy Reptile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:58.320 --> 02:00:03.000
breached containment. It’d somehow adapted&nbsp;
a cloaking ability and escaped further from&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:03.000 --> 02:00:05.040
the site it was being held at than ever before.

02:00:05.040 --> 02:00:10.200
The next time it was spotted was over two&nbsp;
weeks later, causing pandemonium in the Amazon&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:10.200 --> 02:00:16.140
Rainforest, devouring tribesmen and wreaking&nbsp;
havoc on the local ecosystem. They needed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:16.140 --> 02:00:21.120
get on the scene fast and put that monster down&nbsp;
before the damage it caused was irreparable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:21.120 --> 02:00:26.400
and news of the Beast in the Amazon leaked&nbsp;
out into the local area. And thankfully,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:26.400 --> 02:00:29.280
Dr. Karella had just the tool for the job.

02:00:29.280 --> 02:00:33.180
Field Agent Hammersmith was&nbsp;
fitted with an SCP-A5 suit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:33.180 --> 02:00:39.000
complete with the SCP-018 enhancement in&nbsp;
his dominant right leg. He was dispatched,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:39.000 --> 02:00:42.480
and immediately, the augmented&nbsp;
suit began to show its efficacy.

02:00:42.480 --> 02:00:47.580
Agent Hammersmith bounded above the treeline&nbsp;
at incredible speeds, using the HUD in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:47.580 --> 02:00:52.740
suit’s mask to track the heat signature of&nbsp;
SCP-682 moving among the trees below him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:52.740 --> 02:00:57.060
He’d been able to bind a tracking collar to&nbsp;
the beast’s neck during an earlier engagement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:57.060 --> 02:01:02.640
but now the true battle was on. His heart was&nbsp;
pounding, adrenaline coursing through his veins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:02.640 --> 02:01:08.220
but he’d been ensured that even 682 would have&nbsp;
a hard time tearing through his suit’s armor.

02:01:08.220 --> 02:01:14.400
A commander directing him through an earpiece&nbsp;
told him now was the time to engage, before 682&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:14.400 --> 02:01:18.960
detected his presence and began developing&nbsp;
countermeasures. Dr. Karella was watching&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:18.960 --> 02:01:23.760
a live feed from the control room, confident&nbsp;
that his 018-augmented suit would do the job.

02:01:23.760 --> 02:01:29.700
Following his orders to the letter, Hammersmith&nbsp;
descended down onto 682 from above. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:29.700 --> 02:01:34.140
before he could make contact, the reptile&nbsp;
swiped him with its tail, sending his body&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:34.140 --> 02:01:40.140
sailing into a nearby tree. However, the suit&nbsp;
stood firm. He felt the impact, but the actual&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:40.140 --> 02:01:45.300
damage was minimal. Now, it was time for him&nbsp;
to face off against the beast, one on one.

02:01:45.300 --> 02:01:50.100
He didn’t have time to be afraid. As the&nbsp;
beast descended upon him, fangs bared,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:50.100 --> 02:01:54.420
he gave it a swift kick to the bottom&nbsp;
jaw, tearing off half of its face. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:54.420 --> 02:01:59.700
anyone who knows SCP-682 would tell you that&nbsp;
simply removing its bottom jaw wouldn’t even&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:59.700 --> 02:02:06.840
slow 682 down. It was relentless, attacking&nbsp;
Hammersmith with its fangs and jaws. It tore&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:06.840 --> 02:02:11.760
deep ruts into the suit’s metal, but the suit&nbsp;
was still managing to keep its occupant safe.

02:02:11.760 --> 02:02:14.280
Then Hammersmith struck back.

02:02:14.280 --> 02:02:20.760
Kick after kick, augmented by the 200% power&nbsp;
of the Super Ball, sliced huge chunks from&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:20.760 --> 02:02:27.060
the reptile’s body. He kicked in its chest,&nbsp;
kicked off limbs, and with a final mighty kick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:27.060 --> 02:02:31.860
even managed to bisect the beast in the&nbsp;
middle. Of course, even that wouldn’t keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:31.860 --> 02:02:37.200
it down for long, but with the hum of evacuation&nbsp;
and containment helicopters rapidly approaching,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:37.200 --> 02:02:43.140
Hammersmith breathed a sigh of relief. Back&nbsp;
in the control room, everyone celebrated - Dr.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:43.140 --> 02:02:48.780
Karella most of all, vindicated in his&nbsp;
belief that SCP-018 could save the day.

02:02:48.780 --> 02:02:51.540
And then Agent Hammersmith, without even thinking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:51.540 --> 02:02:55.740
rested his right foot on the floor&nbsp;
just that little bit too firmly.

02:02:55.740 --> 02:03:01.260
The sudden bounce sent Hammersmith a mile into the&nbsp;
air. His Foundation-trained composure evaporated;&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:01.260 --> 02:03:06.480
all he could do was scream as he blasted&nbsp;
up into the clouds, then began to plummet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:06.480 --> 02:03:11.100
Back in the control room, everyone could see&nbsp;
a live feed of Agent Hammersmith hurtling into&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:11.100 --> 02:03:15.780
a nearby lake and landing with an epic&nbsp;
splash. While the regenerating remains&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:15.780 --> 02:03:20.220
of SCP-682 were contained and brought&nbsp;
back to the nearest containment site,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:20.220 --> 02:03:25.200
another team was sent in to retrieve&nbsp;
the heavily injured Agent Hammersmith.

02:03:25.200 --> 02:03:30.660
His medical examination afterward revealed he’d&nbsp;
suffered two broken legs, seven broken ribs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:30.660 --> 02:03:34.860
a missing arm, and a skull fracture.&nbsp;
If he hadn’t been wearing the suit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:34.860 --> 02:03:39.060
his mangled remains probably would have&nbsp;
been devoured by the local piranhas by now.

02:03:39.060 --> 02:03:44.280
But despite that minor hiccup in the plan, Dr.&nbsp;
Karella was still delighted with the results,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:44.280 --> 02:03:49.380
and immensely proud of the little red ball that&nbsp;
could. In his closing remarks on the incident,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:49.380 --> 02:03:53.280
he appended a final note to&nbsp;
the SCP-018 file, reading:

02:03:53.280 --> 02:03:57.660
“Don't worry, it's fixed. But, I&nbsp;
have some more ideas. If I can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:57.660 --> 02:04:01.800
granted the use of some water from&nbsp;
SCP-006, as well as some other SCPs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:01.800 --> 02:04:06.240
I can deliver you a set of SCP-A5 armor&nbsp;
and an agent that can capture any,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:06.240 --> 02:04:11.820
if not all, rogue or unattained SCPs.&nbsp;
All I'm waiting on is your approval.”

02:04:11.820 --> 02:04:17.640
Welcome to Planet Earth. She’s got the toughest&nbsp;
streets around, and if you’re not careful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:17.640 --> 02:04:21.660
that mean old missy won’t hesitate&nbsp;
to wash out her gutters with your&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:21.660 --> 02:04:26.940
blood and decorate the sidewalks with your&nbsp;
teeth. It’s a tough world for the good and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:26.940 --> 02:04:31.620
innocent, with the looming shadow of&nbsp;
crime lurking around every corner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:31.620 --> 02:04:37.560
just waiting to prey on those who are unable to&nbsp;
defend themselves. If you don’t have the tools&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:37.560 --> 02:04:43.200
to survive, it’ll chew you up and spit&nbsp;
you out like a wad of stale chewing gum.

02:04:43.200 --> 02:04:50.460
That’s why we need a hero. A dark&nbsp;
defender. A cloak in the night. We need…

02:04:50.460 --> 02:04:52.020
The Specter.

02:04:52.020 --> 02:04:58.260
New Delhi, India.A woman walks home from&nbsp;
her late-night job. She’s exhausted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:58.260 --> 02:05:03.660
she can feel the bone-deep ache of fourteen&nbsp;
hours on a factory line, running herself&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:03.660 --> 02:05:09.900
ragged to support her three kids back home. She&nbsp;
can barely stand; can you really blame her for&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:09.900 --> 02:05:14.700
wanting to take the shortcut down that dark alley,&nbsp;
knowing it’ll shave fifteen minutes off her trip?

02:05:14.700 --> 02:05:20.820
She ducks in, clutching her purse tight and&nbsp;
keeping her head down, but it won’t do any good.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:20.820 --> 02:05:28.080
A predator has been laying in wait. He emerges&nbsp;
from the darkness, wielding a huge knife, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:28.080 --> 02:05:33.180
grabs her by the shoulder. He’s a few heads&nbsp;
taller than her, with arms thicker than her&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:33.180 --> 02:05:38.640
neck. He tells her to hand over the money she’d&nbsp;
spent all month working for, or he’d turn her&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:38.640 --> 02:05:45.420
kids into orphans. His knife had tasted blood&nbsp;
before and he’d have no problem killing again.

02:05:45.420 --> 02:05:49.380
With tears streaking down her face,&nbsp;
knowing it’s futile to resist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:49.380 --> 02:05:51.840
she reaches out and passes him the money.

02:05:51.840 --> 02:05:57.960
The bandit snatches it from her trembling hand&nbsp;
and snickers. Candy from a baby. He’s ready to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:57.960 --> 02:06:02.520
turn around and make a run with tonight’s&nbsp;
takings when he feels an odd chill drift&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:02.520 --> 02:06:09.840
into the alley. A soft whoosh. He turns and sees&nbsp;
a figure standing and watching him. He’s tall and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:09.840 --> 02:06:15.960
well-built, with a long, black coat and a black&nbsp;
wide-brimmed hat. He was darker than the dark:&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:15.960 --> 02:06:21.660
Light seemed to disappear into him,&nbsp;
a black shape against the background.

02:06:21.660 --> 02:06:29.520
It’s him. The man that all criminals know&nbsp;
and fear, all over the world, even if they’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:29.520 --> 02:06:34.860
never met him. The intrusive thought, the&nbsp;
moment of creeping doubt that slithers into&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:34.860 --> 02:06:41.340
their minds the second they even contemplate&nbsp;
breaking the law. The bad guys’ boogeyman.

02:06:41.340 --> 02:06:42.600
The Specter.

02:06:42.600 --> 02:06:48.840
“Halt, evildoer!” the Specter commands.&nbsp;
“You’ll return this fine lady’s money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:48.840 --> 02:06:51.000
or you’ll suffer the consequences.”

02:06:51.000 --> 02:06:54.600
The bandit feels a tremor&nbsp;
of fear quiver through him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:54.600 --> 02:07:02.100
but he won’t back down. Not yet. He charges The&nbsp;
Specter with his blade, slashing like a madman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:02.100 --> 02:07:08.220
but not a single hit lands. The Specter weaves&nbsp;
perfectly away from every strike and effortlessly&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:08.220 --> 02:07:13.140
disarms the bandit with a perfectly-placed whack&nbsp;
to the wrist. The knife clatters to the ground.

02:07:13.140 --> 02:07:17.940
He discombobulates the bandit with an&nbsp;
open-palmed strike to either side of his head,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:17.940 --> 02:07:22.380
then knocks him out with a laser-focused&nbsp;
headbutt. He crumples to the ground,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:22.380 --> 02:07:27.300
unconscious, and The Specter&nbsp;
breathes a sigh. Another battle won.

02:07:27.300 --> 02:07:31.140
This whole time, the woman has&nbsp;
been watching, frozen in awe.

02:07:31.140 --> 02:07:35.100
The Specter picks up her stolen money and&nbsp;
hands it back to her with a gentlemanly&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:35.100 --> 02:07:41.280
doff of his wide-brimmed hat. He really&nbsp;
is just darkness underneath. Unseeable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:41.280 --> 02:07:46.920
unknowable, a living shadow. She thanks&nbsp;
him, and he assures her that it’s all&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:46.920 --> 02:07:52.140
in a day’s work for a crimefighter like&nbsp;
him. After all, somebody needs to do it.

02:07:52.140 --> 02:07:57.240
And in a flash of smoke, he’s gone. The&nbsp;
woman makes it home safely that night.

02:07:57.240 --> 02:08:03.360
London, England. A man lays beaten and bloody&nbsp;
on the ground. Three assailants surround him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:03.360 --> 02:08:09.240
circling like hungry wolves. One wields&nbsp;
a long lead pipe, still slick with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:09.240 --> 02:08:14.580
man’s blood. Another, a switchblade, that&nbsp;
he keeps clicking in and out of the handle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:14.580 --> 02:08:20.700
And the third carries a handgun. The same&nbsp;
handgun he’s used to take two lives before.

02:08:20.700 --> 02:08:26.580
The wounded man on the ground - with bruises,&nbsp;
cuts, a broken ankle, a broken wrist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:26.580 --> 02:08:32.580
four broken ribs, and three missing teeth -&nbsp;
is desperate and afraid. He’s here because&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:32.580 --> 02:08:37.440
he was forced into a corner. He needed to&nbsp;
borrow money from a dangerous man tucked&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:37.440 --> 02:08:43.260
away in a dingy building in London’s East&nbsp;
End, the city’s organized crime epicenter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:43.260 --> 02:08:48.360
The money he’d borrowed came with predatory&nbsp;
interest that he couldn’t pay back. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:48.360 --> 02:08:52.860
he’s paying the difference in blood to&nbsp;
that same loan shark’s violent goons.

02:08:52.860 --> 02:08:57.780
The man with the gun, in a thick cockney&nbsp;
accent, tells him that this is why he&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:57.780 --> 02:09:02.760
shouldn’t have messed around with Mr. Ford.&nbsp;
Now he’s going to be an example for anyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:02.760 --> 02:09:08.280
else stupid enough to think they can stiff the&nbsp;
big man on a payment and live to tell the tale.

02:09:08.280 --> 02:09:12.720
He nods to the man with the pipe&nbsp;
to finish the job. The beaten and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:12.720 --> 02:09:16.620
bloodied man on the ground closes&nbsp;
his eyes as the goon steps closer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:16.620 --> 02:09:22.080
lifting up the red-stained pipe and preparing&nbsp;
to finally cave his pitiful head in with it.

02:09:22.080 --> 02:09:28.680
In the dark, he hears the whoosh of the pipe’s&nbsp;
downswing, and the breeze hits his bruised cheek,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:28.680 --> 02:09:34.320
but the pipe never connects. Instead,&nbsp;
there’s a chorus of gasps. The man on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:34.320 --> 02:09:37.980
ground opens his eyes to get a better&nbsp;
look at what on earth has happened.

02:09:37.980 --> 02:09:44.700
A tall, dark man in a long, black coat and&nbsp;
a wide-brimmed hat is standing over him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:44.700 --> 02:09:50.400
holding the lead pipe that he just easily&nbsp;
snatched from the hands of its previous user.

02:09:50.400 --> 02:09:56.580
The goons are all stepping back. The gunman raises&nbsp;
his pistol. The other, his knife. All of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:56.580 --> 02:10:01.440
look afraid. The man on the ground doesn’t&nbsp;
know the stranger who just saved his life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:01.440 --> 02:10:08.520
but in his presence, he feels something wash over&nbsp;
him that he hasn’t felt in a long while: Safety.

02:10:08.520 --> 02:10:11.700
It’s him. It’s The Specter.

02:10:11.700 --> 02:10:16.020
“You wretched villains. Don’t you&nbsp;
know better than to pick on the weak,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:16.020 --> 02:10:20.700
a bunch of ‘tough guys’ like you? How&nbsp;
about fighting someone your own size?”

02:10:20.700 --> 02:10:27.360
In a blind panic, the gunman raises his pistol&nbsp;
and fires. The Specter moves like smoke in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:27.360 --> 02:10:32.100
wind. He dodges the bullet and tosses the&nbsp;
lead pipe. It sails through the air and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:32.100 --> 02:10:37.140
hits the gunman’s skull with an almighty&nbsp;
crack. He collapses, dropping his weapon.

02:10:37.140 --> 02:10:41.460
The other two goons bum rush him,&nbsp;
but it doesn’t serve them any better.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:41.460 --> 02:10:46.140
One slashes, the other punches. With&nbsp;
a few skillful, fluid movements,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:46.140 --> 02:10:51.000
he guides one goon’s knife-wielding hand into&nbsp;
the other’s shoulder, then disables both with&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:51.000 --> 02:10:55.920
a pair of simultaneous chops to the throat&nbsp;
that Jackie Chan himself would be proud of.

02:10:55.920 --> 02:11:01.920
All three goons are laid out on the ground,&nbsp;
either writhing and gasping or knocked out cold.

02:11:01.920 --> 02:11:07.140
The Specter straightens his coat and hat&nbsp;
with practiced finesse and returns to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:07.140 --> 02:11:12.540
man laying on the ground. After checking that&nbsp;
he was still alive and conscious, he passed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:12.540 --> 02:11:17.280
man a wad of cash and a phone, telling him to&nbsp;
call an ambulance and get his wounds treated.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:17.280 --> 02:11:23.100
None of those men would ever bother him again,&nbsp;
and if they or any others dared to, then they&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:23.100 --> 02:11:29.400
could expect to see the dark figure of The Specter&nbsp;
appearing behind them in their bathroom mirror.

02:11:29.400 --> 02:11:35.220
After all, someone needs to be there to&nbsp;
fight crime, even when nobody else will.

02:11:35.220 --> 02:11:42.360
New York City, USA. Things are going sideways&nbsp;
at a major bank downtown. A group of well-armed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:42.360 --> 02:11:47.040
highly organized robbers has broken in. They’ve&nbsp;
taken at least fifty hostages throughout the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:47.040 --> 02:11:51.420
building, and have informed the police blockade&nbsp;
outside that unless their demands are met and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:51.420 --> 02:11:55.980
they’re given free passage out of the building,&nbsp;
they’ll start executing people left and right.

02:11:55.980 --> 02:11:59.940
And given the ruthless organization&nbsp;
of this particular criminal crew,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:59.940 --> 02:12:04.080
it seems more than likely that they’ll put&nbsp;
their blood money where their mouth is.

02:12:04.080 --> 02:12:07.740
Police squad cars form a horseshoe&nbsp;
blockade around the building outside,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:07.740 --> 02:12:14.460
armed with handguns, shotguns, and assault rifles.&nbsp;
Police snipers are finding their ideal perches in&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:14.460 --> 02:12:19.860
the surrounding skyscrapers. SWAT is on its way&nbsp;
and a police helicopter circles above. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:19.860 --> 02:12:24.000
even if everything goes right, the&nbsp;
higher-ups know that some fatalities&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:24.000 --> 02:12:28.140
are basically inevitable. Crews of&nbsp;
hardcore career criminals like this&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:28.140 --> 02:12:34.140
wouldn’t go down without a fight. One way&nbsp;
or another, some lives would be taken today.

02:12:34.140 --> 02:12:39.300
Another transmission is delivered from the&nbsp;
inside after that: The robbers know that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:39.300 --> 02:12:45.840
time isn’t on their side, so they’re flipping the&nbsp;
script. Five minutes, exactly. If their demands&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:45.840 --> 02:12:51.840
aren’t met by then, blood would be staining&nbsp;
the bank’s immaculate marble floor. Tick tock.

02:12:51.840 --> 02:12:55.680
The negotiators on the front line are&nbsp;
in a cold sweat. Five minutes!? No,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:55.680 --> 02:13:00.240
that isn’t enough time. We can’t mobilize,&nbsp;
it’ll be a massacre in there! How could&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:00.240 --> 02:13:04.800
ten greedy maniacs with assault rifles pull&nbsp;
the rug out from under them all like this?

02:13:04.800 --> 02:13:10.020
Panicked thoughts swim through the head of a&nbsp;
sergeant heading the blockade. He can’t concede,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:10.020 --> 02:13:14.280
his superiors would never allow it. Does&nbsp;
he wait five minutes and see what happens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:14.280 --> 02:13:18.900
or authorize his officers to breach the&nbsp;
front doors, try to reclaim the bank,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:18.900 --> 02:13:23.760
and potentially create one of the worst bank&nbsp;
robbery bloodbaths in New York City history?

02:13:23.760 --> 02:13:30.720
Damned if he does, damned if he doesn’t. And&nbsp;
every second, the time to decide runs out…

02:13:30.720 --> 02:13:35.940
That’s when he hears a soft whoosh behind&nbsp;
him. Paranoid enough to jump at shadows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:35.940 --> 02:13:41.280
the sergeant turns, reaching for his gun, and&nbsp;
sees what seems to be a dapper shadow standing&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:41.280 --> 02:13:48.000
right behind him. A tall man, with a long,&nbsp;
black coat and a wide-brimmed hat. No face,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:48.000 --> 02:13:53.880
just darkness itself under the shade of the brim.&nbsp;
The stranger places a calm hand on his shoulder,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:53.880 --> 02:13:58.140
and suddenly, a sense of trust like&nbsp;
he hasn’t felt in years just sets in.

02:13:58.140 --> 02:14:03.240
Somehow, deep in his marrow, he&nbsp;
knows this man is here to help.

02:14:03.240 --> 02:14:08.220
“Rest assured, sergeant, everything is&nbsp;
under control now. For I, The Specter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:08.220 --> 02:14:11.340
have arrived to save the day.&nbsp;
How many criminals are inside?”

02:14:11.340 --> 02:14:14.220
The sergeant murmurs ten, in awe.

02:14:14.220 --> 02:14:18.660
The Specter nods. “Worry not,&nbsp;
sergeant. Nobody will die today.”

02:14:18.660 --> 02:14:24.660
And with that, like the ghost that is&nbsp;
his very namesake, The Specter is gone.

02:14:24.660 --> 02:14:28.800
Inside the bank, the leader of the&nbsp;
robbers checks his watch. Two minutes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:28.800 --> 02:14:34.440
and still no word from the cops outside.&nbsp;
He’s starting to lose his patience. A few&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:34.440 --> 02:14:39.600
unfortunate bank tellers and customers crouch&nbsp;
around him, their hands behind their heads.

02:14:39.600 --> 02:14:43.920
Five of his men are out here with him,&nbsp;
armed and keeping the situation under&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:43.920 --> 02:14:47.760
control. The other four have cracked&nbsp;
the security on the vault and they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:47.760 --> 02:14:52.260
hauling out money by the bagful.&nbsp;
And still, silence from the cops.

02:14:52.260 --> 02:14:57.780
He sneers, and the thought crosses his mind that&nbsp;
perhaps if he takes out a hostage or two now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:57.780 --> 02:15:03.780
the mooks out there might actually take his&nbsp;
threats seriously. That thought amuses him. Yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:03.780 --> 02:15:06.780
that’ll show ‘em. They’ll finally&nbsp;
know who they’re dealing with here.

02:15:06.780 --> 02:15:11.040
The leader shoulders his assault rifle&nbsp;
and draws a bead on the hostage sitting&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:11.040 --> 02:15:15.780
closest to him. Even his men are shocked by the&nbsp;
suddenness of it all, but they know better than&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:15.780 --> 02:15:20.880
to second guess their boss at a time like&nbsp;
this. He always was a little trigger happy…

02:15:20.880 --> 02:15:25.260
The hostage - an older gentleman who’d only&nbsp;
come into the bank to check his balance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:25.260 --> 02:15:29.100
cause he hadn’t figured out how to do&nbsp;
it online just yet - sees the gun and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:29.100 --> 02:15:34.260
winces. He survived ‘Nam, and this&nbsp;
was how he was going to go. Gunned&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:34.260 --> 02:15:39.060
down on a Wednesday afternoon by some&nbsp;
creep for chump change. What a world.

02:15:39.060 --> 02:15:43.680
The leader’s finger curls around the trigger,&nbsp;
but before he can complete a squeeze,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:43.680 --> 02:15:48.420
an expertly-aimed fist collides with the&nbsp;
back of his head, knocking him unconscious&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:48.420 --> 02:15:53.580
in a single strike. The leader collapses,&nbsp;
his rifle sliding across the ground. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:53.580 --> 02:15:59.340
Specter stands where he stood, straightening his&nbsp;
coat and blowing dandruff off of his knuckles.

02:15:59.340 --> 02:16:02.940
“Are you fellas ready to dance?” he growls.

02:16:02.940 --> 02:16:07.860
He glides through them, moving with the grace&nbsp;
of a prima ballerina and hitting with the force&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:07.860 --> 02:16:13.080
of a freight train. The goons are down before&nbsp;
any of them can fire a single shot. With the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:13.080 --> 02:16:18.060
help of some quick-thinking bank tellers, The&nbsp;
Specter closes the vault door from the outside,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:18.060 --> 02:16:23.760
trapping the remaining four robbers&nbsp;
inside. Textbook crimefighting once more.

02:16:23.760 --> 02:16:30.000
The bank customers and employees, weeping with&nbsp;
joy and relief, thank the Specter for saving&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:30.000 --> 02:16:35.880
their lives. He shakes his head and assures them&nbsp;
it was nothing. Just doing what he could, as any&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:35.880 --> 02:16:41.640
concerned citizen should. He would have left&nbsp;
after that, were it not for one little problem.

02:16:41.640 --> 02:16:45.540
The NYPD has a number of employees at&nbsp;
different levels of their sprawling&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:45.540 --> 02:16:49.800
structure that are actually deeply&nbsp;
embedded undercover agents of the SCP&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:49.800 --> 02:16:54.600
Foundation. And this isn’t a comic book.&nbsp;
There’s a word for when a mysterious man&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:54.600 --> 02:16:59.640
made from darkness appears from nowhere, and&nbsp;
effortlessly takes down ten heavily-armed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:59.640 --> 02:17:04.260
highly-trained combatants by himself,&nbsp;
and that word was “Anomalous.”

02:17:04.260 --> 02:17:09.840
Knowing what this mysterious combatant was capable&nbsp;
of, the Foundation sends some of their best Mobile&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:09.840 --> 02:17:14.700
Task Force Operatives to apprehend him, under&nbsp;
the guise of just being standard SWAT Team&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:14.700 --> 02:17:20.280
members sent in to get the situation back under&nbsp;
control in the aftermath of the robbery. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:20.280 --> 02:17:25.439
much to their extreme surprise, The&nbsp;
Specter puts up absolutely no resistance&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:25.439 --> 02:17:30.419
to them. He understands that the men here to&nbsp;
apprehend him are servants of law and order,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:30.420 --> 02:17:34.560
and would not defy their will, but&nbsp;
he pleads with them to reconsider.

02:17:34.560 --> 02:17:39.000
“You don’t understand, sirs, you’re&nbsp;
making a huge mistake,” he says,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:39.000 --> 02:17:43.859
distraught. “I’m here to help. I’m&nbsp;
The Specter, defender of the innocent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:43.859 --> 02:17:49.259
scourge of the evildoer. You can’t lock&nbsp;
me away! You can’t! The people need me!”

02:17:49.260 --> 02:17:53.940
As tragic as it is to see a genuinely&nbsp;
benevolent anomaly beg for his freedom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:53.939 --> 02:17:59.399
the Mobile Task Force’s hearts have long since&nbsp;
hardened to this kind of thing. He wouldn’t be&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:59.399 --> 02:18:04.620
the first sapient humanoid anomaly to speedrun&nbsp;
all seven stages of grief on their knees in front&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:04.620 --> 02:18:10.080
of them, and he probably wouldn’t be the last.&nbsp;
Foundation protocol is to tag and bag either way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:10.080 --> 02:18:14.460
even if they’d just used their anomalous&nbsp;
abilities to thwart a bank robbery.

02:18:14.460 --> 02:18:19.740
The Specter is hauled back to the nearest&nbsp;
Foundation Containment Site and held in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:19.740 --> 02:18:25.679
holding cell while researchers prepare to conduct&nbsp;
initial tests and questioning. Everything seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:25.680 --> 02:18:31.201
so run of the mill - Little do they notice,&nbsp;
something extremely strange is starting to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:31.200 --> 02:18:36.840
happen to New York City beyond the containment&nbsp;
chamber’s walls. They’re about to learn the true,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:36.840 --> 02:18:42.840
devastating consequences of keeping a pure&nbsp;
force of good like The Specter locked up.

02:18:42.840 --> 02:18:47.520
Like a switch has been flipped, nearby&nbsp;
police suddenly become oddly listless.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:47.520 --> 02:18:52.081
Instead of working, they just chat with each&nbsp;
other and random citizens about the weather.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:52.080 --> 02:18:58.080
Some watch SCP Explained on their phone while&nbsp;
active robberies are happening. A man is mugged&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:58.080 --> 02:19:03.540
right in front of a squad car and the cop inside&nbsp;
just listens to his Van Halen CDs at max volume.

02:19:03.540 --> 02:19:09.600
In actual precincts, the on-duty cops start to&nbsp;
wonder why on earth they’re actually here. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:09.600 --> 02:19:13.800
this building, all together, listening to&nbsp;
phone calls from weirdly panicked people&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:13.800 --> 02:19:20.340
and typing up reports on… Stuff? It all&nbsp;
just seems oddly confusing. They decide&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:20.340 --> 02:19:25.440
to go outside and watch clouds, or go home&nbsp;
and play video games. It doesn’t make any&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:25.439 --> 02:19:29.700
sense that they’re just standing around&nbsp;
in here doing nothing, for no reason.

02:19:29.700 --> 02:19:35.519
Across the city, strange incidents start to&nbsp;
occur. People who so much as knock into each other&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:35.520 --> 02:19:40.020
while walking on the crowded New York streets&nbsp;
begin to brawl with one another on the ground,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:40.020 --> 02:19:43.920
while nobody around them even really&nbsp;
acknowledges that they’re doing it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:43.920 --> 02:19:48.300
let alone tries to break them up.&nbsp;
Nearby, cars crash at the intersection,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:48.300 --> 02:19:53.640
and rather than getting out to exchange insurance&nbsp;
information, the irate drivers draw guns and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:53.640 --> 02:19:59.161
start to duel like bit-characters in an old&nbsp;
cowboy movie. Nobody really seems to mind.

02:19:59.160 --> 02:20:04.200
As if noticing a change in the wind, organized&nbsp;
crime rackets decide that secrecy isn’t the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:04.200 --> 02:20:09.300
way to go anymore. Mob enforcers rob stores&nbsp;
and shake down random people in the streets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:09.300 --> 02:20:14.821
taking wallets, watches, phones, and jewelry.&nbsp;
Criminals break into people’s homes and start&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:14.820 --> 02:20:20.340
ransacking the place, stealing everything from&nbsp;
flat-screen TVs to priceless family heirlooms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:20.340 --> 02:20:25.920
while their owners sit on the couch, seemingly&nbsp;
unable to even comprehend that something is&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:25.920 --> 02:20:30.840
going wrong here. But everything is&nbsp;
still about to get a whole lot worse.

02:20:30.840 --> 02:20:36.780
The city breaks into riots and looting. People&nbsp;
battling in the streets. News helicopters circle&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:36.780 --> 02:20:43.500
above, reporting the chaos unfolding below them,&nbsp;
but they do so with an odd kind of detached calm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:43.500 --> 02:20:47.220
not really comprehending the full&nbsp;
scope of what’s happening or why.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:47.220 --> 02:20:52.380
Just that a whole lot more is on fire&nbsp;
now than it was before. How strange.

02:20:52.380 --> 02:20:58.260
This is the moment when the SCP Foundation&nbsp;
realizes the meaning of The Specter’s pleading.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:58.260 --> 02:21:04.500
He really was being entirely unselfish,&nbsp;
because you see, The Specter isn’t just&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:04.500 --> 02:21:10.380
an extremely devoted crime fighter, he’s the very&nbsp;
personification of the concept of fighting crime.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:10.380 --> 02:21:16.500
He can’t ever be locked up or contained, because&nbsp;
if you do so, then the very concept of resisting&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:16.500 --> 02:21:22.680
crime fades from the human consciousness.&nbsp;
Order ceases to be and chaos reigns.

02:21:22.680 --> 02:21:30.900
That’s why The Specter, also known as SCP-4494,&nbsp;
is classified as Archon - Meaning that he can’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:30.899 --> 02:21:34.920
be contained, because the danger of&nbsp;
containing him would be far greater&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:34.920 --> 02:21:39.060
than the danger of letting him roam&nbsp;
around and do his thing unhindered.

02:21:39.060 --> 02:21:44.281
He maintains an amicable relationship with the&nbsp;
SCP Foundation after everything that happened,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:44.280 --> 02:21:49.679
willing to swoop in and help their agents when&nbsp;
they’re in a bind, and need a little hand from a&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:49.680 --> 02:21:56.340
true hero. And when he’s not fighting crime, he&nbsp;
relaxes in The Specter Cave by watching TV and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:56.340 --> 02:22:02.880
playing video games. No, we’re not making that up.&nbsp;
Unsurprisingly, his favorite video games are ones&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:02.880 --> 02:22:07.620
where you get to play as superheroes, though he&nbsp;
will occasionally indulge in some Grand Theft Auto&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:07.620 --> 02:22:13.019
V - Just don’t expect him to break any traffic&nbsp;
laws while he’s playing, thank you very much.

02:22:13.020 --> 02:22:18.541
So if ever you feel afraid, or that the dark&nbsp;
forces that are all too human in this world are&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:18.540 --> 02:22:24.780
marshaling against you, know this: You don’t&nbsp;
fight alone. There’s a man in the shadows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:24.780 --> 02:22:30.960
ready to come to your aid. He’s a warrior&nbsp;
for justice. He’s a defender of the innocent.

02:22:30.960 --> 02:22:34.980
But most of all, he is… The Specter.

02:22:34.979 --> 02:22:39.000
Oh god, oh god, oh god. I’m trapped&nbsp;
in the Infinite Ikea! How long have&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:39.000 --> 02:22:44.280
I been here? How can I get out? How can I&nbsp;
survive the vicious Staff of the Infinite&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:44.280 --> 02:22:47.639
Ikea and work with the other survivors&nbsp;
in this terrifying, endless building?

02:22:47.640 --> 02:22:50.220
Okay. Let’s start from the beginning.

02:22:50.220 --> 02:22:54.120
Day 1. I’d come to this flat-pack&nbsp;
nightmare with my lovely wife,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:54.120 --> 02:22:58.859
Brenda, to pick up some stupid sofa she&nbsp;
saw online. We could have ordered it in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:58.859 --> 02:23:03.780
but me, being a cheapskate and a fool,&nbsp;
instead decided it’d be a dandy idea to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:03.780 --> 02:23:08.639
head in and pick it up ourselves - even though&nbsp;
I can’t stand shopping in these giant stores.

02:23:08.640 --> 02:23:12.900
Of course, it didn’t take long for us to&nbsp;
get separated. I was wandering around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:12.899 --> 02:23:18.719
just pretending I knew what I was doing.&nbsp;
Surrounded by unfamiliar people. Then… Not&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:18.720 --> 02:23:24.120
surrounded by any people at all. Like the&nbsp;
complete doofus I was, I’d somehow gotten&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:24.120 --> 02:23:28.380
lost. Just needed to find my bearings again&nbsp;
and then I could call Brenda to come save me.

02:23:28.380 --> 02:23:34.140
But I never did find my bearings. The&nbsp;
hours went on, and I was still lost…

02:23:34.140 --> 02:23:39.781
Day 2. My dominant emotion on this day&nbsp;
was nothing more than sheer humiliation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:39.780 --> 02:23:44.759
knowing I’d been bested by a damn Swedish&nbsp;
furniture store. I spent the night before&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:44.760 --> 02:23:49.140
sleeping on a futon, wondering how I’d&nbsp;
gotten myself into this flat-pack calamity.

02:23:49.140 --> 02:23:53.760
I spent the day searching for food, my&nbsp;
confusion and exhaustion increasing by&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:53.760 --> 02:23:56.760
the moment. For a while, I even&nbsp;
entertained the idea I might have&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:56.760 --> 02:24:00.720
died and gone to some Nordic hell.&nbsp;
That night, I went to bed hungry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:00.720 --> 02:24:05.520
knowing that if I didn’t eat soon, I might&nbsp;
be found as a skeleton on a dusty old futon.

02:24:06.540 --> 02:24:09.359
It can’t end like this. I can’t die on day 2…

02:24:09.359 --> 02:24:14.880
Day 3. I continued my journey through&nbsp;
the labyrinthine bowels of the Ikea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:14.880 --> 02:24:19.500
disoriented by the endlessly iterating&nbsp;
collections of cheap furniture. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:19.500 --> 02:24:24.120
was something terrifying about the emptiness&nbsp;
of it all, this affordable but impossible to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:24.120 --> 02:24:28.380
assemble void. Starvation has always been&nbsp;
one of the most horrific deaths, hasn’t it?

02:24:28.380 --> 02:24:32.460
You could only imagine my relief when I saw&nbsp;
the figure standing a few feet in front of me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:32.460 --> 02:24:36.780
dressed like a member of Ikea staff.&nbsp;
I’d found salvation! I’d found someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:36.780 --> 02:24:41.399
who could help me out of here! But when&nbsp;
I approached, I realized something was&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:41.399 --> 02:24:47.219
horribly wrong: This wasn’t a human being&nbsp;
standing before me, it was a monster. A&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:47.220 --> 02:24:51.660
being I’d later come to know is called The&nbsp;
Staff by the many people who fear them.

02:24:51.660 --> 02:24:56.220
It chased me, repeating, “The store is&nbsp;
closed. You need to vacate the premises”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:56.220 --> 02:25:01.440
flailing for me with its long, frightening&nbsp;
limbs. I only survived Day 3 because I&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:01.439 --> 02:25:04.919
locked myself in a closet and just&nbsp;
waited while it hammered against the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:04.920 --> 02:25:09.600
wood with its fists. Once the “night” was&nbsp;
over, it left, and I was able to escape.

02:25:09.600 --> 02:25:15.180
Day 4. I was feeling some intense hunger&nbsp;
pangs on this day, not to mention the fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:15.180 --> 02:25:19.680
that I now knew there were monsters out there,&nbsp;
just waiting to beat me to death if they caught&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:19.680 --> 02:25:24.000
me when the lights turned down. Needless&nbsp;
to say, I wasn’t in the best headspace,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:24.000 --> 02:25:28.859
and I didn’t have enough charge on my phone&nbsp;
to justify opening up my meditation app.

02:25:28.859 --> 02:25:35.099
Then… I found Nirvana. I found the&nbsp;
cafeteria, stocked full of delicious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:35.100 --> 02:25:41.220
warm Swedish meatballs. No food had&nbsp;
ever tasted so sweet to me. And this&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:41.220 --> 02:25:46.080
delicious meal also gave way to one of the&nbsp;
most exciting new developments: Gloria.

02:25:46.080 --> 02:25:50.519
Gloria was a veteran. She taught me&nbsp;
everything I needed to know at this place.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:50.520 --> 02:25:54.900
Even on the first day I met her, she felt like&nbsp;
someone I’d known for years. It was her that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:54.899 --> 02:25:59.700
took me back to her home in this place: A little&nbsp;
fortress made of Ikea furniture, filled with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:59.700 --> 02:26:04.260
whole community of other people trapped in there.&nbsp;
It was like being allowed into the Garden of Eden.

02:26:04.260 --> 02:26:08.160
Day 5, and it feels good to be alive.&nbsp;
I met up with all the different people&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:08.160 --> 02:26:13.500
around the camp. They tell the most bizarre&nbsp;
and fascinating stories. This sounds crazy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:13.500 --> 02:26:18.720
I know, but I get the sense not all of them&nbsp;
came from the same place as me. Different&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:18.720 --> 02:26:25.920
Ikeas in different countries, or maybe even -&nbsp;
as nutty as it sounds - from different worlds.

02:26:25.920 --> 02:26:31.020
One guy, Tony, who’s been trapped in here for&nbsp;
a year and change, and we got talking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:31.020 --> 02:26:35.100
different vacations we’d taken on the outside.&nbsp;
He told me he was from New York, and I told him&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:35.100 --> 02:26:39.720
I visited there once, and I loved going to see&nbsp;
the Statue of Liberty up close. That’s when he&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:39.720 --> 02:26:44.820
told me that he’d never heard of the Statue of&nbsp;
Liberty. I didn’t know what to make of that.

02:26:44.819 --> 02:26:49.439
Strange little details aside, I couldn’t be&nbsp;
happier to be there with other people. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:49.439 --> 02:26:52.259
next step would be finding a way&nbsp;
out of here, and back to Brenda.

02:26:52.260 --> 02:26:57.660
Day 6. Gloria and several others led me out on&nbsp;
our first excursion - Missions where the goal&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:57.660 --> 02:27:01.260
was to collect more food and supplies,&nbsp;
and map the surrounding area. I nearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:27:01.260 --> 02:27:04.620
jumped out of my skin when I saw a Staff&nbsp;
member standing in our path at one point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:27:04.620 --> 02:27:09.660
and the others all just laughed at me. The&nbsp;
Staff member just stood there, placid and still.

02:27:09.660 --> 02:27:14.760
Gloria told me that it’s okay, the Staff are&nbsp;
harmless during the day. It’s only nighttime&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:27:14.760 --> 02:27:18.900
when they enter their pattern of aggression. So&nbsp;
as long as you don’t get lost during the daytime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:27:18.899 --> 02:27:22.799
you’re generally fine. The team&nbsp;
often used string, like Theseus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:27:22.800 --> 02:27:27.060
to trail behind them and ensure they don’t get&nbsp;
lost. It was clear they’d been here long enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:27:27.060 --> 02:27:31.140
to work out systems for every possibility.&nbsp;
Felt like I was in good hands with them.

02:27:31.140 --> 02:27:34.920
We collected meatballs and some rugs&nbsp;
to fortify the walls and headed back.

02:27:34.920 --> 02:27:41.100
Day 7. This was the first night that we had&nbsp;
to fend off a full-on attack. Those monsters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:27:41.100 --> 02:27:46.800
the Staff, came at us in huge groups, pounding&nbsp;
at the outside of our perimeter with their&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:27:46.800 --> 02:27:51.600
balled fists. It was terrifying. As a way&nbsp;
of fighting them off, we tied Ikea kitchen&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:27:51.600 --> 02:27:56.460
knives to the end of curtain rails and speared&nbsp;
them, one by one, until all of them were dead.

02:27:56.460 --> 02:28:01.560
But they just kept coming. More and&nbsp;
more and more of them. When it looked&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:01.560 --> 02:28:04.020
like one of the walls to the north&nbsp;
of the community was going to fall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:04.020 --> 02:28:07.980
everyone around me started to panic. That’s&nbsp;
when Barry, one of the biggest men in the camp,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:07.979 --> 02:28:12.179
grabbed a hammer in each hand and&nbsp;
when outside. He fought like a beast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:12.180 --> 02:28:17.520
taking on Staff member after Staff member,&nbsp;
tanking hit after hit. It was something to see.

02:28:17.520 --> 02:28:19.920
That’s when he took off into&nbsp;
the depths of the store,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:19.920 --> 02:28:24.720
drawing the Staff away behind him and saving&nbsp;
us all. We never saw Barry again after that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:24.720 --> 02:28:29.580
but he’s the reason all of us made it past&nbsp;
day 7. Thank you, Barry, wherever you are.

02:28:29.580 --> 02:28:35.519
Day 8. Gloria took me out alone today, on another&nbsp;
search for the escape. That’s when she told me&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:35.520 --> 02:28:39.541
about her sister: She’d gone shopping in&nbsp;
Ikea with her well over a year ago now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:39.540 --> 02:28:44.640
when she was separated and got lost in here,&nbsp;
just like me. I had a lot in common with her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:44.640 --> 02:28:49.800
including my feelings of guilt for abandoning&nbsp;
my loved ones and my drive to escape and be&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:49.800 --> 02:28:54.780
united with them. While we were out that day,&nbsp;
we didn’t find anything useful. Gloria seemed&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:54.780 --> 02:28:59.939
sad but unsurprised. The Infinite Ikea&nbsp;
had its way of slowly grinding you down.

02:28:59.939 --> 02:29:06.719
Days 9 to 17. Despite a rocky start, I was finding&nbsp;
my legs in the Infinite Ikea. I started to get to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:29:06.720 --> 02:29:11.580
know my fellow Ikea prisoners, I started to&nbsp;
understand and truly befriend them. We went&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:29:11.580 --> 02:29:15.720
on expeditions pretty much daily, either&nbsp;
to collect new food, more supplies to help&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:29:15.720 --> 02:29:20.040
build up our community, or to keep searching&nbsp;
for an exit. To me, it started to feel like&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:29:20.040 --> 02:29:25.019
we were making progress, and that helped a&nbsp;
great deal to keep my emotions semi-stable.

02:29:25.020 --> 02:29:29.460
But it wasn’t the same for Gloria. After all,&nbsp;
she’d been here for so much longer than me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:29:29.460 --> 02:29:35.040
and she had her sister to consider on the outside.&nbsp;
To her, these routines I was becoming part of now&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:29:35.040 --> 02:29:39.840
felt like a prison within the prison. She&nbsp;
was trapped. Had her sister forgotten her&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:29:39.840 --> 02:29:44.040
out there? Had she been declared dead?&nbsp;
Were people even still looking for her?

02:29:44.040 --> 02:29:48.660
And it was on Day 18 that it all got&nbsp;
a little too much for poor Gloria.

02:29:48.660 --> 02:29:53.519
She’d snuck out of the camp at night, when the&nbsp;
staff were most active, trying to find the exit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:29:53.520 --> 02:29:59.520
Sadly, that’d cost Gloria her life. There’s no&nbsp;
way of knowing what happened to her exactly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:29:59.520 --> 02:30:03.000
but considering how bruised up&nbsp;
her body was when we found her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:30:03.000 --> 02:30:07.800
it was easy to make an educated guess: She’d&nbsp;
gotten bum-rushed by the Staff and beaten to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:30:07.800 --> 02:30:12.600
death before she could even muster up a defense.&nbsp;
It was a horrible day to go. We tried to give&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:30:12.600 --> 02:30:18.000
her as dignified a funeral as we could, given the&nbsp;
circumstances: Closing her up in a body-sized box.

02:30:18.000 --> 02:30:23.220
That was the day I decided to stop just&nbsp;
trying to survive, and start trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:30:23.220 --> 02:30:28.260
escape. I owed that to Brenda, if she really&nbsp;
had gotten out. I couldn’t keep her waiting.

02:30:28.260 --> 02:30:32.040
But I wouldn’t be alone. As it&nbsp;
turns out, another two members&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:30:32.040 --> 02:30:35.939
of the little Ikea community I’d come to&nbsp;
know were willing to risk it all with me:&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:30:35.939 --> 02:30:40.740
A man named Kelvin and a young woman named&nbsp;
Vicky. They were sick of just waiting around&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:30:40.740 --> 02:30:44.939
and fending off attacks from the Staff,&nbsp;
night after night. They both told me they’d&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:30:44.939 --> 02:30:49.200
rather die during an escape attempt than&nbsp;
while cowering under a pile of cheap rugs.

02:30:49.200 --> 02:30:54.000
And so, each armed with claw hammers&nbsp;
from the Six-Piece Ikea Fixa tool kit&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:30:54.000 --> 02:30:57.660
and as many Pruta Tupperware containers&nbsp;
full of meatballs as we could carry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:30:57.660 --> 02:31:00.899
we set off into the great unknown… of Ikea.

02:31:00.899 --> 02:31:06.059
We traveled for weeks, marking out tracks on&nbsp;
the ground with the MÅLA mixed-colors chalk&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:31:06.060 --> 02:31:10.140
selection so we never got caught going&nbsp;
in circles. One day bled into the next:&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:31:10.140 --> 02:31:14.220
Nights were spent trying to hide&nbsp;
in closets and bathtub while the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:31:14.220 --> 02:31:19.741
Staff hunted relentlessly for people just&nbsp;
like us. Every single time, we got lucky.

02:31:20.580 --> 02:31:22.260
Until day 41.

02:31:22.260 --> 02:31:26.340
Here’s something you need to know about the&nbsp;
Infinite Ikea: You’re probably already aware,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:31:26.340 --> 02:31:30.840
if you’re watching this, that the 24-hour&nbsp;
cycle of night and day is dictated by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:31:30.840 --> 02:31:36.060
store lights up above. But the space between&nbsp;
day and night isn’t a gradient here, it’s a&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:31:36.060 --> 02:31:39.180
cliff. You can be minding your&nbsp;
own business, when suddenly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:31:39.180 --> 02:31:45.000
pitch darkness, and now the staff are on your&nbsp;
ass. That’s exactly what happened on Day 41.

02:31:45.000 --> 02:31:49.080
We were in the middle of a kitchenware section,&nbsp;
surrounded by a few docile members of staff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:31:49.080 --> 02:31:55.019
when suddenly, the lights switched off and they&nbsp;
went hostile on us. They look pretty goofy after&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:31:55.020 --> 02:31:59.880
the initial shock has worn off, but believe me&nbsp;
when I say that these monsters can really pack&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:31:59.880 --> 02:32:04.920
a mean wallop when they want to, and we received&nbsp;
a reminder of this unfortunate fact that night.

02:32:04.920 --> 02:32:10.740
The staff swarmed us, repeating that awful&nbsp;
phrase, “The store is closed. You need to vacate&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:32:10.740 --> 02:32:15.359
the premises”, while they struck and flailed at&nbsp;
us. If it wasn’t for our trusty claw hammers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:32:15.359 --> 02:32:18.780
we would have been dead that night.&nbsp;
Thankfully, we were able to give better&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:32:18.780 --> 02:32:23.040
than we were getting. We managed to kill a&nbsp;
decent number of staff members and then make&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:32:23.040 --> 02:32:28.500
a run for a section with better hiding places.&nbsp;
Myself, Vicky, and Kelvin all stowed away in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:32:28.500 --> 02:32:32.580
large wardrobe until we saw light filtering&nbsp;
through the crack in the door, like we were&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:32:32.580 --> 02:32:37.500
rejected extras for some painful community&nbsp;
theatre take on The Chronicles of Narnia.

02:32:37.500 --> 02:32:42.600
But while we survived that night, we didn’t&nbsp;
survive unscathed. My face was swollen from&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:32:42.600 --> 02:32:46.560
a nasty punch one of the staff members&nbsp;
dealt me, and from the pain in my chest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:32:46.560 --> 02:32:52.081
I might’ve been dealing with a few broken ribs.&nbsp;
Kelvin sprained his ankle during the escape, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:32:52.080 --> 02:32:55.859
Vicky had a cut on her forehead from when&nbsp;
one of the staff members kneed her in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:32:55.859 --> 02:33:00.000
face during the fray. You never win&nbsp;
these fights, you just survive them.

02:33:00.000 --> 02:33:03.540
We made a temporary camp in the area&nbsp;
where we could rest and recover,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:03.540 --> 02:33:07.800
as well as shaking off the justifiable&nbsp;
fear of death or grievous harm that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:07.800 --> 02:33:12.600
dampened our resolve to get out of&nbsp;
this place. That took us to day 53.

02:33:12.600 --> 02:33:17.281
Of course, food was always a concern. I don’t&nbsp;
want to romanticize what happened in there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:17.280 --> 02:33:21.179
as much as I’m sure someone on the outside&nbsp;
might want to imagine this whole experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:21.180 --> 02:33:26.760
as some kind of exciting, survival horror game.&nbsp;
But I assure you, it was less “survival horror”&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:26.760 --> 02:33:32.520
and more survival and horror. Meaning not only&nbsp;
are we suffering from constant fear, stress,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:32.520 --> 02:33:36.840
and paranoia for our safety in here, but we&nbsp;
also need to keep ourselves fed and watered.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:36.840 --> 02:33:41.220
You’re just as likely to die from starvation&nbsp;
in here as you are to be beaten to death.

02:33:41.220 --> 02:33:43.440
We went in search of another Ikea Kitchen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:43.439 --> 02:33:47.399
where we could fill up on more water and&nbsp;
meatballs - your lifeblood in a place&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:47.399 --> 02:33:52.379
like this. It took us several more days of&nbsp;
searching and hiding, searching and hiding&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:52.380 --> 02:33:58.380
before we hit paydirt. By the time we actually got&nbsp;
our hands on the food and water, we were starving&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:58.380 --> 02:34:03.780
and practically coughing up dust. Those meatballs&nbsp;
were the most delicious food I’ve ever tasted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:34:03.780 --> 02:34:08.040
and I could tell from their faces that&nbsp;
Kelvin and Vicky felt exactly the same way.

02:34:08.040 --> 02:34:13.740
Though at that moment, I told myself,&nbsp;
if - no, when - I get out of here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:34:13.740 --> 02:34:20.399
I’d never eat another meatball. It’d probably give&nbsp;
me war flashbacks - or, I guess, store flashbacks.

02:34:20.399 --> 02:34:24.960
We filled up our Tupperware and shoved them&nbsp;
back into our Ikea Pivring backpacks. Then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:34:24.960 --> 02:34:29.280
we needed to keep moving, keep searching,&nbsp;
and keep marking the ground behind us as&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:34:29.280 --> 02:34:34.679
we fanned out into the great flat-pack yonder,&nbsp;
avoiding confrontations whenever we could. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:34:34.680 --> 02:34:39.420
three of us still had no idea what insanity was&nbsp;
waiting for us out there. We had no idea that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:34:39.420 --> 02:34:43.020
there were even more dangerous things&nbsp;
than the Staff lurking in the shadows.

02:34:43.020 --> 02:34:47.880
Day 68. Of course, I kept count, writing&nbsp;
it down in my on the back of a JÄTTELIK&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:34:47.880 --> 02:34:51.180
coloring book. Trust me, when every&nbsp;
night could mean a horrible death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:34:51.180 --> 02:34:57.060
you keep track of the nights. Not a single&nbsp;
one of them escapes you. At a certain point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:34:57.060 --> 02:35:02.400
I think we all adapted, in our own way. It&nbsp;
was back to caveman times again, learning&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:35:02.399 --> 02:35:07.200
to be like our primal ancestors, hiding away&nbsp;
from the dark and the monsters that hid there.

02:35:07.200 --> 02:35:12.660
So it was extremely surprising for us to&nbsp;
get the most brutal attack during the day.

02:35:12.660 --> 02:35:17.040
At first, I thought we were being attacked by&nbsp;
the staff during daylight hours, like a bolt&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:35:17.040 --> 02:35:22.200
from the blue. That’s when we noticed they weren’t&nbsp;
attacking with their hands: They were all holding&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:35:22.200 --> 02:35:27.599
kitchen knives, holding us up like bandits. That’s&nbsp;
when we realized what had actually happened here:&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:35:27.600 --> 02:35:32.701
We weren’t being attacked by the staff, we&nbsp;
were being attacked by other humans dressed&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:35:32.700 --> 02:35:37.439
like the staff, wearing their hollowed-out&nbsp;
heads like grisly masks. They told us that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:35:37.439 --> 02:35:42.179
we were coming with them, and if we resisted,&nbsp;
they’d cut us to ribbons. And seeing as none&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:35:42.180 --> 02:35:47.100
of us were movie action heroes, we thought&nbsp;
it’d be best to do exactly what they said.

02:35:47.100 --> 02:35:51.781
This was how we fell into the&nbsp;
clutches of Generalissimo Vardagen.

02:35:51.780 --> 02:36:00.300
Day 69, but things were not nice. I&nbsp;
don’t know if I mentioned this before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:36:00.300 --> 02:36:04.680
but the community I became a part of in the&nbsp;
Infinite Ikea after meeting Gloria was just&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:36:04.680 --> 02:36:09.060
one of many. Nobody knows exactly how many&nbsp;
people are trapped in here. I’m hardly a&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:36:09.060 --> 02:36:13.500
martyr for spending 68 days in there, I know&nbsp;
people who’ve been trapped in there for years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:36:13.500 --> 02:36:17.399
who’ve given up all hope of escape&nbsp;
and accepted their lot in life. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:36:17.399 --> 02:36:20.759
became the elders of a lot of these&nbsp;
communities, helping others adjust.

02:36:20.760 --> 02:36:25.440
Though of course, Generalissimo&nbsp;
Vardagen was not one of these people.

02:36:25.439 --> 02:36:30.419
Myself, Vicky, and Kelvin were dragged by the&nbsp;
strangers dressed as staff members into a fort&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:36:30.420 --> 02:36:36.240
made of smashed-up wood, nailed together into&nbsp;
a huge, ominous structure. It was a far more&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:36:36.240 --> 02:36:41.040
extensive structure than any of the communities&nbsp;
I’d visited or even heard about before in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:36:41.040 --> 02:36:46.740
Infinite Ikea. It was a true fortress, guarded&nbsp;
by many more of those knife-wielding people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:36:46.740 --> 02:36:51.059
dressed in the clothes and the flesh&nbsp;
of the staff. They looked like some&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:36:51.060 --> 02:36:55.680
evil cult straight out of a damn horror&nbsp;
movie; I’d never seen anything like it.

02:36:55.680 --> 02:36:58.620
We were dragged into a kind of&nbsp;
tent in the middle of the camp,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:36:58.620 --> 02:37:03.899
made out of stitched-together rugs.&nbsp;
That’s where we met Generlissimo Vardagen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:37:03.899 --> 02:37:08.280
surrounded by his guards. I’d later learn&nbsp;
his namesake was a set of steak knives&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:37:08.280 --> 02:37:12.719
stocked in the Ikea kitchenware sections -&nbsp;
similar to the knives being wielded by his&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:37:12.720 --> 02:37:18.060
gallery of goons. The Generalissimo himself&nbsp;
was dressed like some absurd tinpot dictator,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:37:18.060 --> 02:37:24.121
wearing a silly hat and a jacket covered in fake&nbsp;
medals. His whole presence felt like a cosmic&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:37:24.120 --> 02:37:28.319
punishment for daring to believe things couldn’t&nbsp;
get any more absurd than they already were.

02:37:28.319 --> 02:37:33.120
His men forced us down onto our knees,&nbsp;
and Vardagen cleared his throat to speak.&nbsp;

02:37:33.120 --> 02:37:41.880
“Quake in fear at the sight of this Ikea’s ruler,&nbsp;
the great and powerful Generalissimo Vardagen. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:37:41.880 --> 02:37:47.520
have united kingdoms from the gardening and&nbsp;
bathroom supplies departments, and crushed&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:37:47.520 --> 02:37:55.680
dissenting tribes in the office furniture section&nbsp;
to the West. If you wish to live, you will swear&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:37:55.680 --> 02:38:02.100
fealty to me and join my legion of servants.&nbsp;
If you do as I say, you will be given safety&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:38:02.100 --> 02:38:10.500
and security from the staff come nightfall. If you&nbsp;
do not bend the knee, I will have you destroyed!”

02:38:10.500 --> 02:38:15.420
By the time his little spiel was done, the man&nbsp;
was red in the face and sweating profusely. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:38:15.420 --> 02:38:19.140
was clear that, much like your average Ikea&nbsp;
shelving unit after a couple weeks of use,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:38:19.140 --> 02:38:24.781
the great Generalissimo Vardagen had a few&nbsp;
screws loose. None of us liked the idea of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:38:24.780 --> 02:38:30.599
becoming slaves to some flat-pack ghenghis khan,&nbsp;
so we tried to persuade him to just let us go,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:38:30.600 --> 02:38:35.161
telling him that we hoped to escape the store,&nbsp;
and with our help, he could escape, too.

02:38:35.160 --> 02:38:40.920
That’s when we learned a sobering lesson:&nbsp;
For some people, life inside the Infinite&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:38:40.920 --> 02:38:45.480
Ikea was better than life outside. In the&nbsp;
real world, the Generalissimo had been a&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:38:45.479 --> 02:38:50.700
twice-divorced ex-salaryman with nothing to&nbsp;
his name but debts and regrets. But here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:38:50.700 --> 02:38:55.800
he was a demi-god. A leader among the&nbsp;
rest of us mere mortals. Why would he&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:38:55.800 --> 02:39:00.300
ever want to go back to the world that had&nbsp;
given him nothing and taken everything?

02:39:00.300 --> 02:39:06.420
Day 70 to Day 84. We were forced into weeks&nbsp;
of hard labor after that, toiling under the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:39:06.420 --> 02:39:11.640
Generalissimo and his gang of brigands. The&nbsp;
soldiers worked us like dogs, making us carry&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:39:11.640 --> 02:39:17.100
food and furniture back to the Generalissimo’s&nbsp;
Scandanavian furniture fortress. One day bled into&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:39:17.100 --> 02:39:21.900
the next. The best I could say about any of this&nbsp;
was that at least we were safe behind the walls of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:39:21.899 --> 02:39:26.160
the fortress at night, so we didn’t need to worry&nbsp;
about getting murdered by the staff in our sleep.

02:39:26.160 --> 02:39:29.819
However, tragedy struck again on day 85.

02:39:29.819 --> 02:39:36.059
Kelvin couldn’t take the work anymore. One day, I&nbsp;
think his mind just snapped. He refused to follow&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:39:36.060 --> 02:39:41.161
orders from the Generalissimo and his lieutenants,&nbsp;
even when they threatened him with death. Sadly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:39:41.160 --> 02:39:46.139
that night they would prove that this wasn’t just&nbsp;
some empty threat. When night fell, they tied up&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:39:46.140 --> 02:39:50.820
Kelvin’s arms and legs and left him outside&nbsp;
the fortress. We were just forced to watch as&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:39:50.819 --> 02:39:55.920
the staff assembled and beat our friend to death&nbsp;
while he lay there, unable to defend himself. Even&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:39:55.920 --> 02:40:01.380
in all the time I’d spent in the Infinite Ikea,&nbsp;
that was the most harrowing thing I’d ever seen.

02:40:01.380 --> 02:40:04.740
But in one of the few acts of&nbsp;
righteous cosmic justice that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:40:04.740 --> 02:40:07.679
we’d seen since being trapped&nbsp;
in here, all those months ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:40:07.680 --> 02:40:13.680
just a few days after Kelvin’s brutal&nbsp;
execution. Day 90. The day of the revolution.

02:40:13.680 --> 02:40:18.300
While I’d love to tell you I started&nbsp;
this, there are no heroes in this story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:40:18.300 --> 02:40:22.440
I just happen to be the one telling you&nbsp;
about it. Some internal conflict between the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:40:22.439 --> 02:40:27.120
Generalissimo and his men boiled over into&nbsp;
a kind of civil war that tore the fortress&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:40:27.120 --> 02:40:32.040
apart from within. Vicky and I escaped, but&nbsp;
in different directions. I like to imagine&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:40:32.040 --> 02:40:36.000
she got out in the end, it helps me sleep&nbsp;
at night. But one thing I will tell you:&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:40:36.000 --> 02:40:40.979
Generalissimo Vardagen found out what happened&nbsp;
to tyrants, big and small, when his closest&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:40:40.979 --> 02:40:45.419
confidantes gave him the Julias Caesar treatment&nbsp;
with the knives from which he took his name.

02:40:45.420 --> 02:40:48.899
I don’t think there’s anything wrong&nbsp;
in taking a little joy in that.

02:40:48.899 --> 02:40:53.639
For days afterwards, I just walked. I&nbsp;
felt so empty, but I refused to just&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:40:53.640 --> 02:40:58.201
lay down and let myself die. Even though so&nbsp;
much of the hope had been beaten out of me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:40:58.200 --> 02:41:03.599
I couldn’t betray Brenda by just giving up in&nbsp;
here. It wouldn’t be right. It just wouldn’t be&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:41:03.600 --> 02:41:10.980
right. The days ticked on, and nothing changed. I&nbsp;
had no food, no weapons. I was getting so tired.

02:41:10.979 --> 02:41:15.419
Then… night fell, and the Staff&nbsp;
started chasing me. They seemed&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:41:15.420 --> 02:41:19.380
even more aggressive than before. I&nbsp;
couldn’t fight; all I could do was run.

02:41:19.380 --> 02:41:24.300
I ran and I ran and I ran, not even looking&nbsp;
where I was going, as the crowd of staff&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:41:24.300 --> 02:41:29.760
started catching up with me. Getting closer and&nbsp;
closer. I ran until there was a doorway before me.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:41:29.760 --> 02:41:34.440
I didn’t even think, I was just trying to get&nbsp;
away. That’s when I noticed that the store’s roof&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:41:34.439 --> 02:41:39.960
was no longer above me. For the first time in one&nbsp;
hundred days, I was once again tasting fresh air.

02:41:39.960 --> 02:41:46.080
That’s right, folks. On day one hundred,&nbsp;
I was out. I’d truly made it out.

02:41:46.080 --> 02:41:51.540
This victory, however, was short-lived: A&nbsp;
group of about six staff members burst out&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:41:51.540 --> 02:41:56.100
of the front door behind me, charging towards&nbsp;
me with ferocious speed. I couldn’t move,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:41:56.100 --> 02:42:00.360
all I could do was grit my teeth and wince,&nbsp;
ready to accept my death at what had otherwise&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:42:00.359 --> 02:42:04.859
been the high point of my recent life.&nbsp;
What a depressing irony that would be.

02:42:04.859 --> 02:42:09.719
But instead, gunshots rang out through the air. A&nbsp;
hail of bullets cleaved through the staff members,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:42:09.720 --> 02:42:14.220
dropping them to the ground mere feet away from&nbsp;
me, stone dead. That’s when I turned to see a&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:42:14.220 --> 02:42:18.540
group of men with assault rifles and tactical&nbsp;
gear walking towards me. In any other situation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:42:18.540 --> 02:42:22.680
this might have been terrifying, but right&nbsp;
then, it was the happiest moment of my life.

02:42:22.680 --> 02:42:26.040
These men took me away from the&nbsp;
parking lot of what was now an&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:42:26.040 --> 02:42:30.120
abandoned Ikea. They told me they were&nbsp;
from a group called the SCP Foundation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:42:30.120 --> 02:42:34.260
and that I’d been declared missing for some&nbsp;
time now. I didn’t care about any of that:&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:42:34.260 --> 02:42:39.360
I just asked them if Brenda had gotten out. If she&nbsp;
hadn’t escaped too, then this was all for nothing.

02:42:39.359 --> 02:42:43.019
You can’t even imagine my relief when&nbsp;
they told me that Brenda was never even&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:42:43.020 --> 02:42:46.560
trapped. She was the one who reported my&nbsp;
disappearance after I’d dropped off the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:42:46.560 --> 02:42:50.220
face of the earth in what she thought was&nbsp;
a perfectly normal Ikea shopping session,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:42:50.220 --> 02:42:55.680
before that building was shut down and&nbsp;
cordoned off under some less-insane pretense.

02:42:55.680 --> 02:43:01.680
But Brenda was alive and safe, and I’d get to&nbsp;
be with her again. I have no shame in telling&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:43:01.680 --> 02:43:06.120
you I cried, but I’d like to specify&nbsp;
that they were absolutely tears of joy.

02:43:06.120 --> 02:43:11.040
The men from the SCP Foundation told me&nbsp;
that they’d give me medicine that’d help&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:43:11.040 --> 02:43:15.120
me forget all this after I told them&nbsp;
my story, and there’s no part of me&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:43:15.120 --> 02:43:19.500
that has a problem with that. Some things&nbsp;
are better left forgotten. But before the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:43:19.500 --> 02:43:24.000
SCP Foundation wipes it all from my mind&nbsp;
and I get to go and live with my beloved&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:43:24.000 --> 02:43:30.600
wife once more, this was how I survived&nbsp;
100 dreadful days in the Infinite Ikea.

02:43:30.600 --> 02:43:36.661
Agent Kister was running for his life. Every&nbsp;
breath had burned in his throat, like inhaling&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:43:36.660 --> 02:43:43.019
chlorine gas. He sped through the dark, surrounded&nbsp;
by deafening metallic screams that bounced off the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:43:43.020 --> 02:43:48.180
eternity of pipes scaling and strangling the&nbsp;
walls around him. As far as he was concerned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:43:48.180 --> 02:43:53.580
this was what Hell looked like. A nightmare&nbsp;
boiler room that would somehow be less scary&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:43:53.580 --> 02:43:58.080
if Freddy Kreuger was there, because at&nbsp;
least there’d be some kind of recognizably&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:43:58.080 --> 02:44:05.280
human intelligence to reason with. The&nbsp;
Pipes - SCP-015 - are truly unknowable.

02:44:05.280 --> 02:44:10.679
As an SCP Foundation Mobile Task Force&nbsp;
member, Agent Kister had seen some truly&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:44:10.680 --> 02:44:16.080
horrific things in the line of duty. He’d been&nbsp;
there during SCP-682 containment breaches,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:44:16.080 --> 02:44:20.939
firing an assault rifle at the beast as its&nbsp;
scales hardened into a bulletproof carapace.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:44:20.939 --> 02:44:26.819
He’d seen the femur breaker used on some poor,&nbsp;
godforsaken D-Class to lure Uncle Larry back into&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:44:26.819 --> 02:44:31.859
the containment chamber for some midnight fun.&nbsp;
In the most anxiety-inducing mission of his life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:44:31.859 --> 02:44:36.780
he’d once even bagged 096’s weeping face&nbsp;
after it slaughtered a whole mountain village.

02:44:36.780 --> 02:44:41.340
And these damn pipes were going&nbsp;
to be the thing that killed him.

02:44:41.340 --> 02:44:46.080
He turned a sharp corner, trying to block out&nbsp;
the screams of his teammates. Four of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:44:46.080 --> 02:44:51.720
had been sent in - The rest were already&nbsp;
dead, or worse. Stupid, stupid, stupid.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:44:51.720 --> 02:44:58.140
They’d made a classic military mistake:&nbsp;
Underestimating the capabilities of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:44:58.140 --> 02:45:03.781
enemy. And in Agent Kister’s experience,&nbsp;
this had always been a capital offense.

02:45:03.780 --> 02:45:09.780
All to install that machine. That stupid&nbsp;
little remote control reconnaissance&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:45:09.780 --> 02:45:12.599
vehicle. That’s what they’d given their lives for…

02:45:12.600 --> 02:45:16.800
Agent Kister tried to blink away the&nbsp;
memory of Agent Montgomery’s face.&nbsp;

02:45:16.800 --> 02:45:22.380
Monty was the youngest member of the team -&nbsp;
28 years old, his first major mission into&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:45:22.380 --> 02:45:26.640
the heart of a skip. Kister wanted to&nbsp;
remember him how he’d first seen him:&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:45:26.640 --> 02:45:31.440
A bright-eyed greenhorn, ready to protect&nbsp;
the human race from the horrors that lay&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:45:31.439 --> 02:45:35.171
in the dark. But he’d been the first&nbsp;
to go when everything got F.U.B.A.R.

02:45:36.660 --> 02:45:42.599
It was the smaller pipes that got Monty. Four&nbsp;
of them shot out, spearing his lungs and heart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:45:42.600 --> 02:45:48.241
pinning him in place. Even if they tried to cut&nbsp;
him out, he would have been a goner. He coughed&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:45:48.240 --> 02:45:54.479
up blood and went ghost-white faster than you’d&nbsp;
ever imagine. Kister would never forget, for what&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:45:54.479 --> 02:45:59.639
little time he had left to live, the horrible&nbsp;
sight of the light leaving that kid’s eyes.

02:45:59.640 --> 02:46:03.840
And all this because one of them had&nbsp;
tripped on one of the pipes on the way&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:46:03.840 --> 02:46:10.140
out and busted it. The mission was already&nbsp;
done. What an awful, pointless way to die.&nbsp;

02:46:10.140 --> 02:46:15.300
Kister was yanked from his aching thoughts&nbsp;
by a sudden obstacle in the way. A huge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:46:15.300 --> 02:46:19.260
towering pipe that looked like a&nbsp;
pillar of flesh, covered in bulbous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:46:19.260 --> 02:46:24.480
staring eyes. The thought crossed his mind, Jesus,&nbsp;
these pipes really can be made out of anything…

02:46:24.479 --> 02:46:28.859
He looked down and saw a series of rubber&nbsp;
pipes slithering towards his feet along&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:46:28.859 --> 02:46:34.380
the ground like snakes. It was time to run&nbsp;
again, but how long could you even run for&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:46:34.380 --> 02:46:38.460
when the very location around you wants&nbsp;
you dead? They should’ve sent the Mole&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:46:38.460 --> 02:46:42.660
Rats for a job like this. The infinitely&nbsp;
reconfiguring pipes changed the layout&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:46:42.660 --> 02:46:48.180
of the warehouse around him, making escape&nbsp;
seem all the more unfeasible. That’s when&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:46:48.180 --> 02:46:51.960
he started thinking about the terrible&nbsp;
thing that’d happened to Agent Greene…

02:46:51.960 --> 02:46:55.740
When 015 turned hostile, they&nbsp;
needed to make a run for it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:46:55.740 --> 02:47:01.019
fast. It wasn’t unlike being a pathogen inside&nbsp;
a living body - You start to make trouble,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:47:01.020 --> 02:47:05.940
and the body’s immune system is going to come&nbsp;
for you. And one of the most common mechanisms&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:47:05.939 --> 02:47:12.359
for a body fighting a virus is a fever - A sudden&nbsp;
increase in temperature that burns out the threat.

02:47:12.359 --> 02:47:17.580
The group turned and ran as the pipes started&nbsp;
twisting towards them. Agent Greene panicked&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:47:17.580 --> 02:47:21.359
and began striking at the pipes around&nbsp;
him with his hands. The first instinct&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:47:21.359 --> 02:47:27.059
would have been to shoot, but no guns were&nbsp;
permitted inside the SCP-015 warehouse. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:47:27.060 --> 02:47:31.741
reacted severely to any kind of tool being&nbsp;
brought into its proximity. Several dead&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:47:31.740 --> 02:47:36.300
agents and researchers could tell you&nbsp;
all about this if they hadn’t made that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:47:36.300 --> 02:47:41.640
same mistake. But when things were already&nbsp;
going sideways, it left them defenseless.

02:47:41.640 --> 02:47:48.480
Agent Greene was soon boxed in, caged by a&nbsp;
latticework of pipes in varying sizes. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:47:48.479 --> 02:47:53.280
tried desperately to push them out of the way and&nbsp;
make an escape route, but the hissing noise was&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:47:53.280 --> 02:47:59.399
getting louder and louder as the pipes started&nbsp;
to turn a glowing red around him. After all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:47:59.399 --> 02:48:04.620
when a body needs to deal with a hostile&nbsp;
foreign object, it burns out the threat.

02:48:04.620 --> 02:48:09.059
Kister wished he could shake the awful,&nbsp;
high-pitched wail that Agent Greene made when&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:48:09.060 --> 02:48:14.880
the heat inside his pipe cage became unbearable.&nbsp;
It was like seeing a human get caught inside a&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:48:14.880 --> 02:48:21.420
giant bug zapper. His skin went black and charred,&nbsp;
and soon after, the heat had risen to such an&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:48:21.420 --> 02:48:26.580
insane degree that Agent Greene was reduced&nbsp;
to little more than ash in his pipe furnace.

02:48:26.580 --> 02:48:31.500
But Kister couldn’t afford to dwell.&nbsp;
He kept running, vaulting over a long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:48:31.500 --> 02:48:37.620
ragged pipe made from stinking human hair.&nbsp;
This terrible place stank of motor oil, mold,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:48:37.620 --> 02:48:43.380
and death, But maybe those perceptions had been&nbsp;
colored by the terrible things he’d seen here.

02:48:43.380 --> 02:48:48.359
There it was again. That awful choked&nbsp;
screaming coming from inside the pipes.&nbsp;

02:48:48.359 --> 02:48:54.120
Agent Boggs. It was a nightmare to hear him shriek&nbsp;
like that. Boggs had been one of the toughest men&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:48:54.120 --> 02:48:59.760
that Kister had ever met - He mentored him, back&nbsp;
when he first became an MTF Member. Having Boggs&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:48:59.760 --> 02:49:05.640
on the team meant experience. It meant safety.&nbsp;
It meant that things would go okay. Kister had&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:49:05.640 --> 02:49:11.340
seen Boggs stare down some of the most horrifying&nbsp;
skips imaginable and not even flinch. And yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:49:11.340 --> 02:49:15.840
here he was, screaming and bawling&nbsp;
like a hurt child in the pipes.

02:49:15.840 --> 02:49:20.040
Boggs was the next one to get taken.&nbsp;
As he and Kister ran through the dark,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:49:20.040 --> 02:49:25.979
trying to find a way out, they’d passed a huge&nbsp;
pipe made of a soft, foam-like substance. A&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:49:25.979 --> 02:49:30.299
seam in the side of the pipe had yawned&nbsp;
open, and a tangle of writhing tentacles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:49:30.300 --> 02:49:36.000
each one barbed with thorns like the stem of a&nbsp;
rosebush. They enveloped Agent Boggs, cutting&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:49:36.000 --> 02:49:42.960
into his weathered skin. He gasped in pain, unable&nbsp;
to scream. It happened so fast. Before Kister even&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:49:42.960 --> 02:49:47.700
had a chance to grab him, he was pulled into&nbsp;
the pipe and the crevice sealed behind him.

02:49:47.700 --> 02:49:51.479
And that was when the screaming&nbsp;
really started for poor Agent Boggs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:49:52.080 --> 02:49:56.040
Kister hoped, for his sake,&nbsp;
that he would die sometime soon.

02:49:56.040 --> 02:50:01.260
Shrouded in the fog of his dark thoughts,&nbsp;
Agent Kister turned a corner and felt cold,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:50:01.260 --> 02:50:06.360
hard metal suddenly collide with his face,&nbsp;
breaking his nose with an unpleasant crunch.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:50:06.359 --> 02:50:11.219
He’d been clotheslined by a low pipe&nbsp;
that hadn’t been there before, and now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:50:11.220 --> 02:50:17.220
he was laid out on his back, face humming with&nbsp;
pain. Thoughts and feelings swam. He looked up&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:50:17.220 --> 02:50:22.260
into the seemingly infinite web of pipes weaving&nbsp;
through the air above him. He was right earlier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:50:22.260 --> 02:50:27.240
he just knew it. The place was Hell.&nbsp;
It’d be his eternal resting place.

02:50:27.240 --> 02:50:33.240
Suddenly, a new, huge shape moved into&nbsp;
the space above him. A pipe of ancient,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:50:33.240 --> 02:50:38.639
rusty iron that looked older than even the&nbsp;
warehouse holding this whole mess. Right&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:50:38.640 --> 02:50:44.281
above him, there was a huge valve fixed into&nbsp;
the pipe’s belly. He watched, helpless, as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:50:44.280 --> 02:50:49.800
valve opened itself with a squeal, and a huge,&nbsp;
dark mass came pouring out of it onto his body.

02:50:49.800 --> 02:50:57.480
A sudden weight. A sudden warmth. Sound and&nbsp;
movement and tiny scratching claws. Rats.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:50:57.479 --> 02:51:03.419
Thousands of big, hungry, black rats with&nbsp;
gnarled yellow teeth, clumps of fur purged&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:51:03.420 --> 02:51:09.660
from their skin by mange. Big, ropey, worm-like&nbsp;
tails swaying and whipping at the air. Who knows&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:51:09.660 --> 02:51:14.340
how long they’d been in that pipe? Who knows&nbsp;
when these rats had eaten anything that wasn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:51:14.340 --> 02:51:20.880
their brothers and sisters? And here was Agent&nbsp;
Kister, given unto them by the glorious pipes.&nbsp;

02:51:20.880 --> 02:51:26.520
The last thing he ever saw were those nasty&nbsp;
yellow teeth, because they ate his eyes first.

02:51:26.520 --> 02:51:30.720
Word of the four disappeared - and&nbsp;
presumed dead - operatives soon&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:51:30.720 --> 02:51:35.340
reached Dr. Charles Ogden Gears,&nbsp;
the legendarily taciturn senior&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:51:35.340 --> 02:51:40.500
researcher that managed the SCP-015&nbsp;
project, among a myriad of others.

02:51:40.500 --> 02:51:45.240
His face didn’t betray a flicker of emotion&nbsp;
when he was informed about the men lost to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:51:45.240 --> 02:51:50.819
SCP-015. It wasn’t that he didn’t feel&nbsp;
for them, more that he deemed it both&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:51:50.819 --> 02:51:55.620
unproductive and unprofessional to dwell&nbsp;
on the deaths of personnel when carrying&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:51:55.620 --> 02:52:01.439
out their duties. After all, being killed by&nbsp;
an anomaly was simply an occupational hazard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:52:01.439 --> 02:52:06.299
and a generous stipend would be sent to their&nbsp;
families to compensate for their tragic loss.

02:52:06.300 --> 02:52:10.140
Dr. Gears simply asked, “Was the&nbsp;
mission successful, in spite of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:52:10.140 --> 02:52:14.880
the casualties? Did the team manage to install&nbsp;
the Modular Robotic Vehicle at the epicenter?”

02:52:14.880 --> 02:52:18.180
He was told that the MRV had&nbsp;
been successfully installed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:52:18.180 --> 02:52:22.741
at least. Dr. Gears nodded and began&nbsp;
scheduling a second exploratory mission&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:52:22.740 --> 02:52:25.979
for a few months in the future to&nbsp;
check the status of the machine.

02:52:25.979 --> 02:52:32.939
Still, in the meantime, the MRV would chug&nbsp;
along, doing what humans couldn’t - or perhaps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:52:32.939 --> 02:52:38.759
shouldn’t - do within the nightmarish mess&nbsp;
of ever-growing pipes. It would roll through,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:52:38.760 --> 02:52:44.640
collecting information on everything around it,&nbsp;
hopefully giving the SCP Foundation an inroad&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:52:44.640 --> 02:52:49.920
to mapping the whole thing without needing to&nbsp;
send a cavalcade of staff into the danger zone&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:52:49.920 --> 02:52:56.040
just to discover more about these peculiar pipes.&nbsp;
Sure, some lives were lost getting it in there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:52:56.040 --> 02:52:59.760
but this measure would likely save&nbsp;
even more lives in the long run.

02:52:59.760 --> 02:53:05.880
That was… Until something went wrong.&nbsp;
The exact nature of what had happened&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:53:05.880 --> 02:53:11.940
to the MRV inside the domain of the pipes was a&nbsp;
mystery, but it wasn’t doing its intended job,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:53:11.939 --> 02:53:18.179
and the SCP Foundation needed to find out why.&nbsp;
The second exploratory mission was required&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:53:18.180 --> 02:53:24.120
much sooner than they’d initially anticipated,&nbsp;
with a dual purpose: Collecting the readings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:53:24.120 --> 02:53:28.559
and finding out what exactly had&nbsp;
happened to the MRV in the first place.

02:53:28.560 --> 02:53:34.440
He included a more specific set of instructions&nbsp;
for the next mission: Only three members of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:53:34.439 --> 02:53:41.580
personnel to enter SCP-015 this time, to minimize&nbsp;
potential loss. One trained technician to collect&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:53:41.580 --> 02:53:46.740
and read the diagnostics, and two guards to&nbsp;
maintain discipline and safety under pressure.

02:53:46.740 --> 02:53:50.460
With a setup like that, what&nbsp;
could possibly go wrong?

02:53:50.460 --> 02:53:56.520
Dr. Gears’ various assistants began headhunting&nbsp;
the perfect team for the job. Junior Researcher&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:53:56.520 --> 02:54:01.741
Lon, wanting to prove herself and climb up the&nbsp;
ranks, put her name forward. She was young,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:54:01.740 --> 02:54:07.019
intelligent, and had the initiative to&nbsp;
get the job done. Though there was one&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:54:07.020 --> 02:54:11.161
thing that she hadn’t disclosed when she was&nbsp;
applying for this important new position:&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:54:11.160 --> 02:54:17.760
Junior Researcher Lon suffered from&nbsp;
claustrophobia, the fear of enclosed spaces.

02:54:17.760 --> 02:54:22.500
Then the hunt was on for the two members of&nbsp;
Foundation military personnel who’d escort&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:54:22.500 --> 02:54:27.060
Lon to the MRV to take the readings.&nbsp;
Like the two priests in The Exorcist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:54:27.060 --> 02:54:32.581
they decided to recruit one grizzled,&nbsp;
experienced operative and one extremely&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:54:32.580 --> 02:54:36.779
cautious young buck straight out of&nbsp;
the Foundation MTF Training Academy.

02:54:36.779 --> 02:54:42.779
The younger of the two operatives, codenamed&nbsp;
Agent Two, was careful and conscientious to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:54:42.779 --> 02:54:48.779
a fault. He did things by the book or not at&nbsp;
all. The older operative, codenamed Agent Six,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:54:48.779 --> 02:54:53.519
was quite the opposite. He’d done this job&nbsp;
long enough to operate solely on instinct,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:54:53.520 --> 02:54:57.960
and resented his employers for expecting&nbsp;
him to approach his missions any other way.

02:54:57.960 --> 02:55:04.380
Lon, Agent Two, and Agent Six. The team&nbsp;
was assembled, and when the time came,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:55:04.380 --> 02:55:10.560
they were taken to SCP-015 to execute their&nbsp;
mission. It should have been simple enough:&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:55:10.560 --> 02:55:17.100
Get in, collect the readings, and get back out.&nbsp;
Though a few weeks earlier, the same thing would&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:55:17.100 --> 02:55:21.661
have been said for the mission to install&nbsp;
the MRV which left for Mobile Task Force&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:55:21.660 --> 02:55:28.859
operatives dead or worse. Nothing was ever as&nbsp;
simple as it seemed when it came to SCP-015.

02:55:28.859 --> 02:55:34.200
They were only allowed flashlights inside.&nbsp;
Anything else carried a substantial risk&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:55:34.200 --> 02:55:39.779
of activating SCP-015’s defensive state,&nbsp;
putting all of their lives in jeopardy.

02:55:39.779 --> 02:55:45.420
Lon had tried to keep things light, masking&nbsp;
her own terrible fear at this strange new&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:55:45.420 --> 02:55:49.680
situation. She’d joked that perhaps the&nbsp;
three of them should get Mario hats,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:55:49.680 --> 02:55:53.580
seeing as they were plumbers now.&nbsp;
Agent Two had happily laughed along,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:55:53.580 --> 02:55:58.439
suggesting that perhaps he should be&nbsp;
Luigi. Agent Six was utterly unamused&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:55:58.439 --> 02:56:03.359
by this whole situation and wanted no part of&nbsp;
it, which is honestly just so Wario of him.

02:56:03.359 --> 02:56:08.279
They followed along a carefully-mapped route&nbsp;
through the pipes, being careful not to touch&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:56:08.279 --> 02:56:15.240
any of them. After all, you never know what could&nbsp;
set SCP-015 off. Agent Six didn’t share in their&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:56:15.240 --> 02:56:20.700
merriment or their concern. In fact, he had&nbsp;
contempt for them and this whole situation.

02:56:20.700 --> 02:56:25.740
He thought Lon and Agent Two were a pair of&nbsp;
frightened, skittish children willing to buy&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:56:25.740 --> 02:56:31.380
whatever Foundation-assigned bullhonkey they&nbsp;
were given. He didn’t believe that SCP-015 was&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:56:31.380 --> 02:56:36.900
anywhere near as dangerous as they’d been told.&nbsp;
In all likelihood, knowing the eggheads up top,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:56:36.899 --> 02:56:41.339
they just didn’t want men of action like him&nbsp;
breaking any of their precious little toys&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:56:41.340 --> 02:56:47.220
while conducting missions on their behalf.&nbsp;
This whole thing was a big, stupid joke.

02:56:47.220 --> 02:56:52.800
As they ducked and weaved through the&nbsp;
confines of SCP-015, the tension between&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:56:52.800 --> 02:56:57.240
them all just silently grew. Their&nbsp;
flashlights played along the pipes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:56:57.240 --> 02:57:04.439
revealing the insane variety of shapes, sizes,&nbsp;
compositions, and materials in here. Wood, steel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:57:04.439 --> 02:57:12.179
flesh, bone, glass, pressed ash, granite,&nbsp;
and so much more. And yet, there were no&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:57:12.180 --> 02:57:18.120
pipes comprised of any standard pipe-making&nbsp;
material, like lead, PVC plastic, or copper.

02:57:18.120 --> 02:57:22.680
It was undeniably freaky, but when&nbsp;
you work with SCPs for long enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:57:22.680 --> 02:57:28.980
you learn to stop asking questions about the more&nbsp;
minor forms of strangeness inherent to anomalies.

02:57:28.979 --> 02:57:33.059
The composition only got narrower as&nbsp;
they delved deeper into the warehouse.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:57:33.060 --> 02:57:37.680
Lon felt the anxiety like a hand&nbsp;
gripping her throat and squeezing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:57:37.680 --> 02:57:43.800
but tried still to keep it hidden. Agent Six, the&nbsp;
largest among them, almost got stuck a few times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:57:43.800 --> 02:57:49.320
trying to crawl into spaces too tight for&nbsp;
him to fit. He felt like murdering Lon when&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:57:49.319 --> 02:57:54.479
she threw out a casual comparison to Winnie the&nbsp;
Pooh in the process. When they reached the MRV,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:57:54.479 --> 02:57:58.979
it gave them an ounce of momentary&nbsp;
relief. The journey was halfway done.

02:57:58.979 --> 02:58:03.599
But that comfort was quickly squashed by&nbsp;
seeing what had actually happened to the MRV:&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:58:03.600 --> 02:58:08.880
It had been speared by a pipe, and new&nbsp;
pipes had grown through and out of it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:58:08.880 --> 02:58:14.880
effectively rooting it in place. This caused&nbsp;
immediate debate among the group around one&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:58:14.880 --> 02:58:20.040
key issue: Did this mean that the MRV was&nbsp;
now technically part of the pipes? And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:58:20.040 --> 02:58:24.840
by extension, would the pipes feel the need&nbsp;
to defend themselves if the databanks were&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:58:24.840 --> 02:58:29.940
removed from the MRV in order to deliver the&nbsp;
readings back to the researchers at Site-17?

02:58:29.939 --> 02:58:35.160
While Lon and Agent Two carefully discussed&nbsp;
the issue, Agent Six lost his temper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:58:35.160 --> 02:58:39.660
and decided to show these two foolish kids&nbsp;
how it was done. He claimed that the two&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:58:39.660 --> 02:58:43.319
of them were worrying about a bunch&nbsp;
of Foundation hooey that they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:58:43.319 --> 02:58:47.040
inexperienced and gullible enough to&nbsp;
believe: As if this bunch of weird&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:58:47.040 --> 02:58:51.720
pipes was actually dangerous! It was like&nbsp;
how every six anomalies there’s apparently&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:58:51.720 --> 02:58:57.241
one capable of ending the world. It’s all a&nbsp;
bunch of overblown fearmongering nonsense!

02:58:57.240 --> 02:59:02.340
And to prove that he wasn’t just all talk&nbsp;
on this front, Agent Six pried open the top&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:59:02.340 --> 02:59:07.380
of the MRV and slid out the data cards - which&nbsp;
seemed to be covered in some kind of viscous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:59:07.380 --> 02:59:12.600
unpleasant liquid from the pipes that’d&nbsp;
penetrated the device. Still, he’d gotten&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:59:12.600 --> 02:59:17.100
what they came for. He could have done this whole&nbsp;
thing alone, without this pair of bedwetters.

02:59:17.100 --> 02:59:20.940
He scoffed and said, “Kids. I&nbsp;
don’t know how you two survive.”

02:59:20.939 --> 02:59:26.879
But he shouldn’t have spoken so soon. The&nbsp;
large pipe that he didn’t even realize he&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:59:26.880 --> 02:59:31.440
was standing on opened up beneath his feet,&nbsp;
and seconds later, he was immersed up to his&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:59:31.439 --> 02:59:35.759
shoulders in it. Six began to let out the&nbsp;
most horrifying shrieks you’ve ever heard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:59:35.760 --> 02:59:40.320
and when Agent Two and Lon ran over to&nbsp;
try to help him out, they realized why.

02:59:40.319 --> 02:59:44.519
The pipe below Agent Six was&nbsp;
filled with flowing molten iron.

02:59:44.520 --> 02:59:49.440
By the time they pulled Agent Six out -&nbsp;
Well, Agent Six from the shoulders up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:59:49.439 --> 02:59:54.299
considering that was all that was left&nbsp;
- he was already dead. And despite the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:59:54.300 --> 03:00:00.900
actual aggressor being destroyed, SCP-015&nbsp;
wasn’t done. It was ready to clean house.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:00:00.899 --> 03:00:05.519
As Agent Two and Lon attempted to flee,&nbsp;
some eye-level pipes around them burst,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:00:05.520 --> 03:00:11.460
firing crystalized glass into their faces,&nbsp;
cutting into their skin and eyes. But they&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:00:11.460 --> 03:00:15.840
needed to push on despite the pain if&nbsp;
they wanted to survive and get out.

03:00:15.840 --> 03:00:22.140
The two of them ran, horrifying noises of twisting&nbsp;
and bursting pipes roaring behind them. Pipes made&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:00:22.140 --> 03:00:28.020
of thorns and bones started to grow, blocking&nbsp;
off their exits until they were trapped. Lon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:00:28.020 --> 03:00:31.920
utilizing her small frame, was able to&nbsp;
crawl through a gap in the pipes into an&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:00:31.920 --> 03:00:36.480
adjoining chamber. Two was given a horrifying&nbsp;
surprise when a pipe burst near his hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:00:36.479 --> 03:00:42.059
showering his flesh in thick, corrosive&nbsp;
gunk. His hand melted away at the wrist.

03:00:42.060 --> 03:00:47.220
As Lon called for help in the tiny, cramped&nbsp;
chamber she was locked in, Two ran off to&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:00:47.220 --> 03:00:52.140
try and find an exit, where he could bring more&nbsp;
people in to help them. But it would already be&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:00:52.140 --> 03:00:57.720
too late for poor Lon. A thick, honey-like&nbsp;
substance began bleeding into the chamber,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:00:57.720 --> 03:01:03.600
disgusting in its overpowering saccharine&nbsp;
flavor and gloopy, viscous thickness. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:01:03.600 --> 03:01:09.600
chamber seemed to get smaller and smaller as Lon&nbsp;
screamed and the honey level rose. Eventually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:01:09.600 --> 03:01:14.400
it got higher than her mouth and nose could&nbsp;
reach. She gave a horrifying final gurgle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:01:14.399 --> 03:01:19.080
and then she was gone, entombed&nbsp;
forever between the pipes.

03:01:19.080 --> 03:01:24.720
Agent Two kept running. His flashlight was&nbsp;
beginning to die, but he didn’t care. As his&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:01:24.720 --> 03:01:28.741
eyes bled from the splinters of glass and&nbsp;
what was left of his hand dripped from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:01:28.740 --> 03:01:34.260
jagged nub of his wrist, all he could do was&nbsp;
keep moving forwards. He panted and screamed&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:01:34.260 --> 03:01:38.880
and stumbled in the dark until he tripped on&nbsp;
something he didn’t notice and pitched forwards.

03:01:38.880 --> 03:01:46.260
He tumbled into the gaping maw of a nearby&nbsp;
pipe, surrounded by unseeable, unknowable ooze,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:01:46.260 --> 03:01:51.900
as he tumbled further down into the seemingly&nbsp;
infinite darkness. It was too dark to see and&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:01:51.899 --> 03:01:57.599
too cramped to move anywhere but straight&nbsp;
down. All he could do was scream and scream&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:01:57.600 --> 03:02:03.000
and scream until his voice was hoarse and&nbsp;
his throat bled, though nobody was there to&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:02:03.000 --> 03:02:08.399
even hear his cries. Days later, when his skin&nbsp;
started to shred off, it was almost welcome.

03:02:08.399 --> 03:02:12.000
Dr. Gears would later receive the&nbsp;
news of this tragically failed&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:02:12.000 --> 03:02:16.500
mission and the three operatives now&nbsp;
declared MIA because of it. He sighed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:02:16.500 --> 03:02:19.740
and wrote up his report on the&nbsp;
matter, closing with the sentence:&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:02:19.740 --> 03:02:26.939
“Data deemed non-vital in light of lost staff.&nbsp;
SCP-015 classification level review suggested.”

03:02:26.939 --> 03:02:33.000
The Foundation was in chaos. One minute, Dr.&nbsp;
Bright was looking into some harmless products&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:02:33.000 --> 03:02:38.220
on Amazon to help the Foundation's anomalies&nbsp;
have a nicer experience in their cells. The next,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:02:38.220 --> 03:02:42.660
he was physically fighting for his life&nbsp;
against the company's CEO. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:02:42.660 --> 03:02:47.460
Dr. Bright’s wacky antics had revealed&nbsp;
the location of the SCP Foundation’s most&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:02:47.460 --> 03:02:52.680
valuable containment site to Jeff&nbsp;
Bezos and his legions of minions.

03:02:52.680 --> 03:02:57.900
Does Dr. Jack Bright have it in him to fix&nbsp;
his mistake, defeat the mighty Jeff Bezos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:02:57.899 --> 03:03:01.859
and save the SCP Foundation from&nbsp;
corporate servitude? Are the forces&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:03:01.859 --> 03:03:07.380
of the SCP Foundation equal in strength to the&nbsp;
Amazon horde? And how, at the eleventh hour,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:03:07.380 --> 03:03:16.980
will anomalies like SCP-096, SCP-682,&nbsp;
SCP-173, and SCP-049 help save the day?

03:03:16.979 --> 03:03:21.599
Buckle up. The only way to&nbsp;
find out is to keep watching.

03:03:21.600 --> 03:03:25.560
The two titans, Dr. Bright and&nbsp;
the freakishly buff Jeff Bezos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:03:25.560 --> 03:03:29.280
were locked into an intense struggle.&nbsp;
The hapless immortal tried his best&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:03:29.279 --> 03:03:33.599
to punch and kick the augmented CEO,&nbsp;
but all it did was make Bezos laugh.

03:03:33.600 --> 03:03:39.540
“Pathetic, Jack,” Bezos roared. “Let&nbsp;
me show you how a real man does it.”

03:03:39.540 --> 03:03:43.380
With one punch, Bezos sent Dr.&nbsp;
Bright skidding across the dirt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:03:43.380 --> 03:03:49.200
seeing stars. Bezos punched him so hard, Dr.&nbsp;
Bright felt like he’d been hit by a runaway&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:03:49.200 --> 03:03:55.139
truck. Where had he even gotten all this anomalous&nbsp;
technology? Something more was going on here...

03:03:55.140 --> 03:04:01.080
But before Dr. Bright could collect his thoughts,&nbsp;
Bezos was already upon him. The ruthless business&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:04:01.080 --> 03:04:06.059
mogul clasped his hands together and brought&nbsp;
them down onto the back of Jack’s head in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:04:06.060 --> 03:04:12.241
brutal pile drive, burying his head in the dirt.&nbsp;
The guards of Site-19 looked on in terror at&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:04:12.240 --> 03:04:16.740
the thousands of Amazon troops surrounding&nbsp;
them - And they were betting all this on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:04:16.740 --> 03:04:21.899
fist fight? And what’s more, it didn’t look&nbsp;
like Dr. Bright was winning that fist fight.

03:04:21.899 --> 03:04:24.719
Unless the good doctor used his special move.

03:04:24.720 --> 03:04:29.160
Bezos, still cackling like a Saturday&nbsp;
morning cartoon villain, grabbed Dr.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:04:29.160 --> 03:04:34.439
Bright by his hair and dragged him out of the&nbsp;
dirt. He lifted the Doctor, bloody and bashed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:04:34.439 --> 03:04:39.419
with one hand. At that moment, Dr. Bright&nbsp;
knew that he had to go with the nuclear option&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:04:39.420 --> 03:04:46.980
here - he needed to use SCP-963 to take over the&nbsp;
body of Jeff Bezos and end this demented war.

03:04:46.979 --> 03:04:52.500
“Had enough, Jack?” Bezos said. “Or&nbsp;
do you want me to humiliate you even&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:04:52.500 --> 03:04:57.540
more in front of your sad little employees?&nbsp;
Or, should I say, my sad little employees!”

03:04:59.220 --> 03:05:03.660
Without another word, Dr. Bright grabbed&nbsp;
the amulet hanging around his neck and&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:05:03.660 --> 03:05:08.399
balled it into his fist. Moving as quickly as&nbsp;
he could, he lunged at Bezos with the medallion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:05:08.399 --> 03:05:12.660
knowing that one touch should be enough&nbsp;
to finish this whole thing. But then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:05:12.660 --> 03:05:18.720
the unexpected happened: Bezos caught Dr.&nbsp;
Bright’s hand. The flesh of the CEO’s fist&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:05:18.720 --> 03:05:25.200
turned obsidian black. He gave a cruel&nbsp;
smirk. He was still himself. Impossible…

03:05:25.200 --> 03:05:29.040
All the SCP Foundation staff in&nbsp;
attendance were shocked and horrified,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:05:29.040 --> 03:05:37.080
but none more than Dr. Bright himself. This was&nbsp;
the first time that SCP-963 simply hadn’t worked!

03:05:37.080 --> 03:05:42.479
“I don’t understand,” Jack said, voice&nbsp;
trembling as Bezos squeezed his hand with&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:05:42.479 --> 03:05:45.479
an iron grip. “I should have taken&nbsp;
you over. How is this possible?”

03:05:45.479 --> 03:05:50.339
Bezos chuckled before unleashing a brutal&nbsp;
headbutt on Dr. Bright, throwing him back&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:05:50.340 --> 03:05:55.500
through the air. Bright was lucky he managed to&nbsp;
keep a grip on the medallion - this body hadn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:05:55.500 --> 03:06:00.540
worn it for thirty days; if he dropped it, he&nbsp;
would have just been another useless necklace.

03:06:00.540 --> 03:06:05.340
“Nanomachines, son!” Bezos barked,&nbsp;
cracking his neck back into place. “I&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:06:07.979 --> 03:06:08.939
had them designed with you in mind.&nbsp;
Any time and anywhere your necklace&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:06:10.439 --> 03:06:13.500
touches me, they reshuffle my genetics to&nbsp;
turn that flesh into inanimate psuedometal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:06:13.500 --> 03:06:17.279
Do you think I’d lead an assault on&nbsp;
the SCP Foundation without planning&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:06:17.279 --> 03:06:20.819
for every possible eventuality?&nbsp;
I’ve been playing the long game!”

03:06:20.819 --> 03:06:25.259
It was at this point that the Foundation guards&nbsp;
assembled outside the building opened fire on&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:06:25.260 --> 03:06:30.960
Bezos, but it was no use. The bullets just&nbsp;
ricocheted off of his anomalously enhanced&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:06:30.960 --> 03:06:35.520
physique. He just gave a bellowing laugh and&nbsp;
ordered the full strength of his forces to&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:06:35.520 --> 03:06:41.460
move in. As far as he was concerned, the fight&nbsp;
was already won. If their forces could plant&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:06:41.460 --> 03:06:47.160
the grinning Amazon flag in the heart of&nbsp;
Site-19, the SCP Foundation would be his.

03:06:47.160 --> 03:06:50.519
It was time for the true final battle to begin.

03:06:50.520 --> 03:06:55.440
Amazon’s troops loaded their weapons and&nbsp;
charged, firing volley after volley of&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:06:55.439 --> 03:06:59.939
bullets onto the Foundation guards entrenched&nbsp;
in their positions. Of course, the Foundation&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:06:59.939 --> 03:07:06.000
personnel was better trained, but the Amazon army&nbsp;
outnumbered them ten to one. It was only a matter&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:07:06.000 --> 03:07:11.340
of time until the barricades gave way, and the&nbsp;
forces spilled into the containment facility.

03:07:11.340 --> 03:07:13.440
And then they did.

03:07:13.439 --> 03:07:17.339
Inside the building, Dr. Clef was&nbsp;
reading his new copy of Shotguns:&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:07:17.340 --> 03:07:21.779
A Comprehensive Guide, when the army breached&nbsp;
the perimeter. It was at this point that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:07:21.779 --> 03:07:27.779
put down the book and picked up his favorite&nbsp;
shotgun, a Remington Model 11-87 - fun fact:&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:07:27.779 --> 03:07:33.059
Also the preferred weapon of Anton Chigurh&nbsp;
from No Country For Old Men - and entered&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:07:33.060 --> 03:07:38.040
the fray. They’d need every SCP Foundation&nbsp;
hand on deck to repel the incoming attack.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:07:38.040 --> 03:07:43.740
The one thing that not even Dr. Clef predicted&nbsp;
was for the anomalies to get involved, too.

03:07:43.740 --> 03:07:46.740
You see, in ordering his troops to attack Site-19,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:07:46.740 --> 03:07:52.500
the fleet of Amazon attack helicopters above&nbsp;
began launching AGM-114 Hellfire missiles&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:07:52.500 --> 03:07:58.140
at the building to knock out guard towers and&nbsp;
strategic communications arrays. This, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:07:58.140 --> 03:08:03.000
had the unintended effect of causing power&nbsp;
fluctuations within the building, and with so many&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:08:03.000 --> 03:08:07.500
of the reserve generators devoted to maintaining&nbsp;
external security against the Amazon army,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:08:07.500 --> 03:08:14.220
internal security failed entirely. In other words,&nbsp;
a whole lot of cell doors were popping open.

03:08:14.220 --> 03:08:19.380
A group of Amazon footsoldiers was making&nbsp;
their way down hallway 6C when they saw&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:08:19.380 --> 03:08:24.359
something strange on the ground: A tide of&nbsp;
green, bubbling chemicals that produced a&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:08:24.359 --> 03:08:30.540
horrific smell. Little did they know, this was&nbsp;
powerful hydrochloric acid, but in this instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:08:30.540 --> 03:08:35.640
it wasn’t the acid that they should be worried&nbsp;
about. They heard a raspy voice behind them say...

03:08:35.640 --> 03:08:37.860
“Disssgussssting...”

03:08:38.819 --> 03:08:44.340
That’s when they turned to see SCP-682,&nbsp;
the giant, rage-fueled immortal reptile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:08:44.340 --> 03:08:50.100
standing right behind them. It had just finished&nbsp;
its exotic beef jerky collection, and now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:08:50.100 --> 03:08:55.680
it was eager for seconds. The Amazon soldiers&nbsp;
panicked and began to open fire on the beast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:08:55.680 --> 03:09:00.600
but all that succeeded in doing&nbsp;
was annoying it. SCP-682 leaped&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:09:00.600 --> 03:09:04.020
onto them and began devouring the&nbsp;
group of hapless mercenaries alive.

03:09:04.020 --> 03:09:09.720
Elsewhere in the facility, another group of Amazon&nbsp;
mercenaries was chasing some defenseless SCP&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:09:09.720 --> 03:09:14.520
Foundation researchers through the secure humanoid&nbsp;
containment wing. The researchers tried to run,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:09:14.520 --> 03:09:19.201
but soon, they were backed into a corner. The&nbsp;
mercenaries laughed and leveled their assault&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:09:19.200 --> 03:09:25.080
rifles. This would be an easy kill. However, the&nbsp;
second they tried to fire, they realized something&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:09:25.080 --> 03:09:31.559
extremely strange had occurred: All of their guns&nbsp;
had turned into delightful bouquets. Standing&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:09:31.560 --> 03:09:39.300
between them and the researchers they intended&nbsp;
to kill was SCP-343, also known to some as God.

03:09:39.300 --> 03:09:44.100
“Now, now, boys,” SCP-343&nbsp;
said with a slight smile.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:09:44.100 --> 03:09:48.360
“Can’t we all just get along? Make love, not war.”

03:09:48.359 --> 03:09:53.880
Frightened by the seemingly impossible act they&nbsp;
just witnessed, the now unarmed mercenaries ran&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:09:53.880 --> 03:09:58.319
into a nearby room with a sturdy-looking&nbsp;
door and locked themselves in. Hopefully,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:09:58.319 --> 03:10:02.939
nobody would get them here. That thought&nbsp;
was interrupted by a grandiose voice with&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:10:02.939 --> 03:10:05.639
an archaic French accent speaking out behind them.

03:10:05.640 --> 03:10:10.860
“Why hello there, good sirs! So kind&nbsp;
of you to step into my practice today.”

03:10:10.859 --> 03:10:16.620
They turned and saw a strange, robed man&nbsp;
wearing a runner pigeon mask. The figure&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:10:16.620 --> 03:10:21.000
removed the mask, only to reveal what&nbsp;
seemed like another strange beaked mask&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:10:21.000 --> 03:10:27.960
underneath. It was SCP-049, his desk covered&nbsp;
in pleasingly-polished Amazon medical tools.

03:10:27.960 --> 03:10:32.760
“My, my, you gents seem a little&nbsp;
under the weather. This is a cause&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:10:32.760 --> 03:10:36.660
for concern,” the Plague Doctor said as&nbsp;
he began to walk toward the frightened&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:10:36.660 --> 03:10:40.680
mercenaries. “The Pestilence runs&nbsp;
rife these days. Not to worry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:10:40.680 --> 03:10:46.320
though. I’m an expert. And I’ve been&nbsp;
itching to try out my lovely new tools...”

03:10:46.319 --> 03:10:50.819
Horrified screaming was heard from&nbsp;
the room shortly after, then silence.

03:10:50.819 --> 03:10:56.099
But Amazon troops kept pouring in. It was&nbsp;
an all-out assault beyond any that Site-19&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:10:56.100 --> 03:11:00.900
had ever experienced. Some Amazon strike teams&nbsp;
were making their way through the break rooms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:11:00.899 --> 03:11:05.399
hunting for more Foundation employees to&nbsp;
capture or execute. One of them remarked&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:11:05.399 --> 03:11:10.019
that these SCP Foundation employees must&nbsp;
be real headcases - Why else would they&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:11:10.020 --> 03:11:14.041
keep such a hideous concrete sculpture&nbsp;
in their break room? Utterly bizarre.

03:11:14.040 --> 03:11:17.519
The second they found there was no one&nbsp;
inside and turned to leave the room,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:11:17.520 --> 03:11:22.320
they all lay dead on the ground with&nbsp;
snapped necks. That would make it seventeen&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:11:22.319 --> 03:11:27.840
Amazon mercenaries they’d killed today - The&nbsp;
Sculpture was having a truly wonderful time.

03:11:27.840 --> 03:11:32.880
Meanwhile, over in his office, Dr. Clef was&nbsp;
fighting for his life. A group of junior&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:11:32.880 --> 03:11:37.500
researchers were taking refuge in there with him&nbsp;
and handing him shotgun shells from his many,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:11:37.500 --> 03:11:42.840
many boxes of them when he needed to reload.&nbsp;
Soldier after soldier was running into the room,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:11:42.840 --> 03:11:46.380
and Clef was using his trusty&nbsp;
Remington pump-action to blow&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:11:46.380 --> 03:11:49.859
them away with incredible precision&nbsp;
every time one of them ran in.

03:11:49.859 --> 03:11:53.399
An awestruck researcher asked,&nbsp;
“How good are you with that thing?”

03:11:53.399 --> 03:11:57.179
Clef laughed and said, “Kid, I could&nbsp;
unbutton your lab coat with it.”

03:11:57.180 --> 03:12:02.340
But then tragedy struck: Dr. Clef ran&nbsp;
out of shells. He sighed and told the&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:12:02.340 --> 03:12:04.860
assembled researchers to run out of the back exit&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:12:04.859 --> 03:12:09.240
and save themselves. He’d hold back&nbsp;
the tide of Amazon mercenaries alone.

03:12:09.240 --> 03:12:15.179
He kissed his shotgun and said, “Forgive me for&nbsp;
this, Remy,” then grabbed it by the barrel. If he&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:12:15.180 --> 03:12:19.980
couldn’t shoot with it, he’d at least use it&nbsp;
as a club. He ran out into the fray, bashing&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:12:19.979 --> 03:12:25.559
down mercenaries left and right with his shotgun&nbsp;
club until he was finally outnumbered. A group of&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:12:25.560 --> 03:12:30.060
heavily-armed soldiers surrounded him, holding him&nbsp;
at gunpoint. One of them gave the order to fire.

03:12:30.060 --> 03:12:34.860
“Wait!” Dr. Clef said. “I can be&nbsp;
valuable to you. You don’t know who I am,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:12:34.859 --> 03:12:37.139
do you? Give me a second; let me show you my ID.”

03:12:37.140 --> 03:12:40.380
Dr. Clef pulled an ID wallet from his lab coat and&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:12:40.380 --> 03:12:44.220
showed it to the soldiers. They looked&nbsp;
at it, confused and a little disturbed.

03:12:44.220 --> 03:12:46.800
The leader of the group&nbsp;
said. “That’s not an ID card,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:12:46.800 --> 03:12:48.660
you schmuck. What the hell&nbsp;
is that ugly thing anyway?”

03:12:48.660 --> 03:12:53.880
By this point, Dr. Clef had already closed his&nbsp;
eyes. A distant screaming got closer and closer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:12:53.880 --> 03:12:59.580
until the wall of his office exploded.&nbsp;
SCP-096 entered the room, shrieking with rage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:12:59.580 --> 03:13:05.040
and began tearing the hapless soldiers to shreds.&nbsp;
Clef rolled away, keeping his eyes closed and&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:13:05.040 --> 03:13:10.560
waiting for the carnage to be over. But in a&nbsp;
grander sense, the carnage had only just begun.

03:13:10.560 --> 03:13:16.560
Oh, and for those who were curious, yes, SCP-811&nbsp;
did get her blobfish plushy. She loved it.

03:13:16.560 --> 03:13:21.121
Outside the facility, Jeff Bezos looked upon&nbsp;
the destruction he wrought and laughed. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:13:21.120 --> 03:13:25.439
was just as wonderful as he’d imagined it in&nbsp;
all his years of intense planning for this&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:13:25.439 --> 03:13:31.500
very moment. It’d all unfolded exactly to&nbsp;
his design. The SCP Foundation would fall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:13:31.500 --> 03:13:35.340
and his master plan would finally be complete.

03:13:35.340 --> 03:13:41.400
Dr. Jack Bright, demoralized but not defeated,&nbsp;
rose shakily to his feet. This whole disaster&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:13:41.399 --> 03:13:47.700
had been his fault. Had had to redeem this. He&nbsp;
had to defeat Jeff Bezos, or the Foundation would&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:13:47.700 --> 03:13:54.000
fall into his iron clutches. Even if it finally,&nbsp;
truly, killed him, Dr. Bright would not give up.

03:13:54.000 --> 03:13:57.779
“Impressive tech,” Dr. Bright said,&nbsp;
cracking his knuckles and getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:13:57.779 --> 03:14:00.660
Jeff’s attention again. “Which&nbsp;
sweat shop built it for you?”

03:14:00.660 --> 03:14:05.099
Jeff just laughed and turned to Bright&nbsp;
again, more than ready for round two.

03:14:05.100 --> 03:14:11.400
“A little place called Prometheus Labs,&nbsp;
Jack,” Jeff said. “Remember them? I was on&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:14:11.399 --> 03:14:16.019
the board of directors before the incident.&nbsp;
Just like you and your Foundation dogs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:14:16.020 --> 03:14:18.600
they left behind plenty of goodies for me.”

03:14:18.600 --> 03:14:24.180
Dr. Bright charged at Bezos, screaming in animal&nbsp;
fury. He punched the smug anomalous businessman&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:14:24.180 --> 03:14:29.940
square in the jaw. Bezos barely even flinched.&nbsp;
He gave Jack an utterly brutal open-handed slap,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:14:29.939 --> 03:14:35.580
knocking him towards the entrance to Site-19,&nbsp;
where more Amazon soldiers were still pouring in.

03:14:35.580 --> 03:14:37.800
Still, Jack began to stand again.

03:14:37.800 --> 03:14:44.340
“What the hell does a corporate&nbsp;
stiff like you want with the SCP&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:14:44.340 --> 03:14:48.000
Foundation?” he said. “We want&nbsp;
to help the world, not rule it.”

03:14:48.000 --> 03:14:51.000
Bezos sneered and began stomping&nbsp;
toward the immortal doctor.

03:14:51.000 --> 03:14:55.740
“I don’t just want to rule the world,&nbsp;
Jack. I want to hold it in my hand like&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:14:55.740 --> 03:15:02.160
an apple and take a big, juicy bite. All&nbsp;
that exists will belong to me,” he said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:15:02.160 --> 03:15:07.319
throwing a punch that Dr. Bright was thankfully&nbsp;
able to dodge. “That’s always been the plan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:15:07.319 --> 03:15:10.979
and the SCP Foundation is the final piece I need.”

03:15:10.979 --> 03:15:15.959
Dr. Bright, summoning all the adrenaline&nbsp;
in his body, unleashed a flurry of sharp&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:15:15.960 --> 03:15:20.640
punches to Jeff’s abdomen. He simply&nbsp;
tanked every strike and laughed.

03:15:20.640 --> 03:15:25.320
“I’ve been funding the Chaos Insurgency for&nbsp;
years, Jack. I’m one of Marshall, Carter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:15:25.319 --> 03:15:29.279
and Dark’s largest shareholders. Amazon&nbsp;
is Dr. Wondertainment’s primary global&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:15:29.279 --> 03:15:32.219
distributor. The Sarkicists,&nbsp;
The Fifthists, The Mekhanites,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:15:32.220 --> 03:15:38.220
those pretentious art snobs at Are We Cool Yet,&nbsp;
they’re all in my deep pockets!” Jeff roared.

03:15:38.220 --> 03:15:41.520
He wrapped one of his massive hands&nbsp;
around Dr. Bright’s throat and&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:15:41.520 --> 03:15:44.940
lifted him clear off the ground.&nbsp;
Jack struggled against his grip.

03:15:44.939 --> 03:15:49.859
“You’ve spent decades worrying about&nbsp;
all the wrong things, Jack,” Jeff said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:15:49.859 --> 03:15:54.240
slowly tightening his grip around Dr.&nbsp;
Bright’s throat. “The Devourer Of Worlds?&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:15:54.240 --> 03:16:01.859
The Black Moon? The Scarlet King? My new world&nbsp;
won’t have a king. It’ll have a CEO. And with&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:16:01.859 --> 03:16:07.740
the power within these walls, I’ll be the&nbsp;
Chairman of the Cosmic Board! And it all&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:16:07.740 --> 03:16:12.899
starts with me killing you and turning your&nbsp;
silly little necklace into a paperweight!”

03:16:12.899 --> 03:16:18.299
But Jack had an ace up his sleeve. Something&nbsp;
he had the good sense to pocket before facing&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:16:18.300 --> 03:16:23.880
Bezos on the battlefield. He reached&nbsp;
into his lab coat and pulled out SCP-662,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:16:23.880 --> 03:16:29.220
The Butler’s Hand Bell. One little ring and&nbsp;
Mr. Deeds suddenly appeared right next to them.

03:16:29.220 --> 03:16:34.080
Jack barely managed to wheeze out,&nbsp;
“Deeds, kick Jeff in the groin!”

03:16:34.080 --> 03:16:38.220
“Of course, sir, “ Mr. Deeds&nbsp;
said, before doing exactly that.

03:16:38.220 --> 03:16:43.800
Jeff winced in pain and loosened his grip on Dr.&nbsp;
Bright’s neck. The plucky immortal took his chance&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:16:43.800 --> 03:16:48.360
to wriggle free from Jeff’s grip and run into&nbsp;
the facility, yelling back, “Deeds, stall him!”

03:16:48.359 --> 03:16:53.040
But before Deeds could execute this order,&nbsp;
Jeff Bezos fractured the anomalous butler’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:16:53.040 --> 03:16:59.399
skull with a single punch, and chased Dr. Bright&nbsp;
into the facility. He would have his revenge!

03:16:59.399 --> 03:17:03.660
As Dr. Bright fled, he grabbed his Foundation&nbsp;
walkie-talkie and frantically spoke into it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:17:03.660 --> 03:17:06.000
“Clef, I’m in big trouble! I&nbsp;
think I have a way to stop this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:17:06.000 --> 03:17:07.979
but there’s just something you&nbsp;
need to do for me first...”

03:17:07.979 --> 03:17:11.939
Battles were raging on throughout the site.&nbsp;
Some Amazon soldiers had unloaded their&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:17:11.939 --> 03:17:16.319
rifles into Cain, only to find themselves&nbsp;
on the floor, dying of massive blood loss.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:17:16.319 --> 03:17:21.599
Other squadrons of mercenaries were being&nbsp;
tortured by the nightmarish SCP-106 in his&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:17:21.600 --> 03:17:28.020
terrifying pocket dimension. He was having a&nbsp;
wonderful time. SCP-999 was even tickling a&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:17:28.020 --> 03:17:32.161
gaggle of Amazon’s hired veteran war&nbsp;
criminals into absolute submission.

03:17:32.160 --> 03:17:36.479
Much to Jeff’s rage and horror, it looked&nbsp;
like, with the help of the anomalies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:17:36.479 --> 03:17:39.479
the Foundation might actually&nbsp;
be able to turn the tide.

03:17:39.479 --> 03:17:45.000
This would not do. He’d destroy Bright&nbsp;
and take care of the rest personally.

03:17:45.000 --> 03:17:50.640
“It has to be this way, Jack!” Bezos yelled&nbsp;
after the fleeing doctor. “I’m making the&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:17:50.640 --> 03:17:54.900
mother of all corporate omelets here, can’t&nbsp;
fret over a few ransacked containment sites!”

03:17:54.899 --> 03:18:00.000
Bezos’ self-aggrandizing rants were&nbsp;
interrupted by SCP-682 leaping onto&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:18:00.000 --> 03:18:04.260
him out of an adjoining hallway, giving Dr.&nbsp;
Bright even more time to create distance&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:18:04.260 --> 03:18:09.600
between them. 682 fought valiantly&nbsp;
and viciously, but Jeff’s Prometheus&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:18:09.600 --> 03:18:15.840
Labs nanomachine augmentations gave him the&nbsp;
edge. He was able to get 682 into a headlock,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:18:15.840 --> 03:18:20.520
Steve Irwin style, then punch it in&nbsp;
the head until it was knocked out.

03:18:20.520 --> 03:18:24.121
Jeff sighed, relieved, and&nbsp;
continued the chase after Jack.

03:18:24.120 --> 03:18:28.680
As Jeff turned a corner to chase Dr.&nbsp;
Bright, he ran right into one of Jack’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:18:28.680 --> 03:18:33.660
traps. There was Dr. Bright, standing about&nbsp;
fifteen feet away from him, holding his&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:18:33.660 --> 03:18:38.279
favorite item that he normally wasn’t&nbsp;
allowed to use: The Chainsaw Cannon.

03:18:38.279 --> 03:18:41.519
Jack smirked and said, “Smile, you son of a—“

03:18:41.520 --> 03:18:45.960
BOOM! A chainsaw projectile blasted&nbsp;
towards Jeff at incredible speeds. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:18:45.960 --> 03:18:49.260
was through pure luck and superhuman&nbsp;
reflexes that Jeff was able to catch&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:18:49.260 --> 03:18:54.000
it between his palms by the blade and&nbsp;
hold it in place, even as it cut into&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:18:54.000 --> 03:18:58.560
his nanite-hardened skin. With a grunt&nbsp;
of rage, he threw the saw to the ground.

03:18:58.560 --> 03:19:01.680
“Is that all you got, Jack?” he growled.

03:19:01.680 --> 03:19:07.620
“Not quite,” Dr. Bright replied. “I’ve&nbsp;
got a few friends who owe me some favors.”

03:19:07.620 --> 03:19:12.420
Suddenly, Bezos heard footsteps behind him.&nbsp;
He turned to see three figures standing there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:19:12.420 --> 03:19:15.899
primed and ready for battle: SCP-4494,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:19:15.899 --> 03:19:22.799
The Specter, SCP-5151 The Black&nbsp;
Knight, and SCP-2800, Cactusman.

03:19:22.800 --> 03:19:27.600
“You fight without honor, Sir Jeffrey!” The&nbsp;
Black Knight proclaimed as he drew his longsword.

03:19:27.600 --> 03:19:32.280
“Aye, and I can’t stand bullies!” Said&nbsp;
Cactusman as his spikes began to grow.

03:19:32.279 --> 03:19:37.019
“Wage theft and tax evasion is a&nbsp;
crime, evildoer!” The Specter added.

03:19:37.020 --> 03:19:41.701
“Well, then,” Jeff said. “I suppose&nbsp;
I’ll just have to kill you all...”

03:19:41.700 --> 03:19:46.800
The fight began. Our group of four heroes&nbsp;
held their own impressively, to begin with.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:19:46.800 --> 03:19:51.600
They consistently helped each other block or avoid&nbsp;
the deadly strikes that Bezos was unleashing and&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:19:51.600 --> 03:19:57.540
return their own. Four anomalies against one.&nbsp;
The Black Knight even gave Dr. Bright one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:19:57.540 --> 03:20:02.399
his spare swords to give him a better chance&nbsp;
of landing a meaningful blow against Bezos.

03:20:02.399 --> 03:20:08.040
But in the end, it was all for nothing.&nbsp;
Bezos powered up, his nanomachines drawing&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:20:08.040 --> 03:20:13.260
energy from the building around him, until he&nbsp;
was even larger and stronger. In his state,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:20:13.260 --> 03:20:18.480
he easily beat down his four attackers,&nbsp;
incapacitating them and standing over Bright.

03:20:18.479 --> 03:20:23.879
“It’s useless, Jack,” he said with&nbsp;
another evil laugh. “You can’t beat me.”

03:20:23.880 --> 03:20:29.580
“I know,” Jack groaned in pain. “But I&nbsp;
did a pretty good job of stalling you.”

03:20:29.580 --> 03:20:33.779
At that moment, Clef came running down&nbsp;
the hall, wielding another firearm. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:20:33.779 --> 03:20:38.580
it wasn’t a shotgun this time. In fact, it&nbsp;
didn’t even look like a real gun, it looked&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:20:38.580 --> 03:20:44.939
like a spray-painted Nerf gun. That got another&nbsp;
laugh out of Jeff. What an utterly feeble attempt.

03:20:44.939 --> 03:20:49.740
That’s when Dr. Clef leveled the nerf gun&nbsp;
and fired one of its darts at Jeff’s chest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:20:49.740 --> 03:20:51.779
where it seemed to bounce off uselessly.

03:20:51.779 --> 03:20:55.920
“Really?” He asked, incredulous.&nbsp;
“Come on. This is just getting—“

03:20:55.920 --> 03:20:59.819
His words were cut off by an uppercut&nbsp;
from Dr. Bright. Inexplicably,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:20:59.819 --> 03:21:04.920
he actually felt that one. Jeff staggered back,&nbsp;
rubbing his jaw as Bright rose to his feet.

03:21:04.920 --> 03:21:09.000
“How the hell did you hurt me?”&nbsp;
Jeff muttered, genuinely shocked.

03:21:09.000 --> 03:21:11.520
Dr. Bright smirked. “That was the Nerfing Gun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:21:11.520 --> 03:21:14.880
chrome dome. We just made your&nbsp;
precious nanomachines useless.”

03:21:14.880 --> 03:21:19.500
With another incredibly well-earned punch in&nbsp;
the face from Dr. Bright, Jeff was down on&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:21:19.500 --> 03:21:23.580
the ground with a bleeding nose. Elsewhere&nbsp;
in the facility, the researchers, guards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:21:23.580 --> 03:21:28.019
and anomalies working together had defeated&nbsp;
the dark forces of Amazon. Outside, Hammer&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:21:28.020 --> 03:21:33.661
Down - The largest and most militarily powerful&nbsp;
Mobile Task Force - had arrived and wiped out&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:21:33.660 --> 03:21:39.660
what remained of Jeff’s invasion force outside.&nbsp;
It wasn’t easy, but the SCP Foundation had won.

03:21:39.660 --> 03:21:44.639
As Jeff lay on the ground, holding his broken&nbsp;
nose, Dr. Bright held him at sword point.

03:21:44.640 --> 03:21:50.100
“Wait, Wait!” Jeff said. “Let’s not let&nbsp;
hard feelings linger. We’re both adults,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:21:50.100 --> 03:21:53.940
right? And I’m a businessman. I make deals.&nbsp;
Compromises. I can give you anything from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:21:53.939 --> 03:21:57.839
Amazon stock, free of charge! As long as we&nbsp;
agree that I can go and we forget all this!”

03:21:57.840 --> 03:21:59.819
Dr. Bright smiled and thought it over.

03:21:59.819 --> 03:22:03.719
“Well...” he said. “There are&nbsp;
a few things I think I’d like&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:22:03.720 --> 03:22:08.160
for myself. And there are still plenty&nbsp;
more anomalies that need enriching...”

03:22:08.160 --> 03:22:13.500
Victory secured. Want another video abo but the&nbsp;
deranged things Dr. Bright bought for even more&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:22:13.500 --> 03:22:18.120
anomalies and Foundation staff? Let us know&nbsp;
down in the comments, and we’ll continue the&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:22:18.120 --> 03:22:22.439
strangest story that the SCP Foundation’s&nbsp;
most eccentric researcher ever told...

03:22:22.439 --> 03:22:28.080
2:00 AM, a few miles outside the city.&nbsp;
The car tore down the asphalt at sixty&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:22:28.080 --> 03:22:32.040
miles an hour. We kept the beams&nbsp;
low, the dark road around us only&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:22:32.040 --> 03:22:36.960
illuminated by the occasional street lamp&nbsp;
overhead. Things moved unseen in the trees.

03:22:36.960 --> 03:22:40.680
An old song rattled off the&nbsp;
radio. The connection was patchy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:22:40.680 --> 03:22:45.420
so it was interrupted by intermittent spikes of&nbsp;
static. It was the kind of night when you knew,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:22:45.420 --> 03:22:50.580
deep down, that anything could happen. You just&nbsp;
hoped that “anything” would be in your favor.

03:22:50.580 --> 03:22:54.420
I’d rolled the window down a few&nbsp;
minutes ago, breathing in all that cold,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:22:54.420 --> 03:22:58.920
fresh air to stave off the looming specter&nbsp;
of sleep. Thank god I wasn’t the one driving,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:22:58.920 --> 03:23:04.020
or things would’ve gotten deadly way sooner. Cops&nbsp;
would’ve found us with our bumper wrapped around&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:23:04.020 --> 03:23:09.360
a tree, our heads one with the steering wheel&nbsp;
or the windshield, dead on impact or from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:23:09.359 --> 03:23:14.219
unforgiving cold overnight. They might’ve even&nbsp;
felt sorry for us, until they found the case.

03:23:14.220 --> 03:23:19.260
Perhaps it would have been better that&nbsp;
way. At least it would’ve been quick. A&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:23:19.260 --> 03:23:22.739
lot of bad things can happen on lonely&nbsp;
state highways in the dead of night,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:23:22.739 --> 03:23:27.359
and we were about to find out that just&nbsp;
crashing your car was one of the more mild ones.

03:23:27.359 --> 03:23:30.960
Scott was driving. He was also the&nbsp;
one who brokered this whole deal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:23:30.960 --> 03:23:35.580
I was just coming along to provide backup.&nbsp;
There was a fully-loaded Saturday Night Special&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:23:35.580 --> 03:23:39.479
sitting in my inner coat pocket, hoping&nbsp;
that it wouldn’t see any action tonight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:23:39.479 --> 03:23:44.879
and a pump-action shotgun sitting in the back in&nbsp;
case things got really hairy. Deals like this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:23:44.880 --> 03:23:49.859
you either come well prepared or reckon with the&nbsp;
heavy chance you sure as hell won’t be leaving.

03:23:49.859 --> 03:23:53.700
I never asked Scott how he came into the&nbsp;
goods currently sitting in the briefcase&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:23:53.700 --> 03:23:58.620
and he never offered an explanation, either.&nbsp;
He only told me that he’d already secured a&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:23:58.620 --> 03:24:03.120
very interested potential buyer from a&nbsp;
syndicate out of town. Serious people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:24:03.120 --> 03:24:08.880
Dangerous people. They’d pay top dollar or&nbsp;
leave us tied up in trash bags in a ditch&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:24:08.880 --> 03:24:12.779
off the side of the highway. But we both&nbsp;
needed money, and we were willing to take&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:24:12.779 --> 03:24:16.080
that risk if it meant we could return&nbsp;
from this deal as a pair of rich men.

03:24:16.080 --> 03:24:20.040
The terms of the deal were simple: We’d&nbsp;
drive out onto a certain state highway&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:24:20.040 --> 03:24:25.019
at a little after 2:00 AM with the goods, meet&nbsp;
the buyers at a certain rest stop along the way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:24:25.020 --> 03:24:29.220
and make the exchange. We’d then all go&nbsp;
our separate ways, and if we were lucky,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:24:29.220 --> 03:24:32.700
none of us would ever see each other&nbsp;
ever again. Seemed simple enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:24:32.700 --> 03:24:36.779
sure. Scott seemed downright chipper about&nbsp;
the whole thing. And for a little while,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:24:36.779 --> 03:24:41.639
I was excited too, until he told me about the&nbsp;
road that the buyers wanted to meet us on.

03:24:41.640 --> 03:24:45.900
We’d all heard legends about that&nbsp;
place. Superstitions, really. People&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:24:45.899 --> 03:24:51.299
think criminals are scary, but believe me,&nbsp;
we’re a surprisingly superstitious bunch.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:24:51.300 --> 03:24:56.460
Our profession is one largely based on luck.&nbsp;
Being in the right place at the right time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:24:56.460 --> 03:25:01.739
and being lucky enough to avoid the cops along&nbsp;
the way. But something you need to know, whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:25:01.739 --> 03:25:07.679
you’re a criminal or arrow-straight, is that some&nbsp;
places are always going to be the wrong place.

03:25:07.680 --> 03:25:12.300
I’m not gonna tell you which road it is. I&nbsp;
know what you knuckleheads are like. You’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:25:12.300 --> 03:25:17.820
curious. You’re thrill-seekers. Hey, we were all&nbsp;
young once. But if I tell you what this road is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:25:17.819 --> 03:25:21.840
I know you’re gonna try to find it. Maybe&nbsp;
you’ll even decide it’s worth the trip down&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:25:21.840 --> 03:25:28.020
for a pleasant Sunday drive. Ha! After what I&nbsp;
went through on that road, I wouldn’t wish a trip&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:25:28.020 --> 03:25:33.060
down it on my worst enemy. There’s no other word&nbsp;
for what we encountered there other than evil.

03:25:33.060 --> 03:25:37.680
When we were kids, we used to call it The&nbsp;
Devil’s Passage. Every spooky rumor and&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:25:37.680 --> 03:25:43.920
scary story in the book circulated about that&nbsp;
place. Lemme see what I can remember… Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:25:43.920 --> 03:25:48.000
there was the Watcher In The Woods.&nbsp;
People used to say that there was a long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:25:48.000 --> 03:25:53.640
tall figure with the biggest eyes you’d ever&nbsp;
seen - eyes like headlights - standing amongst&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:25:53.640 --> 03:25:57.540
the trees. If the moonlight shone in at&nbsp;
the right angle as you were driving past,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:25:57.540 --> 03:26:03.180
you’d see it standing there, just staring&nbsp;
at you, thinking about doing who-knows-what.

03:26:03.180 --> 03:26:09.360
Then there was Old Beth, the Ghostly Hitchhiker.&nbsp;
People used to say they saw a strange old woman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:26:09.359 --> 03:26:13.500
hobbling down the side of the road in the middle&nbsp;
of the night. Sometimes, people said they could&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:26:13.500 --> 03:26:18.420
hear her crying, even if they were far enough&nbsp;
away to make such a thing seem impossible. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:26:18.420 --> 03:26:22.859
you took pity on her and pulled over, asking&nbsp;
her if she needed a ride, she’d tell you that&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:26:22.859 --> 03:26:27.420
you were a very kind person, but that she&nbsp;
was fine and dandy walking along by herself.

03:26:27.420 --> 03:26:31.980
But if you didn’t stop and offer her&nbsp;
a lift, if you just drove away… Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:26:31.979 --> 03:26:36.299
local legend had it that the next time you’d&nbsp;
see her face would be in your rearview mirror,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:26:36.300 --> 03:26:40.620
as she sat in your back seat, reaching&nbsp;
for you with her ancient, bony claws.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:26:40.620 --> 03:26:44.640
It’d make you think twice about leaving an&nbsp;
old woman to walk home alone in the dark.

03:26:44.640 --> 03:26:49.920
And of course, there was the Lone Jogger.&nbsp;
The stories my dad told me about him always&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:26:49.920 --> 03:26:54.420
used to scare the hell out of me. He was&nbsp;
a pale man, dressed unnaturally light for&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:26:54.420 --> 03:26:58.200
the cold winter months, jogging along the&nbsp;
side of the road. If you looked at him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:26:58.200 --> 03:27:02.700
he’d look back. If your eyes ever met, the&nbsp;
stories went that he’d start running after&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:27:02.700 --> 03:27:06.779
you. It wouldn’t matter how fast you&nbsp;
drove, he’d somehow always catch up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:27:06.779 --> 03:27:11.399
and stare at you through the glass of your&nbsp;
car’s windows. He never hurt anyone directly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:27:11.399 --> 03:27:16.799
but I imagine he probably caused a heart attack&nbsp;
or two in his time, if he ever really existed.

03:27:16.800 --> 03:27:22.920
But all of these were nothing - I repeat,&nbsp;
nothing - compared to the phantom cruiser.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:27:22.920 --> 03:27:26.880
You had to drive cautiously at night,&nbsp;
cause if you didn’t, you might find a&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:27:26.880 --> 03:27:32.340
ghostly 1970s police cruiser tailgating you,&nbsp;
and that’d be the worst thing you ever saw.

03:27:32.340 --> 03:27:35.340
There were fewer stories about&nbsp;
this one than all the others,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:27:35.340 --> 03:27:39.060
because if you ever ran afoul of the&nbsp;
phantom cruiser, chances are that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:27:39.060 --> 03:27:43.140
wouldn’t survive to tell people about what&nbsp;
happened to you. Though people could still&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:27:43.140 --> 03:27:47.520
make an educated guess about what happened&nbsp;
to you based on whatever was left behind.

03:27:47.520 --> 03:27:52.860
Here’s a not-so-fun fact: The Devil’s Passage&nbsp;
is technically qualified for one of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:27:52.859 --> 03:27:57.660
dangerous highways in the country. From crashes&nbsp;
to hitchhiker murders to unexplained deaths on&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:27:57.660 --> 03:28:03.599
the side of the road, since 1974, this road has&nbsp;
been an incredibly unpleasant place to drive.

03:28:03.600 --> 03:28:07.260
Every time I saw another horror story&nbsp;
about a strange death on the road,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:28:07.260 --> 03:28:11.940
I’d think of the Phantom Cruiser. And it was&nbsp;
those same thoughts polluting my brain that night,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:28:11.939 --> 03:28:16.080
as Scott drove the two of us to the rest&nbsp;
stop halfway down the Devil’s Passage.

03:28:16.080 --> 03:28:18.899
I only realized I’d dozed&nbsp;
off when he nudged me awake,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:28:18.899 --> 03:28:22.620
and the blurry lights of distant street&nbsp;
lamps flashed into my field of vision.

03:28:22.620 --> 03:28:25.019
“Look alive,” he said. “We’re here.”

03:28:25.020 --> 03:28:29.581
The rest stop wasn’t much to look at. All&nbsp;
that was there was an abandoned gas station,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:28:29.580 --> 03:28:32.340
really the perfect place for&nbsp;
this kind of illicit deal.

03:28:32.340 --> 03:28:35.520
My hand moved instinctively&nbsp;
to the Special in my coat and&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:28:35.520 --> 03:28:38.701
clicked back the hammer. Something&nbsp;
about this whole setup wasn’t right.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:28:38.700 --> 03:28:43.319
Sting operation? Police ambush? This whole&nbsp;
thing reeked of a deal too good to be true.

03:28:43.319 --> 03:28:48.479
My instincts turned out to be right, in a&nbsp;
sense, just not in the way I was expecting.

03:28:48.479 --> 03:28:54.239
As we turned into the rest stop, Scott turned up&nbsp;
his beams. All we saw was carnage waiting for us:&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:28:54.239 --> 03:28:59.819
A car - presumably one that once belonged&nbsp;
to our prospective buyers - in a state of&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:28:59.819 --> 03:29:04.139
horrific disarray. It looked as though a&nbsp;
train had impacted the side of the vehicle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:29:04.140 --> 03:29:08.161
completely caving it in. The metal&nbsp;
was covered in deep scratches and&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:29:08.160 --> 03:29:11.519
ruts that looked almost like claw&nbsp;
marks. It had been eviscerated.

03:29:11.520 --> 03:29:16.320
Scott broke, hard, and we both got out&nbsp;
of the car. I drew the Special out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:29:16.319 --> 03:29:20.460
my jacket and he grabbed the shotgun out of&nbsp;
the back seat. We approached with caution,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:29:20.460 --> 03:29:24.660
worrying this might just be another part of&nbsp;
the setup until we saw the thick puddle of&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:29:24.660 --> 03:29:28.800
blood congealing under the driver’s side&nbsp;
door. We drew closer, propelled mostly by&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:29:28.800 --> 03:29:33.960
morbid curiosity. Was it a hit from a rival gang&nbsp;
looking to intercept the deal? It seemed logical,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:29:33.960 --> 03:29:39.660
but there were no bullet holes in the car. Just&nbsp;
ripping, tearing, and massive impact damage.

03:29:39.660 --> 03:29:44.040
Scott shined the light of his phone into&nbsp;
the destroyed car, and I vomited when I saw&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:29:44.040 --> 03:29:49.260
what was inside. The buyers looked less like&nbsp;
people and more like two stacks of pulled pork&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:29:49.260 --> 03:29:53.520
in tattered clothes. If I hadn’t seen them&nbsp;
inside the car, I wouldn’t have even guessed&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:29:53.520 --> 03:29:58.800
they were human. And the damage wasn’t just to&nbsp;
them: The upholstery was torn up and burned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:29:58.800 --> 03:30:03.180
with violent symbols carved into the walls&nbsp;
and scrawled on the cracked windows in blood.

03:30:03.180 --> 03:30:08.040
When I turned to Scott, he was ghost-white,&nbsp;
clutching his phone and the shotgun with trembling&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:30:08.040 --> 03:30:13.140
hands. We didn’t exchange a word, but we both&nbsp;
knew it was time to leave. We could find another&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:30:13.140 --> 03:30:18.720
buyer. There would be other opportunities, other&nbsp;
deals. But lives? You only get one, and we both&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:30:18.720 --> 03:30:23.100
silently acknowledged that if we stuck around&nbsp;
here much longer, we wouldn’t even have that.

03:30:23.100 --> 03:30:27.420
We sped back into the car and locked the doors&nbsp;
behind us - for whatever good that would do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:30:27.420 --> 03:30:30.600
considering the damage that’d been&nbsp;
done to the buyers and their car.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:30:30.600 --> 03:30:34.440
Perhaps we just needed that illusion of&nbsp;
security to get us the hell out of here.

03:30:34.439 --> 03:30:38.279
The car pulled out of the rest stop&nbsp;
at break-neck speed. Scott floored it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:30:38.279 --> 03:30:41.759
trying to put some good distance between&nbsp;
us and the horror at the rest stop.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:30:41.760 --> 03:30:46.800
Whatever had happened to the Buyers, we&nbsp;
didn’t fancy sharing that same awful fate.

03:30:46.800 --> 03:30:50.880
My heart dropped down into my guts when I heard&nbsp;
the sirens and the flashing lights behind us.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:30:50.880 --> 03:30:55.980
After all this, we’d been busted for speeding.&nbsp;
They’d pull us over and find the guns and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:30:55.979 --> 03:30:59.939
briefcase in the car, and they’d have a lot of&nbsp;
questions that neither of us would have good&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:30:59.939 --> 03:31:04.319
answers to. We didn’t know whether to slow&nbsp;
down and hope for the best or speed up and&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:31:04.319 --> 03:31:08.939
take this boy in blue on a genuine car chase.&nbsp;
This whole thing couldn’t have gone more wrong.

03:31:08.939 --> 03:31:13.080
But my thoughts soon drifted from getting&nbsp;
used to the taste of prison food to something&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:31:13.080 --> 03:31:17.939
altogether more sinister: When I saw the&nbsp;
car getting closer in the rearview mirror,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:31:17.939 --> 03:31:24.299
I realized that this wasn’t a modern cop car&nbsp;
tailing us. It was a beat-up old 70s cruiser,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:31:24.300 --> 03:31:30.840
traveling at insane speeds, gaining on us. The&nbsp;
high beams cut through me like razor blades.

03:31:30.840 --> 03:31:36.420
I heard the radio crackle into life, even though&nbsp;
neither I nor Scott touched it. It wasn’t music,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:31:36.420 --> 03:31:43.080
just a hoarse, scratchy voice repeating the word&nbsp;
“Run” again and again. And seeing as whatever&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:31:43.080 --> 03:31:48.059
was behind us clearly wasn’t a real cop, we&nbsp;
were more than happy to oblige that request.

03:31:48.060 --> 03:31:51.960
Scott hit the gas like our lives&nbsp;
depended on it - Which, to be fair,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:31:51.960 --> 03:31:57.120
they did. But no matter how fast we sped&nbsp;
up, the cruiser kept getting closer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:31:57.120 --> 03:32:02.640
like a demon on our tail. I screamed at Scott to&nbsp;
go faster, but we were going as fast as we could.

03:32:02.640 --> 03:32:06.120
Next thing I knew, the phantom cruiser&nbsp;
collided with the back side of our car&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:32:06.120 --> 03:32:10.080
and sent us into a spin, showering the&nbsp;
two of us with broken glass crystals&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:32:10.080 --> 03:32:13.380
as the tires screeched across&nbsp;
the asphalt. It felt like an&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:32:13.380 --> 03:32:18.359
eternity before the car came to rest, and at&nbsp;
that moment, the phantom cruiser stopped too.

03:32:18.359 --> 03:32:24.540
Someone got out. He was dressed like a cop, he&nbsp;
even looked like a cop - a dude in his forties,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:32:24.540 --> 03:32:29.640
balding, overweight, with a handlebar&nbsp;
mustache - but something was wrong about&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:32:29.640 --> 03:32:32.400
him. He didn’t say a word&nbsp;
as he approached the car,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:32:32.399 --> 03:32:36.120
and he didn’t seem to register me sliding&nbsp;
the Special out of my jacket, either.

03:32:36.120 --> 03:32:39.540
He was inches away from Scott’s window&nbsp;
when I panicked and opened fire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:32:39.540 --> 03:32:44.100
sending a hail of small-caliber rounds&nbsp;
into his gut. He stumbled back slightly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:32:44.100 --> 03:32:48.121
as though shocked, and then&nbsp;
everything got a whole lot worse.

03:32:48.120 --> 03:32:55.080
The cop let out the most awful bellow. Not of&nbsp;
pain, but of pure rage. Something happened to&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:32:55.080 --> 03:33:00.300
his face: His eyes started to glow a bright,&nbsp;
hellish red, and his jaw began to extend until&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:33:00.300 --> 03:33:05.279
he looked like a Munch painting. There were no&nbsp;
teeth in there, just an infinite, black void.

03:33:05.279 --> 03:33:09.599
He grabbed a dazed Scott through the window,&nbsp;
pulling him into a brutal headlock and dragging&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:33:09.600 --> 03:33:15.060
him out of the car, releasing those deranged&nbsp;
bellows the entire time. Scott screamed and&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:33:15.060 --> 03:33:19.560
pleaded for help; I grabbed his body and tried&nbsp;
to pull him back in, but the cop was inhumanly&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:33:19.560 --> 03:33:24.661
strong. He just kept pulling until he was&nbsp;
all the way out, thrashing on the asphalt.

03:33:24.660 --> 03:33:30.120
I… I don’t want to tell you what he did&nbsp;
next. Wouldn’t be right. But suffice to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:33:30.120 --> 03:33:34.140
I couldn’t save Scott, and I definitely&nbsp;
didn’t want it happening to me.

03:33:34.140 --> 03:33:37.800
While he was working on Scott, I scrambled&nbsp;
into the driver’s seat and floored it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:33:37.800 --> 03:33:42.540
hoping that Scott would at least buy me some&nbsp;
time. I was weeping in terror as I drove away&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:33:42.540 --> 03:33:47.760
into the dark, leaving my friend to a horrible&nbsp;
fate with the driver of the phantom cruiser.

03:33:47.760 --> 03:33:51.239
So you can only imagine how I&nbsp;
felt when, a few minutes later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:33:51.239 --> 03:33:56.219
I heard the sirens again, and saw the&nbsp;
bright lights getting closer behind me.

03:33:56.220 --> 03:33:57.060
“Run…Run…Run…”

03:33:57.960 --> 03:34:04.800
The year was 1991. It was a quiet&nbsp;
night in Lethargy Valley, Arizona,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:34:04.800 --> 03:34:11.640
the quietest town in all of Maricopa County.&nbsp;
Seriously, despite the hardcore Arizona dry heat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:34:11.640 --> 03:34:17.220
this is one of the most idyllic little towns&nbsp;
you could imagine. Everyone who lived there knew&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:34:17.220 --> 03:34:22.800
each other by name and regularly invited their&nbsp;
neighbors to wholesome neighborhood cookouts. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:34:22.800 --> 03:34:27.900
town’s historic main street had an old-fashioned&nbsp;
ice cream parlor, a knitting supplies store,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:34:27.899 --> 03:34:35.099
and even a Build-A-Bear workshop. And of course,&nbsp;
a few local car dealerships and auto body shops.

03:34:35.100 --> 03:34:38.040
Lethargy Valley really is just the sweetest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:34:38.040 --> 03:34:44.700
calmest place you can possibly imagine, and&nbsp;
tonight, my friends, it’s going to rock.

03:34:44.700 --> 03:34:48.540
While the citizens of Lethargy Valley&nbsp;
went about their evening duties,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:34:48.540 --> 03:34:55.140
something hardcore was heading their way,&nbsp;
rumbling across the scorching Arizona deserts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:34:55.140 --> 03:35:03.060
burning fuel and belching smog. The hottest hot&nbsp;
rods this side of the sun. Wheels so fly they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:35:03.060 --> 03:35:09.720
ready to take off, with riders so badass their&nbsp;
own reflections are scared of them. That’s right:&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:35:09.720 --> 03:35:15.840
These are the High-Octane Full-Throttle&nbsp;
Adventures of the Exploding Zombie Gearheads,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:35:15.840 --> 03:35:20.819
and they’re about to give this sleepy&nbsp;
Arizona town a Four-Loko enema.

03:35:20.819 --> 03:35:25.739
The sun was setting on Lethargy Valley when&nbsp;
the lights of distant fire cut through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:35:25.739 --> 03:35:30.300
night. Citizens and shopkeepers,&nbsp;
enjoying an evening summer breeze,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:35:30.300 --> 03:35:35.460
halted in town as the redness in the East&nbsp;
got closer and brighter. They could feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:35:35.460 --> 03:35:40.739
the rumble of torqued-up subwoofers rattling&nbsp;
the ground. The roar of the engines felt more&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:35:40.739 --> 03:35:47.099
fitting on 747s than road-legal cars. And when&nbsp;
the distance was close enough that they could&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:35:47.100 --> 03:35:52.740
taste the vapors of burning gasoline in the&nbsp;
air, they could finally hear the laughter, too.

03:35:52.739 --> 03:36:00.120
Meet SCP-3885-01, also known as&nbsp;
the Exploding Zombie Gearheads,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:36:00.120 --> 03:36:05.760
probably the most metal of all anomalies on&nbsp;
the SCP Foundation catalog. But let’s talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:36:05.760 --> 03:36:10.320
about the time before these gas-chugging dust&nbsp;
devils were just another number in The Man’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:36:10.319 --> 03:36:17.160
filing cabinet. Cause these bad boys don’t care&nbsp;
about filing - They only care about defiling.

03:36:17.160 --> 03:36:21.899
They rolled into Lethargy Valley like a&nbsp;
pack of easy-riding coyotes on gearhead&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:36:21.899 --> 03:36:26.939
steroids. Their skin is green from&nbsp;
rot, and covered in cuts, lesions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:36:26.939 --> 03:36:32.580
and gnarly burn scars. Some have their heads&nbsp;
completely cracked open, exposing what little&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:36:32.580 --> 03:36:38.519
brains they have underneath. Others have fully&nbsp;
opened rib cages and disemboweled bellies. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:36:38.520 --> 03:36:44.701
they don’t care. These ain’t your grandpa’s&nbsp;
zombies. Forget the Walking Dead, these dudes&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:36:44.700 --> 03:36:50.939
are the Riding Dead. Romero meets Ratfink after&nbsp;
ten lines of Columbian Bam-Bam and a gallon of&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:36:50.939 --> 03:36:56.879
Monster Energy drink, decked out in a patchwork&nbsp;
of Motorhead-style leather and stolen clothes.

03:36:56.880 --> 03:37:02.700
And you better believe these fun-dead&nbsp;
freakazoids are here to party hardy.&nbsp;

03:37:02.700 --> 03:37:08.639
Paulie Poundtown rounded the corner of Main street&nbsp;
first on his ride, The Murderlizer. In a past&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:37:08.640 --> 03:37:13.620
life, it’d been a coffin, but now, with the aid of&nbsp;
two monster truck wheels on the back and a pair of&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:37:13.620 --> 03:37:20.040
circular saw blades on the front, it was a vehicle&nbsp;
ready to tear up the streets. Literally. Paulie&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:37:20.040 --> 03:37:25.920
drove down the road at a break-neck pace, leaving&nbsp;
a trail of sparks and black smog behind him. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:37:25.920 --> 03:37:30.779
was screaming something about being king of the&nbsp;
world when the vehicle exploded underneath him.

03:37:30.779 --> 03:37:35.399
The force of the boom flung Paulie down the&nbsp;
length of the street. He hit the window of&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:37:35.399 --> 03:37:39.599
a local barbershop and shattered through&nbsp;
it like a fleshy missile. For a moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:37:39.600 --> 03:37:44.460
he lay on the tiles, a leather pincushion&nbsp;
of broken glass and wooden shrapnel from&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:37:44.460 --> 03:37:50.460
the recently-detonated Murderlizer. But at no&nbsp;
moment in this whole insane ordeal did Paulie&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:37:50.460 --> 03:37:55.680
stop laughing. He got up, noticing his neck&nbsp;
was twisted backward at an odd angle - we&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:37:55.680 --> 03:38:00.540
told you it was a break-neck pace - but Paulie&nbsp;
just grabbed that sucker and crunched it back&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:38:00.540 --> 03:38:05.580
into place. It’d take a little more than&nbsp;
a totaled spine to stop Paulie Poundtown.

03:38:05.580 --> 03:38:09.720
After all, the night’s fun was only just starting.

03:38:09.720 --> 03:38:13.980
More tricked-out zombie roadsters were&nbsp;
pouring into the town from every angle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:38:13.979 --> 03:38:19.439
hopped up on a combination of tequila, lighter&nbsp;
fluid, and some stuff we can’t even mention if&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:38:19.439 --> 03:38:25.259
we want to keep monetization on this video. Steely&nbsp;
Dan was roaring into the Build-A-Bear workshop on&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:38:25.260 --> 03:38:30.420
a modified toilet with all-crushing caterpillar&nbsp;
tracks. Once he’d busted in through the facade,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:38:30.420 --> 03:38:34.440
he dismounted his porcelain throne and&nbsp;
began incinerating walls of teddy bears&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:38:34.439 --> 03:38:39.000
with a custom flamethrower powered by&nbsp;
a tube going into his stomach cavity.

03:38:39.000 --> 03:38:43.859
Once he was done burning 90% of the bears in&nbsp;
the store, he used the remaining unsullied&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:38:43.859 --> 03:38:48.599
parts to make a grungy-looking bear in his&nbsp;
own likeness. He strapped it to the front&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:38:48.600 --> 03:38:53.640
of his terrifying mobile toilet, and then&nbsp;
burnt that, too, just for the fun of it.

03:38:53.640 --> 03:38:57.840
Bareback Boris was making a beeline&nbsp;
for the ice cream parlor, riding what&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:38:57.840 --> 03:39:02.640
would look like a bull to the untrained&nbsp;
eye. It was actually a taxidermied bull,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:39:02.640 --> 03:39:07.260
gutted and filled with a nightmarish configuration&nbsp;
of motorcycle parts that would function better&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:39:07.260 --> 03:39:12.600
together as a method of execution than a&nbsp;
workable vehicle, but that’s just how the&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:39:12.600 --> 03:39:17.460
Exploding Zombie Gearheads like it. Boris pulled&nbsp;
the handbrake, which was made out of some old&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:39:17.460 --> 03:39:22.500
bones he dug up once, and ground the Bullcycle&nbsp;
to a screeching halt in front of the parlor.

03:39:22.500 --> 03:39:26.580
He climbed off and kicked in the door,&nbsp;
before running in to cause some chaos.

03:39:26.580 --> 03:39:32.880
Boris, the animal that he was, grabbed handfuls&nbsp;
of gelato and shoved them into his mouth, before&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:39:32.880 --> 03:39:38.400
turning and spitting them at the wall. He pulled&nbsp;
out a bottle of 90% vodka and took a long swig,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:39:38.399 --> 03:39:42.540
before taking a court summons out of his&nbsp;
jacket and shoving it into the bottle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:39:42.540 --> 03:39:47.100
He lit the top of the litigious fuse with&nbsp;
his lighter, which was shaped like a knife,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:39:47.100 --> 03:39:52.740
and made a molotov cocktail. Moments&nbsp;
later, the whole store was in flames.

03:39:52.739 --> 03:39:58.260
Boris stepped out of the burning ice cream&nbsp;
parlor, on fire but utterly unphased. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:39:58.260 --> 03:40:02.100
pulled out no less than four cigars&nbsp;
and lit them on his own burning skin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:40:02.100 --> 03:40:07.020
before smoking each one in a single&nbsp;
pull. If his lungs didn’t already look&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:40:07.020 --> 03:40:11.400
like blackened lumps of decaying coal,&nbsp;
they would have been screaming at him.

03:40:11.399 --> 03:40:15.779
But right now, the only person screaming&nbsp;
was Gene Simmons of the band KISS,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:40:15.779 --> 03:40:21.599
as some of Boris’ fellow riders rampaged through&nbsp;
the streets past him, “UNHOLY” blaring from their&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:40:21.600 --> 03:40:27.300
radios at ear-exploding volumes. The Exploding&nbsp;
Zombie Gearheads didn’t throw out-of-town ragers&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:40:27.300 --> 03:40:33.239
like this often, but when they did, they always&nbsp;
tried to make it one for the history books. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:40:33.239 --> 03:40:38.279
before you start worrying about the safety of the&nbsp;
citizens of Lethargy Valley, Arizona, you should&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:40:38.279 --> 03:40:44.340
know that the Zombie Gearheads never hurt anybody.&nbsp;
Well, never hurt anybody on purpose, that is.

03:40:44.340 --> 03:40:50.220
If a piece of stray shrapnel from an exploding&nbsp;
Camaro, reshaped with scrap metal to look like&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:40:50.220 --> 03:40:56.220
a giant fist spraying fire from its knuckles,&nbsp;
happens to take out somebody’s eye, well...That&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:40:56.220 --> 03:41:01.980
can’t really be seen as anybody’s fault, can&nbsp;
it? Especially when you don’t have eyes anymore.

03:41:01.979 --> 03:41:07.919
The closest thing to the brains of this operation&nbsp;
- which, for these guys, really isn’t saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:41:07.920 --> 03:41:12.720
much - was a free-thinking, free-spirited,&nbsp;
free-liquored individual known as Joey&nbsp;

03:41:12.720 --> 03:41:17.820
]nuts. The whole gang had probably&nbsp;
about 40 brain cells between them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:41:17.819 --> 03:41:22.620
but at least five of those cells belonged to&nbsp;
Joey. While many of his rotting buddies were&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:41:22.620 --> 03:41:27.720
goofing off and causing mayhem across town,&nbsp;
Joey was already acting on the real reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:41:27.720 --> 03:41:33.000
everybody was here: Getting new parts&nbsp;
for their sick-as-all-hell car mods.

03:41:33.000 --> 03:41:37.620
Sure, as mechanics, they couldn’t produce&nbsp;
actually workable vehicles worth a damn.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:41:37.620 --> 03:41:41.640
But at the end of the day, isn’t a vehicle&nbsp;
looking awesome much more important than&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:41:41.640 --> 03:41:46.080
boring old functionality? If you think&nbsp;
otherwise, you’re probably just a square.

03:41:46.080 --> 03:41:52.439
But we digress, back to Joey
nuts. Joey and a crew of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:41:52.439 --> 03:41:57.960
boys - including Dirty Mike, Scuzzy Steve, and&nbsp;
Generally Unclean Gary - rolled up on one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:41:57.960 --> 03:42:02.580
the local auto body shops and bashed the door&nbsp;
down with their vehicle. Which, by the way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:42:02.580 --> 03:42:08.580
was a modified SUV modded with bulldozer parts&nbsp;
and a makeshift cannon. It was a powerful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:42:08.580 --> 03:42:14.160
if structurally unsound, motorized siege weapon.&nbsp;
Joey and the boys jumped off the vehicle and&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:42:14.160 --> 03:42:18.899
stormed into the building, wielding pipe wrenches&nbsp;
with nails and fishing hooks welded onto them.

03:42:18.899 --> 03:42:22.620
A confused mechanic was quaking in&nbsp;
his boots as the gaggle of zombie&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:42:22.620 --> 03:42:27.000
gearheads approached him. Joey stood at the&nbsp;
front, swinging his pipe wrench around with&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:42:27.000 --> 03:42:32.580
menacing randomness. He was chewing twelve&nbsp;
toothpicks, making him look extra tough.

03:42:32.580 --> 03:42:36.359
The mechanic, with a quiet, trembling&nbsp;
voice, told Joey and the boys that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:42:36.359 --> 03:42:40.739
needed to leave. They weren’t supposed to&nbsp;
be back here. They’d need to leave and come&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:42:40.739 --> 03:42:45.719
back during opening hours. It was the most&nbsp;
polite telling-off he could possibly muster.

03:42:45.720 --> 03:42:50.100
The exploding zombie gearheads just&nbsp;
laughed. Once they were done cackling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:42:50.100 --> 03:42:55.800
Joey lifted up his pipe wrench and pointed it&nbsp;
at the terrified mechanic’s face. Joey cleared&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:42:55.800 --> 03:42:58.320
his tobacco-and-gasoline-burned throat and said:&nbsp;

03:42:58.319 --> 03:43:04.019
“Listen, ya stupid grease monkey, I’m only gonna&nbsp;
say this once so open your earholes real wide and&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:43:04.020 --> 03:43:09.000
listen up, ‘kay? You’re gonna pack up your&nbsp;
crap and leave, so me and my boys can loot&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:43:09.000 --> 03:43:14.399
this place to our heart’s content, and beef&nbsp;
up our sick-ass rides. And if you don’t leave?&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:43:14.399 --> 03:43:20.819
You’re gonna be holding this here pipe wrench&nbsp;
for me in your prison pocket. You get me?”

03:43:20.819 --> 03:43:26.219
The mechanic definitely got him. He didn’t&nbsp;
waste a moment in high-tailing it out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:43:26.220 --> 03:43:30.420
the store while Joey and the boys pulled&nbsp;
a classic “smash and grab” on his wares.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:43:30.420 --> 03:43:35.640
They stole everything from whole cars&nbsp;
to wrenches and lug nuts. Across town,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:43:35.640 --> 03:43:40.980
the Exploding Zombie Gearheads were doing the&nbsp;
exact same thing: Stealing or stripping every&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:43:40.979 --> 03:43:46.439
vehicle in sight and cannibalizing the parts&nbsp;
for their own righteous whips. Town by town,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:43:46.439 --> 03:43:53.819
vehicle by vehicle, explosion by explosion, they’d&nbsp;
one day figure this whole “mechanic” deal out.

03:43:53.819 --> 03:43:58.979
With the night’s revelry finally concluded, it was&nbsp;
time to return home and get to work on the next&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:43:58.979 --> 03:44:04.799
set of rides. This legion of awesome, unkillable&nbsp;
morons piled back into their stylish death traps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:44:04.800 --> 03:44:09.960
and rode off into the misty dawn, leaving&nbsp;
hundreds of thousands of dollars in property&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:44:09.960 --> 03:44:15.720
damage behind them - But not a single&nbsp;
death. As hyper-destructive anomalies go,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:44:15.720 --> 03:44:21.000
they’re honestly pretty benevolent. Or at&nbsp;
least, too self-centered to be truly malevolent.

03:44:21.000 --> 03:44:27.300
They tore up the desert on the way back&nbsp;
to the real SCP-3885: Vulture Gulch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:44:27.300 --> 03:44:33.000
the home of the Exploding Zombie Gearheads.&nbsp;
It’s a desert shantytown that’s “officially”&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:44:33.000 --> 03:44:39.720
been abandoned since July 9th, 1973, due to high&nbsp;
volumes of dangerous radon gas being emitted from&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:44:39.720 --> 03:44:45.480
the uranium deposits in a nearby mine. But these&nbsp;
intense, joy-riding mutants hardly mind a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:44:45.479 --> 03:44:53.099
bit of radiation in their sweet pad. Be it ever&nbsp;
so radioactive, there’s no place like home, right?

03:44:53.100 --> 03:44:58.080
Some of the vehicles even exploded on the&nbsp;
way back to the place, but the boys didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:44:58.080 --> 03:45:02.760
mind. The flaming explosion survivors just&nbsp;
crawled out of the fire and hopped onto the&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:45:02.760 --> 03:45:07.980
rides of their closest buddies. All in all,&nbsp;
everyone in attendance had a damn good time&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:45:07.979 --> 03:45:12.299
that night. They’d spend the rest of the&nbsp;
early morning setting rocks on fire for fun&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:45:12.300 --> 03:45:17.640
and chewing on ignited fireworks. That’s the&nbsp;
kind of brain trust we’re dealing with here.

03:45:17.640 --> 03:45:20.701
When the Foundation eventually&nbsp;
contained them in Vulture Gulch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:45:20.700 --> 03:45:24.779
the gearheads didn’t even put up a fuss,&nbsp;
as long the Foundation kept supplying&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:45:24.779 --> 03:45:29.340
them with three decommissioned vehicles&nbsp;
every month. It’s a pretty sweet deal:&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:45:29.340 --> 03:45:35.700
A crazed car-lover’s paradise, where as long as&nbsp;
you keep it within the walls, anything goes, baby.

03:45:35.700 --> 03:45:42.300
But there is one strange little detail. A&nbsp;
question that remains unanswered. Drones sent&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:45:42.300 --> 03:45:46.440
in by the Foundation to spy on the residents&nbsp;
of Vulture Gulch have picked up one strange&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:45:46.439 --> 03:45:52.679
recurring theme in their chatter: Mentions of&nbsp;
an individual known only as “The Boss” paying&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:45:52.680 --> 03:45:58.620
them a visit. And we can only assume they don’t&nbsp;
mean Bruce Springsteen. The only clues we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:45:58.620 --> 03:46:03.899
are that the Gearheads believe this Boss created&nbsp;
them and put them here on this earth to be totally&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:46:03.899 --> 03:46:11.219
rad. The other clue is a seemingly pervasive&nbsp;
belief that, someday soon, the Boss will return.

03:46:11.220 --> 03:46:14.040
Who do you think the boss of the Exploding Zombie&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:46:14.040 --> 03:46:17.279
Gearheads truly is? Let us&nbsp;
know down in the comments.

03:46:17.279 --> 03:46:19.859
Man, it’s even better on the fifteenth read...

03:46:19.859 --> 03:46:25.559
Oh, hello! I didn’t see you there, dear viewers&nbsp;
of SCP Explained. I’m on break between supervising&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:46:25.560 --> 03:46:31.740
SCP-682 termination attempts and inspecting&nbsp;
the mops we use on SCP-173’s leavings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:46:31.739 --> 03:46:36.000
so I decided to do what all the cool people&nbsp;
are doing in their spare time right now:&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:46:36.000 --> 03:46:42.779
Rereading Chainsaw Man, the hit manga by&nbsp;
Japanese author and artist Tatsuki Fujimoto.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:46:42.779 --> 03:46:48.120
For the uninitiated, it’s the story of Denji, a&nbsp;
poor young man from Japan who makes his living&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:46:48.120 --> 03:46:53.640
hunting devils - dangerous creatures that&nbsp;
are embodiments of mankind’s greatest fears.

03:46:53.640 --> 03:46:59.161
But when that living leads to him dying at the&nbsp;
hands of a gang of zombie yakuza - believe me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:46:59.160 --> 03:47:04.500
it makes sense in context - he bonds with&nbsp;
the legendary Chainsaw devil and is reborn&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:47:04.500 --> 03:47:11.640
as Chainsaw Man, an unconventional superhero&nbsp;
who chainsaws first and asks questions later.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:47:11.640 --> 03:47:16.080
Naturally, I was eager to see how one&nbsp;
of our own bloodthirsty killers would&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:47:16.080 --> 03:47:21.420
fare against Denji’s chainsaws of fury, so I&nbsp;
selected the most violent, battle-hardened,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:47:21.420 --> 03:47:29.460
and carnage-hungry anomaly out there:&nbsp;
SCP-076-2, the immortal warrior known as Able.

03:47:29.460 --> 03:47:34.680
The two of them have a surprising amount in&nbsp;
common. Both are effectively immortal and&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:47:34.680 --> 03:47:40.080
can revive after sustaining massive physical&nbsp;
injuries. Both absolutely love to fight with&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:47:40.080 --> 03:47:44.580
their array of deadly weapons and anomalous&nbsp;
strength. Both have been part of experimental&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:47:44.580 --> 03:47:50.220
operations groups - with Denji being a member of&nbsp;
Japan’s Public Safety Devil Hunter’s Division 4,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:47:50.220 --> 03:47:55.680
and Able being an ex-member of the SCP&nbsp;
Foundation’s disastrous Pandora’s Box&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:47:55.680 --> 03:48:00.480
Mobile Task Force, most of which he&nbsp;
later massacred out of boredom. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:48:00.479 --> 03:48:04.379
can probably see why these two really&nbsp;
did feel like the perfect match-up.

03:48:04.380 --> 03:48:10.380
So after forcing the trusty Anon-O-Tron 6000 to&nbsp;
read every currently available volume of Chainsaw&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:48:10.380 --> 03:48:16.500
Man, and compute years of Able’s gruesome battle&nbsp;
data, I’ve set up the perfect simulation for your&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:48:16.500 --> 03:48:22.260
viewing pleasure. And hey, fellow fans of the&nbsp;
manga, isn’t it horrifying that even we beat&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:48:22.260 --> 03:48:29.160
MAPPA to the punch of animating this thing?
God, that joke will age poorly if that anime&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:48:29.160 --> 03:48:34.380
comes out before this. Anyway, let’s crank&nbsp;
this machine into action and let her rip!

03:48:34.380 --> 03:48:42.300
Japan, 1997. Everything is roasting in the July&nbsp;
Heat. Men In Black, Harrison Ford’s Air Force One,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:48:42.300 --> 03:48:47.880
and Air Bud are hitting theaters for the first&nbsp;
time. Everything is right with the world. That’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:48:47.880 --> 03:48:52.620
why, over in the headquarters of the Public&nbsp;
Safety Devil Hunting Department, Denji - our&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:48:52.620 --> 03:48:58.080
Chainsaw-loving hero - is being praised by&nbsp;
his kind boss and mentor figure, Makima.

03:48:58.080 --> 03:49:02.040
A lovable, supportive woman who&nbsp;
will never do anything wrong.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:49:02.040 --> 03:49:06.840
She even loves dogs - how could a&nbsp;
person who loves dogs ever be evil?

03:49:06.840 --> 03:49:12.360
That very morning, Denji, in his Chainsaw&nbsp;
Man form, managed to defeat the Accidentally&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:49:12.359 --> 03:49:15.660
Making A Mistake On Your Tax Forms&nbsp;
And Now You’re Going To Prison Devil,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:49:15.660 --> 03:49:21.720
who had been terrorizing downtown Tokyo. It was a&nbsp;
challenging battle, but in the end, he’d managed&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:49:21.720 --> 03:49:26.760
to turn the tables and defeat the creature by&nbsp;
setting it on fire. Needless to say, Makima&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:49:26.760 --> 03:49:33.540
was extremely pleased with Denji’s work here, but&nbsp;
now, she had considerable graver news to impart.

03:49:33.540 --> 03:49:38.340
She’d gotten word from an envoy of another&nbsp;
organization that hunts down dangerous and&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:49:38.340 --> 03:49:44.460
anomalous creatures - the SCP Foundation - that&nbsp;
an extremely lethal entity had breached their&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:49:44.460 --> 03:49:50.100
containment and was now somewhere in Japan.&nbsp;
The entity in question was not a devil, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:49:50.100 --> 03:49:55.920
thereby would be working on a different rule&nbsp;
set. Makima opened a file faxed to her by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:49:55.920 --> 03:50:02.160
Foundation - yes, remember, this is set in&nbsp;
1997 - and gave Denji the crucial lowdown.

03:50:02.160 --> 03:50:07.979
Several hours ago, the entity known as Able had&nbsp;
resurrected from his huge, black sarcophagus&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:50:07.979 --> 03:50:14.160
in the underwater chamber of the classified&nbsp;
facility, Containment Area 25b. After waking up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:50:14.160 --> 03:50:19.139
Able had slaughtered his way through the&nbsp;
entire base, killing every SCP Foundation&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:50:19.140 --> 03:50:25.020
operative in his path and then swimming out&nbsp;
into the Pacific Ocean. Sometime after that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:50:25.020 --> 03:50:30.840
he infiltrated a Japanese cargo ship and murdered&nbsp;
all the workers on board, before steering the ship&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:50:30.840 --> 03:50:36.060
back towards the land of the rising sun,&nbsp;
where he hoped to claim even more victims.

03:50:36.060 --> 03:50:41.220
Makima told Denji that it would fall to&nbsp;
him and his associates to stop this Able,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:50:41.220 --> 03:50:46.200
with a little help from the SCP&nbsp;
Foundation’s intelligence. But be warned:&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:50:46.200 --> 03:50:50.040
Able is an incredible combatant&nbsp;
with extreme physical strength&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:50:50.040 --> 03:50:56.160
and durability, as well as surprising tactical&nbsp;
intelligence. It wouldn’t be an easy fight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:50:56.160 --> 03:51:02.160
but Makima promised that if Denji won, she’d&nbsp;
hug him and go to a nearby karaoke bar with him.&nbsp;

03:51:02.160 --> 03:51:05.939
Denji replied, “Consider him&nbsp;
dead already, Miss Makima.”

03:51:05.939 --> 03:51:12.299
Meanwhile, Able was walking through the slums&nbsp;
of Tokyo, marveling at the neon signs for bars&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:51:12.300 --> 03:51:17.760
and clubs. His journey to Japan hadn’t been an&nbsp;
accident. Able had been to Japan once before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:51:17.760 --> 03:51:24.780
in the year 1605. He’d faced the legendary&nbsp;
Japanese philosopher and Swordsman Miyamoto&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:51:24.779 --> 03:51:30.239
Musashi, considered by many to be one of the&nbsp;
greatest warriors in human history. Able had&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:51:30.239 --> 03:51:34.620
dueled Musashi - who famously wielded&nbsp;
two Katanas at once - in the hills of&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:51:34.620 --> 03:51:38.939
the Harima Province, where, after a&nbsp;
tense battle, Musashi cut him down.

03:51:38.939 --> 03:51:42.179
Able would not resurrect again&nbsp;
during Musashi’s lifetime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:51:42.180 --> 03:51:46.140
but the battle gave him a deep and&nbsp;
abiding respect for the legendary&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:51:46.140 --> 03:51:51.300
warrior. Able knew that if the opportunity&nbsp;
ever rose again, he would return to Japan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:51:51.300 --> 03:51:57.180
in hopes of experiencing such a brilliant battle&nbsp;
yet again. But the industrial and technological&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:51:57.180 --> 03:52:02.940
boom had changed so much. It was no longer&nbsp;
the quiet and pastoral Japan he’d experienced,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:52:02.939 --> 03:52:08.819
but a booming epicenter of trade and commerce.&nbsp;
He found it all strange and perplexing.

03:52:08.819 --> 03:52:13.799
Suddenly, he found himself surrounded by a group&nbsp;
of Japanese street toughs, many of them wielding&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:52:13.800 --> 03:52:18.360
switchblades. They laughed at his strange&nbsp;
outfit, which to them looked like an old,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:52:18.359 --> 03:52:23.160
worn bedsheet. One of the smarter members of&nbsp;
the group had already decided to go home when&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:52:23.160 --> 03:52:28.559
the others made up their mind to mug Able - the&nbsp;
warrior’s extensive tattoos made him look like a&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:52:28.560 --> 03:52:33.600
Middle-Eastern yakuza don. The rest, however,&nbsp;
were happy to take their chances with him.

03:52:33.600 --> 03:52:38.040
“Empty your pockets, if that goofy toga&nbsp;
even has pockets,” the leader said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:52:38.040 --> 03:52:40.560
holding up his switchblade.&nbsp;
“Unless you wanna get cut.”

03:52:40.560 --> 03:52:45.960
Able just smirked and drew a pair of long,&nbsp;
obsidian daggers. In the following moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:52:45.960 --> 03:52:50.100
the alley was filled with screams,&nbsp;
then was silent yet again.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:52:50.100 --> 03:52:54.060
Able walked on, breathing a sigh&nbsp;
of disappointment at how incredibly&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:52:54.060 --> 03:52:58.440
mediocre this first fight had been,&nbsp;
his blades dripping with fresh blood.

03:52:58.439 --> 03:53:01.979
Musashi is rolling in his&nbsp;
grave, Able thought to himself.

03:53:01.979 --> 03:53:07.979
Meanwhile, across town, Denji and the rest&nbsp;
of Division 4 were mobilizing. It was him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:53:07.979 --> 03:53:13.139
the serious, sword-wielding Aki, and the&nbsp;
adorable, pathologically-lying Blood Fiend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:53:13.140 --> 03:53:18.120
Power. They’d been told over the phone by&nbsp;
a man named Dr. Bright that Able would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:53:18.120 --> 03:53:23.460
relatively easy to track down - He’s not known&nbsp;
for his subtlety; all you need to do is follow&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:53:23.460 --> 03:53:28.500
the trail of carnage he causes wherever&nbsp;
he goes. From the way he talks about him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:53:28.500 --> 03:53:34.500
it seems almost as though Dr. Bright bears&nbsp;
a personal grudge against Able. How strange.&nbsp;

03:53:34.500 --> 03:53:40.620
Power didn’t seem intimidated. She proudly&nbsp;
proclaimed, “I don’t think this battle will&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:53:40.620 --> 03:53:47.939
be difficult at all. In fact, I’ve faced&nbsp;
this Able before and defeated him handily.”

03:53:47.939 --> 03:53:51.179
Aki sighed and asked. “When did this happen?”

03:53:51.180 --> 03:53:58.440
“Last Tuesday, of course!” she replied.&nbsp;
Power had only heard about Able this morning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:53:58.439 --> 03:54:02.879
but Denji and Aki had learned better&nbsp;
than to dispute her at this point.

03:54:02.880 --> 03:54:07.260
Suddenly, a large television screen that&nbsp;
had been previously relaying an ad for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:54:07.260 --> 03:54:12.840
cutting-edge stereo system cut to an emergency&nbsp;
news report. There’d been a horrific incident&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:54:12.840 --> 03:54:18.720
in downtown Tokyo, where a bar had been attacked&nbsp;
and most of its patrons murdered by a deranged,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:54:18.720 --> 03:54:23.520
tattooed man carrying a pair of huge&nbsp;
swords. Aki immediately recognized the&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:54:23.520 --> 03:54:28.440
place - The bar was yakuza-owned. If this&nbsp;
Able was on the hunt for worthy opponents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:54:28.439 --> 03:54:33.899
it makes all the sense in the world that Japan’s&nbsp;
iconic crime syndicates would be his first target.

03:54:33.899 --> 03:54:38.879
Denji, Aki, and Power knew&nbsp;
exactly where they needed to go.

03:54:38.880 --> 03:54:43.500
Over at the bar, Able was having a whale&nbsp;
of a time. Innocent patrons were running&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:54:43.500 --> 03:54:48.120
and screaming, while the Yakuza engaged&nbsp;
in an all-out war with the terrifying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:54:48.120 --> 03:54:54.239
inhuman warrior. Several of them had already been&nbsp;
cut down. Two Yakuza soldiers behind the bar were&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:54:54.239 --> 03:54:58.800
reloading illegal Uzis and preparing&nbsp;
to return fire. Both were sweating,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:54:58.800 --> 03:55:03.060
terrified by the sudden, random&nbsp;
attack. When they’d shot him before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:55:03.060 --> 03:55:08.340
he’d managed to dodge most of the bullets,&nbsp;
and expertly block the rest with his swords.

03:55:08.340 --> 03:55:13.680
Who the hell had sent this monster? Was&nbsp;
he with the Triads? The Russian mob? Or&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:55:13.680 --> 03:55:16.560
some devil summoned by the Japanese&nbsp;
government to crack down on them?

03:55:16.560 --> 03:55:22.621
Whatever the case, he seemed almost impossible&nbsp;
to kill. The two men stood back up and opened&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:55:22.620 --> 03:55:27.239
fire. Able held his two swords and spun them&nbsp;
like a propeller, blocking all the bullets&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:55:27.239 --> 03:55:31.800
almost effortlessly. He then produced&nbsp;
another dagger, seemingly from thin air,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:55:31.800 --> 03:55:36.360
and threw it directly into the heart of one&nbsp;
of the two remaining Yakuza behind the bar.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:55:36.359 --> 03:55:42.479
He dropped to the ground, dead instantly, leaving&nbsp;
only his friend alive in a bar full of corpses.

03:55:42.479 --> 03:55:47.939
That’s when Able noticed a decorative katana&nbsp;
behind the bar. He smiled and ordered the&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:55:47.939 --> 03:55:53.099
surviving Yakuza soldier to pick up the sword&nbsp;
and give him a real fight. The hapless mobster&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:55:53.100 --> 03:55:59.940
realized in that moment that this guy was truly&nbsp;
crazy, whoever he was. But what choice did he have&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:55:59.939 --> 03:56:05.040
now? With terror in his heart, the last surviving&nbsp;
Yakuza grabbed the katana and unsheathed it.

03:56:05.040 --> 03:56:10.800
“Good,” Able said, his voice deep&nbsp;
and menacing. “Now come fight me.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:56:10.800 --> 03:56:15.840
Let’s see if you last a few seconds longer&nbsp;
than your worthless friends, shall we?”

03:56:15.840 --> 03:56:21.960
He did not. The second the Yakuza ran towards&nbsp;
Able, and the ancient Swordsman swiped at him&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:56:21.960 --> 03:56:26.699
with one of his blades, cutting through the&nbsp;
katana and then the opponent holding it. A&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:56:26.699 --> 03:56:30.540
puny gangster never stood a chance&nbsp;
against a deadly immortal warrior,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:56:30.540 --> 03:56:37.140
and Able was furious. The last time he was here,&nbsp;
he faced a truly expert killer, who’d even managed&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:56:37.140 --> 03:56:44.400
to end Able’s life in single combat. And now he&nbsp;
was slaying insects in a karaoke bar. Pathetic…

03:56:44.399 --> 03:56:49.739
Suddenly, his ears pricked up. He turned to see a&nbsp;
red axe flying at his head at incredible speeds.

03:56:50.279 --> 03:56:55.380
With his superhuman reflexes, he managed to&nbsp;
dodge just in time, but the axe still cleaved&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:56:55.380 --> 03:56:59.520
off a chunk of his hair as it passed. Able&nbsp;
could see the one who threw it standing at&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:56:59.520 --> 03:57:04.920
the entrance to the bar: It was the Blood Fiend,&nbsp;
Power, who’d made the axe out of her own blood.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:57:04.920 --> 03:57:10.739
Standing next to her were Denji and Aki, Denji&nbsp;
wielding an axe and Aki stoically observing.

03:57:10.739 --> 03:57:14.279
“Damn, Miss Makima promised we’d&nbsp;
do karaoke at this bar if I beat&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:57:14.279 --> 03:57:17.040
you,” Denji said. “You’re going down for this.”

03:57:17.040 --> 03:57:23.939
Able smiled and pulled out another pair of&nbsp;
blades. “Finally!” he roared. “Warriors who&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:57:23.939 --> 03:57:29.160
fight the old-fashioned way. I feared&nbsp;
the years had stolen you all from me.”

03:57:29.160 --> 03:57:34.019
Power stepped forward, producing another&nbsp;
blood axe from nowhere. She yelled,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:57:34.020 --> 03:57:41.640
“Tremble in fear, Able. ‘Tis I,&nbsp;
your archnemesis, the mighty Power!”

03:57:41.640 --> 03:57:47.700
Able had literally never seen her before in his&nbsp;
thousands of years of life, but he appreciated&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:57:47.699 --> 03:57:52.500
that these warriors were at least able to match&nbsp;
his level of drama. As far as he was concerned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:57:52.500 --> 03:57:57.239
the fight was on. But even Able didn’t know the&nbsp;
level of fighting he was getting in for here.

03:57:57.239 --> 03:58:01.800
As he charged forward, the trio split,&nbsp;
immediately surrounding him. Good tactics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:58:01.800 --> 03:58:05.520
Able thought to himself.&nbsp;
Already, this was promising.

03:58:05.520 --> 03:58:10.920
Aki, who had remained quiet up to this point,&nbsp;
attacked first. He drew a tanto knife from his&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:58:10.920 --> 03:58:15.660
suit jacket and slashed at Able with impressive&nbsp;
speed. But unlike three of the other combatants&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:58:15.660 --> 03:58:21.899
in this situation, Aki was only human, which gave&nbsp;
him a serious disadvantage. Able decided it would&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:58:21.899 --> 03:58:25.920
be best to put him out of commission first.&nbsp;
With a quick and brutal kick to the chest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:58:25.920 --> 03:58:29.040
Aki was thrown against the wall with&nbsp;
the majority of his ribs broken.

03:58:29.040 --> 03:58:33.720
Revved up by his own bloodlust, Able&nbsp;
turned to Denji and Power and grinned&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:58:33.720 --> 03:58:38.940
like a maniac. This was already the&nbsp;
most fun he’d had in a long time. Who&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:58:38.939 --> 03:58:43.139
are these people? Doesn’t matter, he&nbsp;
thought. They’ll be dead soon anyway.

03:58:43.140 --> 03:58:48.000
While Able was still locked in thought, Power&nbsp;
pulled out a comically large hammer made out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:58:48.000 --> 03:58:53.279
her own blood, and brought it down towards Able.&nbsp;
He was surprised by the sudden attack - Did this&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:58:53.279 --> 03:58:58.439
girl have the same weapon-producing powers as&nbsp;
him!? This just keeps getting more interesting!

03:58:58.439 --> 03:59:00.599
“‘Tis the end, Able!”&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:59:00.600 --> 03:59:07.560
Power screamed as the hammer came down. “You have&nbsp;
once again been defeated by the mighty Power!”

03:59:07.560 --> 03:59:11.880
Again, just to clarify, these&nbsp;
two had never met each other.

03:59:11.880 --> 03:59:15.120
But it was already too late. Able punched upwards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:59:15.120 --> 03:59:19.080
his clenched fist colliding with the&nbsp;
hammer. He hit it with such terrifying&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:59:19.080 --> 03:59:23.519
force that Power’s blood hammer shattered&nbsp;
against his knuckles. In that same instant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:59:23.520 --> 03:59:28.680
Able noticed Denji running at him with an axe from&nbsp;
behind. Able produced another obsidian dagger and&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:59:28.680 --> 03:59:33.600
threw it into Denji’s forehead, dropping&nbsp;
him to the ground immediately. Pathetic.

03:59:33.600 --> 03:59:38.340
Power tried to produce another weapon, but&nbsp;
she’d used up too much blood already. Before&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:59:38.340 --> 03:59:42.840
she had a chance to make anything substantial,&nbsp;
Able sprung forwards with terrifying speed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:59:42.840 --> 03:59:47.699
trying to land a killing blow. But even&nbsp;
weakened, Power was freakishly fast.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:59:47.699 --> 03:59:52.439
She was able to dodge his blow and kick him in&nbsp;
the ribs, momentarily stunning him. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:59:52.439 --> 03:59:56.580
she took the time to gloat, putting her&nbsp;
hands on her hips and laughing victoriously.

03:59:56.580 --> 04:00:04.920
“Need a second to catch your breath, Able? It&nbsp;
is to be expected. None can keep up with me!”&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:00:04.920 --> 04:00:12.359
She grandiosely announced. “Perhaps you should&nbsp;
just give up and agree to become my servant.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:00:13.080 --> 04:00:17.040
I might even teach you a&nbsp;
thing or two about fighting—“

04:00:17.040 --> 04:00:20.880
Suddenly, Able was standing right in front&nbsp;
of her, squeezing her throat with his iron&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:00:20.880 --> 04:00:27.480
grip. He smiled, flashing teeth, and said,&nbsp;
“If you want to kill, kill. Don’t talk.”

04:00:27.479 --> 04:00:29.699
With a surprisingly minimal amount of strength,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:00:29.699 --> 04:00:34.439
he squeezed and heard a crunch from Power’s&nbsp;
neck. He dropped her limp body to the ground.

04:00:34.439 --> 04:00:39.299
Lucky for Power, Able wasn’t aware that a&nbsp;
Fiend like Power can survive an injury like&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:00:39.300 --> 04:00:43.920
this as long as she’s fed some more blood.&nbsp;
Instead, he just sighed in disappointment.

04:00:43.920 --> 04:00:48.420
“Is that all you weaklings&nbsp;
have to offer?” He bellowed.

04:00:48.420 --> 04:00:51.180
Aki, barely conscious after&nbsp;
being kicked against the wall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:00:51.180 --> 04:00:56.100
remained just conscious enough to activate&nbsp;
his contract with a powerful beast known&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:00:56.100 --> 04:01:01.680
as the Fox Devil. He twists his hands into a&nbsp;
strange gesture and whispers the word, “Kon”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:01:01.680 --> 04:01:04.680
before falling unconscious.&nbsp;
But that’s still enough.

04:01:04.680 --> 04:01:10.440
Suddenly, a giant demonic fox claw burst&nbsp;
through the wall of the bar, spraying dust&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:01:10.439 --> 04:01:15.660
and rubble everywhere. Able was definitely&nbsp;
not expecting that. He dodged several times&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:01:15.660 --> 04:01:21.359
as the claw swiped for him, often barely missing&nbsp;
him. For its last strike, it lunged forward and&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:01:21.359 --> 04:01:28.019
raked four claw marks across his chest. Able was&nbsp;
shocked by the sudden pain. It felt fantastic.

04:01:28.020 --> 04:01:33.180
He pulled a long, obsidian spear out of one&nbsp;
of his pocket dimensions, and forced it down&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:01:33.180 --> 04:01:39.180
through the Fox Devil’s paw and into the ground,&nbsp;
pinning it in place. After a moment of thrashing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:01:39.180 --> 04:01:44.100
the claw dissipated into smoke, lending&nbsp;
Able another victory - though even he&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:01:44.100 --> 04:01:47.760
would admit this was a more exciting&nbsp;
fight than the other ones had been.

04:01:47.760 --> 04:01:53.220
Was that it? Had he gained total victory once&nbsp;
more, just like he so often did these days?

04:01:53.220 --> 04:01:57.000
He was about to take pity on&nbsp;
himself when Denji rose up behind&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:01:57.000 --> 04:01:57.144
him. The durable devil hybrid teen was pulling the&nbsp;
knife out of his head like nothing, and dropping&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:01:57.144 --> 04:01:57.239
it onto the ground - a display of impressive&nbsp;
strength that certainly caught Able’s attention.

04:01:57.239 --> 04:02:00.120
“How about a rematch?” Denji asked.

04:02:00.120 --> 04:02:02.160
Able grinned. He liked this kid.

04:02:02.160 --> 04:02:04.920
“Challenge me, child,” Able said.

04:02:04.920 --> 04:02:08.399
“Well, since you asked...” Denji smirked.

04:02:08.399 --> 04:02:12.660
Denji reached into his shirt and grabbed the&nbsp;
rip cord emerging from his chest. It was time&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:02:12.660 --> 04:02:18.120
to go into overdrive on this thing. He gave it&nbsp;
a mighty yank, and like the rev of a chainsaw,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:02:18.120 --> 04:02:23.519
the madness began. Denji transformed&nbsp;
- Giant blades emerging from his arms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:02:23.520 --> 04:02:28.140
and his head transforming into a toothy,&nbsp;
saw-blade nightmare. He gave a mechanical&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:02:28.140 --> 04:02:34.440
roar that spewed smoke. This wasn’t just&nbsp;
Denji anymore. This was Chainsaw Man.

04:02:34.439 --> 04:02:40.859
Now this, Able thought, feeling his&nbsp;
adrenaline spike, is more like it!

04:02:40.859 --> 04:02:45.239
Following Denji’s lead, Able reached into one&nbsp;
of his pocket dimensions and pulled out one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:02:45.239 --> 04:02:50.340
his favorite weapons - one he’d only previously&nbsp;
used against the mighty Hard to Destroy Reptile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:02:50.340 --> 04:02:57.420
SCP-682: The Chainsaw Claymore, a huge,&nbsp;
two-handed sword with the eternally twisting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:02:57.420 --> 04:03:03.960
shredding teeth of a chainsaw ever circulating&nbsp;
around it. It was time for Chainsaw Vs Chainsaw.

04:03:03.960 --> 04:03:06.960
“What the hell are you waiting for?” Chainsaw Man&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:03:06.960 --> 04:03:09.720
roared. “Are we gonna stand around&nbsp;
all day, or are we gonna fight?”

04:03:09.720 --> 04:03:12.480
Able couldn’t have said it better himself.

04:03:12.479 --> 04:03:17.879
The two charged at one another at lightning&nbsp;
speeds, chainsaw clashing against chainsword.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:03:17.880 --> 04:03:23.040
The sheer force of contact was enough to send a&nbsp;
shockwave blasting through the bar. It rapidly&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:03:23.040 --> 04:03:27.120
became a power struggle, each one of them&nbsp;
trying hard to force their chainsaws out&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:03:27.120 --> 04:03:31.500
of the stalemate. Realizing that this time&nbsp;
he perhaps couldn’t win with raw strength,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:03:31.500 --> 04:03:34.560
Able backflipped away to reassess his options.

04:03:34.560 --> 04:03:39.000
But Chainsaw Man had no intention of&nbsp;
giving Able time to think about it.

04:03:39.000 --> 04:03:43.620
He darted towards Able with the weight and&nbsp;
momentum of a runaway freight train. If Able&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:03:43.620 --> 04:03:46.979
hadn’t raised his claymore to parry,&nbsp;
he would have been shredded to pieces&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:03:46.979 --> 04:03:51.599
by the devil’s saws in an instant. Instead,&nbsp;
the two of them rocketed out of the nearby&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:03:51.600 --> 04:03:56.940
wall in a cascade of debris, causing everyone&nbsp;
on the outside street to run for their lives.

04:03:56.939 --> 04:04:01.679
The two quickly stood from the&nbsp;
stumble, catching their breath.

04:04:01.680 --> 04:04:05.820
“Impressive,” Able said. “You’re&nbsp;
much better than the others.”

04:04:05.819 --> 04:04:10.019
Instead of replying, Denji briefly&nbsp;
retracted his arm chainsaws and&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:04:10.020 --> 04:04:13.440
grabbed a nearby parked car,&nbsp;
throwing it directly at Able.

04:04:13.439 --> 04:04:17.040
Able reacted quickly, cleaving the&nbsp;
car in half with his claymore and&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:04:17.040 --> 04:04:21.540
charging for Chainsaw Man again. Just&nbsp;
before Able could land a lethal strike,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:04:21.540 --> 04:04:25.380
Chainsaw Man deployed his&nbsp;
chainsaws again, blocking the blow.

04:04:25.380 --> 04:04:28.199
Able sped around him, trying&nbsp;
to strike again and again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:04:28.199 --> 04:04:33.540
but Chainsaw Man blocked every strike with&nbsp;
stunning efficacy. Able was astonished - few&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:04:33.540 --> 04:04:37.439
had ever been able to go toe to toe with&nbsp;
him like this before. He could feel his&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:04:37.439 --> 04:04:42.120
heart pounding gloriously in his chest.&nbsp;
He would give Denji a warrior’s death.

04:04:42.120 --> 04:04:46.019
With a furious yell, Able brought down&nbsp;
the Chainsaw Claymore for a devastating&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:04:46.020 --> 04:04:51.541
vertical strike, but Chainsaw Man was ready. He&nbsp;
arranged his arm chainsaws in a cross formation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:04:51.540 --> 04:04:56.699
like a giant pair of scissors, and caught&nbsp;
Able’s chainsword between them. Chainsaw Man&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:04:56.699 --> 04:05:01.500
pulled his arms in opposite directions,&nbsp;
slicing Able’s mighty sword in half.

04:05:01.500 --> 04:05:04.620
The immortal Swordsman skidded&nbsp;
backwards to avoid the fallout,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:05:04.620 --> 04:05:10.620
producing two smaller blades immediately. This&nbsp;
Chainsaw Man just kept exceeding expectations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:05:10.620 --> 04:05:14.939
didn’t he? Able would need to change&nbsp;
tactics if he wanted to win this one...

04:05:14.939 --> 04:05:19.979
Chainsaw Man was impressed by the speed and&nbsp;
tenacity of his foe. For someone who apparently&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:05:19.979 --> 04:05:25.620
wasn’t even a devil, Able sure packed a hell of a&nbsp;
punch. Did he ever run out of those damn weapons?

04:05:25.620 --> 04:05:28.800
As though attempting to answer&nbsp;
Chainsaw Man’s question for him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:05:28.800 --> 04:05:34.080
Able began running around his flank, rapidly&nbsp;
producing and throwing blades and axes in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:05:34.080 --> 04:05:39.120
startling volley. Chainsaw Man was able to use&nbsp;
his Chainsaw arms and face to block most of them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:05:39.120 --> 04:05:43.859
but not all of them. Several daggers and&nbsp;
small throwing axes splattered into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:05:43.859 --> 04:05:49.620
tender flesh of Chainsaw Man’s chest. Able had&nbsp;
successfully wounded him, and he wasn’t done.

04:05:49.620 --> 04:05:55.319
Feeling a little more confident now, Able decided&nbsp;
to take a different tactic: He produced a large,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:05:55.319 --> 04:06:00.000
spiked mace from thin air, and ran at Chainsaw&nbsp;
Man while the devil was still recovering&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:06:00.000 --> 04:06:05.520
from his projectile attack. With one brutal&nbsp;
whack, he sent Denji flying down the street,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:06:05.520 --> 04:06:11.581
carving a rut into the concrete beneath him.&nbsp;
But Able wasn’t done. While Chainsaw Man was&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:06:11.580 --> 04:06:16.979
still trying to recover, Able leaped onto him and&nbsp;
began beating him into the ground with his mace.

04:06:16.979 --> 04:06:22.080
The strikes were so brutal they shook the earth&nbsp;
and sent cracks across the surrounding ground.

04:06:22.080 --> 04:06:27.300
Then Able stopped. He realized, for a&nbsp;
moment, that he was letting his blood lust&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:06:27.300 --> 04:06:32.340
get the better of him. This was dishonorable.&nbsp;
Where would be the fun in beating this boy to&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:06:32.340 --> 04:06:36.540
death while he lay on the ground, and depriving&nbsp;
himself of one of the greatest opponents he’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:06:36.540 --> 04:06:41.880
had in quite some time? No, that wouldn’t&nbsp;
do at all. He’d give him one more chance.

04:06:41.880 --> 04:06:47.580
“On your feet, boy,” Able said. “You&nbsp;
fight well. Get up and carry on. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:06:47.580 --> 04:06:52.080
won’t let a beast as rare as you die&nbsp;
like a common dog. Rise and fight me!”

04:06:52.080 --> 04:06:57.720
And Chainsaw Man did as he was told. Able was&nbsp;
shocked to see the very ground shatter underneath&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:06:57.720 --> 04:07:02.940
him as Chainsaw Man burst up through it, all his&nbsp;
swords at the ready. The mace was thrown from&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:07:02.939 --> 04:07:08.819
Able’s hands as Chainsaw Man launched up towards&nbsp;
him, all metal teeth and fury. Luckily for Able,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:07:08.819 --> 04:07:13.920
he pulled out a battle axe just in time to&nbsp;
block the flurry of brutal strikes from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:07:13.920 --> 04:07:19.380
patron saint of chainsaws. Now this was&nbsp;
a fight even Musashi would be proud of.

04:07:19.380 --> 04:07:24.420
“Yes, boy, yes!” Able yelled.&nbsp;
“This is true combat!”

04:07:24.420 --> 04:07:27.180
Chainsaw Man replied with the swing of his blades,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:07:27.180 --> 04:07:31.500
which Able was narrowly able to dodge. The&nbsp;
two finally landed back down on the ground,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:07:31.500 --> 04:07:36.720
and Able was fast enough to bury his battle axe&nbsp;
in Chainsaw Man’s shoulder. Before the devil&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:07:36.720 --> 04:07:40.680
could return a blow to Able, the anomalous&nbsp;
Swordsman pulled out a pair of his favorite&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:07:40.680 --> 04:07:47.220
swords and locked Chainsaw Man’s arms in place.&nbsp;
Chainsaw Man was undeniably incredibly powerful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:07:47.220 --> 04:07:52.860
but it looked like Able’s superior experience&nbsp;
and tactics might save him this time.

04:07:52.859 --> 04:07:56.399
“What the hell are you?” Chainsaw Man roared.

04:07:56.399 --> 04:07:58.439
“You’ve been a worthy opponent, boy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:07:58.439 --> 04:08:03.000
those are few and far between,” Able&nbsp;
said. “I’ll remember you for this...”

04:08:03.000 --> 04:08:05.520
But before Able could execute a killing blow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:08:05.520 --> 04:08:10.680
he felt a blood red throwing axe stick into&nbsp;
his back. Able winced in pain and turned to&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:08:10.680 --> 04:08:16.320
see Power and Aki about thirty feet behind him.&nbsp;
Power was propping Aki up - he’d donated some&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:08:16.319 --> 04:08:21.308
of his blood to bring her back to life. And&nbsp;
she was just as delusionally cocky as ever.

04:08:21.308 --> 04:08:21.363
“Tis I once again, my arch-nemesis!” Power yelled.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:08:21.363 --> 04:08:21.420
“Did you really believe you&nbsp;
could beat me so easily?”

04:08:21.420 --> 04:08:25.199
Able was about to say something, but&nbsp;
he’d already made a fatal mistake:&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:08:25.199 --> 04:08:29.519
Letting his guard down. Before another&nbsp;
word could pass the cursed warrior’s lips,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:08:29.520 --> 04:08:33.121
one of Chainsaw Man’s arm chainsaws&nbsp;
passed directly through his heart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:08:33.120 --> 04:08:39.239
tearing it apart within Able’s chest. It was a&nbsp;
sudden and decisive killing blow. Chainsaw Man&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:08:39.239 --> 04:08:43.199
pulled his saw back out of Able’s chest,&nbsp;
stained with the deadly anomaly’s blood.

04:08:43.199 --> 04:08:44.760
Able collapsed to the ground,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:08:44.760 --> 04:08:48.900
wheezing and bleeding profusely from&nbsp;
the hole in his chest. But strangely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:08:48.899 --> 04:08:54.779
as Power, Aki, and Chainsaw Man converged&nbsp;
around him, they realized he was smiling.

04:08:54.779 --> 04:08:58.620
“Thank you...” Able said, and died yet again.

04:08:58.620 --> 04:09:04.500
With the battle won, like an extremely metal&nbsp;
Incredible Hulk, Chainsaw Man transformed back&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:09:04.500 --> 04:09:09.479
into Denji. The trio stood around Able’s corpse,&nbsp;
deeply confused as to what had just happened.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:09:09.479 --> 04:09:14.939
If this was the kind of thing the SCP Foundation&nbsp;
normally dealt with, they all silently agreed that&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:09:14.939 --> 04:09:20.339
perhaps it would be better not to get involved&nbsp;
with them in future. Except Power, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:09:20.340 --> 04:09:27.060
who said, “You two should be thanking me for&nbsp;
defeating him. You both owe me drinks for this!”

04:09:27.060 --> 04:09:32.520
Hundreds of miles away, in a black sarcophagus&nbsp;
deep underwater, surrounded by professional SCP&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:09:32.520 --> 04:09:37.920
Foundation divers, Able’s body once again&nbsp;
returned. Who knows how long he’d remain&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:09:37.920 --> 04:09:43.859
sleeping in there? But what we do know is that his&nbsp;
deathless sleep was suffused with sweet dreams,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:09:43.859 --> 04:09:49.979
knowing that this world still held worthy&nbsp;
opponents. And for Able, that was everything.

